      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <p>FOR DEAL= PANCAKES McDOUGALL'S Patent SELF-RAISING FLOUP. The Examiner Saturday, February 6, 1904.</p>
        <p>(Copyright.)</p>
        <p>Further' betails."</p>
        <p>A hansom speedily took them to the large, some= what gaudy apartments which were L'Alouette's temporary home—but disappointment awaited them. The Darlings were, or appeared to be, in a state of great anxiety about L'Alouette ; they had them- selves left for the opera about ten minutes after her departure with her maid, and they knew no more than did the public about the cause ofher disappear- ance—they seemed to be terribly afraid that some evil had happened to her; and only fell somewhat re-assured by the known promptness of the English police, which made them firmly believe that she would be found by the morning. Not on the following morning, nor yet for many mornings was there, however, any trace obtained of the missing diva, who seemed to have passed away as completely as if she had never existed. Daily visits were paid by the two men to Taverton-street, or rather, to be accurate, by Mr. Nasmyth, for Sir Claude, with his usual fickleness, was getting rather tired of the quest, and had already laid his homage at the feet of a new songstress. Nasmyth, however, for a man who had never either seen or heard L'Alouette, was most 'constant in his inquiries. It seemed almost as if he must have some other motive for these visits, than that aroused by mere curiosity. Pretty Julia Darling was the attraction, much to her mother's annoyance. Accustomed to artistes as she was, she had not yet realised that this rough, somewhat uncouth colonial might be a path worthy even oilier Julia; being a simple-minded man of a retiring disposition, he never bragged or spoke of money. Perhaps he wished to make Julia love him for his own sake, and not for his possessions, whatever they were. At first she had been inclined to laugh at him for his uncouthness, but, being a sensible girl, she soon began to discover the real worth that was concealed beneath this rough exterior; arid one day, after an acquaintance of about a month, when he suddenly asked her if she would share his fortunes, such as they were, she consented, much to his delight and to her mother's intense annoyance. "You are a little fool, Julia," she said. "What was the use of bringing you to Europe to be educated, if you are only going to marry a pauper-clod." "Are you quite sure he is a pauper, mumma ? " ,</p>
        <p>"Don't loOk like money, as far ;is i can see; and now Luline has taken herself off, I don't know what we shall do, unless she sends some. You won't even have a trousseau."</p>
        <p>"Perhaps she will; if not, I must do without. I don't believe Joe Nasmyth will care." "Have you heard again.from.Luline ? "</p>
        <p>"No, and I am veij7 glad I have not, for what- ever you may say, or even dear Luline may wish, I will tell no fibs to my good, honest Joe." "So you are already so hard hit as that, are you? Well, all I can repeat is that you are a fool, girl—with your chances and your pretty face." Julia only smiled. She evidently had more astuteness than her mother, and fully believed that she was doing well for herself. Yet Nasmith had dropped no hints. He had merely told ner that when they were married they would go to Paris, and she should shew him the wonders of the French capital, which he had as yet never visited. The subject of L'Alouette and her strange dis- appearance had, as far as the public was con- cerned, save for an occasional paragraph in a society paper, virtually ceased. The mystery had been succeeded by other mysteries in which London is ever rife, when—just as the season was on the wane—suddenly a whole column in a leading daily was headed:</p>
        <p>"The Mysterious Disappearance_ of L'Alouette.</p>
        <p>On the same page of the paper was a less preten- tious paragraph announcing that the marriage of Joe Nasmyth, the great South African millionaire, with Miss Julia Darling, had taken place on the previous afternoon. It was the first intimation Mrs. Darling had received as to the wealth of her son-in-law, and she was almost as angry at finding be was rich, when she had decided that he was poor, as she was at the tissue of falsehoods which she assured every- one had been invented to account for Luline's disappearance. The somewhat audacious paragraphs had asserted that love was at the root of the matter, and that when she was tired of honeymoon- ing in some obscure corner of the earth, she would return to her engagements and her triumphs. "Luline had no lover. Never was there a simpler, more simple-minded girl. She scorned every advance, sent back every flower that was offered her," so Mrs. Darling asserted stoutly, never vary- ing in her statement, even when she was fiercely attacked by the impresario who had "created" L'Alouette, and who considered that with her departure he had lost one of his most valued possessions. "How dare Mrs. Darling allow her to have a lover? She had been placed with her that she might be thoroughly safeguarded, but she had proved absolutely unreliable. He supposed now that her daughter had married a millionaire, she thought she could do without him; but she would find it was not so easy to escape his vengeance." Poor Mrs. Darling! She could scarcely con- gratulate herself on her present relations with the angry impresario; still she managed to console herself.</p>
        <p>"If L'Alouette had not so wilfully taken her departure, like the wild bird she was, Julia would never have seen Joe Nasmyth," was the consolation she offered herself. Meanwhile the bride and bridegroom reached Paris, where Julia was quite at home, as she had lived there during the time Luline was studying for the musical profession; but she had never been there under the happy auspices that now existed, or. had the money to fling about with which Joe Nasmyth so lavishly provided her. A victoria was always at her command, and Julia, who loved luxury and display, was perfectly happy. One day she and her husband were driving along down a narrow, crowded street on their way to the Bon Marche when she suddenly stopped Ale coachman with a decided "Arretez," and, without a word to Joe Nasmyth, sprang out of the carriage, and threw her arms round a very quietly-dressed young woman, who was trudging along the pavement at a brisk pace, as though on much business intent. It was L'Alouette, the queen of song, but save by one who knew her as well as did Julia, she would scarcely have been recognised, since she had dis- carded all the dainty clothing in which. she had been wont to appear, and looked much like a little "trottin " in the pay of some magasin de modes. Joe Nasmyth , who, his first surprise being over, had joined his wife, could scarcely believe that this girl, beautiful though she was, with her large, expressive eyes, was L'Alouette, of whom he had heard so much.</p>
        <p>"Where are you living? Where have you come from? Where are you going ? " The questions rang out more quickly from Julia's lips than did Luline's answers. At last she said: "I am living close to Paris, at St. Germains ; but oh, Ido not want anyone to know; I could not bear it—could not bear that stilted professional life," and she burst out crying. Then she looked at Joe Nasmyth. "Who is this monsieur? Re will not tell anyone that he has seen me? Not take me back to that horrid theatre ? "</p>
        <p>"No, no; not unless you wish it. He is my. husband, Luline. He will do what I tell him," and he laughed. "But why are you living at St. Germains ? Your parents do not live there ? " " No, no. My poor father is dead ! " And the tears awed fast.</p>
        <p>"Bead! And you did noi; return to us or write and tell us. 0 Luline, how unkind of you! You begged me to say nothing and to know nothing of your disappearance, just for a week, so that you might go and see how the old folks were getting on; but to be absent all these months, it was not right —indeed, it was not! Tell her, Joe, how troubled we have all been, and what a commotion there has been about her broken engagement." "I don't care I don't care!" sobbed Luline. "I will never go back." "I am very sorry, Miss Luline, that you dislike your profession .so much," said Nasmyth, in his straightforward, uncouth way; "nevertheless, I cannot say that I think you hare behaved with due consideration either to your good friends, the Darlings, or to the impresario, who has been put to Si) muol. expense on your account." "Hush, Joe, Hush !" whispered Julia, for Luline was becoming quite hysterical from the annoyance his words produced, especially as her own conscience had been giving her sundry severe pricks. Julia's was at that moment the wisest head of the thre, for she forced Luline into the victoria, and instead of pursuing their way to the Bon March, Mr. and Mrs. Nasmyth returned with the long-lost Luline to their hotel in the Rue Castig- licne, where they were staying. Now surely an explanation must be forthcoming; but they did not demand it at once. They talked kindly to Luline; cossetting and soothing her until the storm of tears was subdued, and sunshine once more irradiated the lovely eyes. • "You will return with us to Eneland "Never!" was tne prompt trid decided reply. "What would Fernand say or do ? " "Fernand I 0 Luline, you do not mean that Fernand has—.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>FASHIONS OF TO-DAY.</head>
        <p>FASHIONS OF TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>We live too much, nowadays, in the past or the future, neglecting the present with its fiossibilities. This, if true of every-day life, is also perhaps more so in regard to every-day dress, especially at this season of the year, when there is rather a lack of novelty about the modes. Still, an advantage may be found in this state of affairs if looked at in the right light, as with the future joys of better and brighter days in store we may use our know- ledge of the past fashions in making dainty blous4s or the many items of dress, which are in themselves trifling, but go so far. to achieve</p>
        <p>the ultimate success of the whole toilette. The many spare hours or half-hours that one has at their disposal, when obliged however un- willingly, to remain indoors, owing to the un- pleasant atmospherical conditions which prevail outside, may thus be turned to great use, and enable a certain amount of economy to be employed, as it is generally these very dainty trifles and elaborate blouses decorated with pretty stitchery and insertions of lace</p>
        <p>A SI</p>
        <p>dPLE DRESSING-JACKET.</p>
        <p>which make such a hole in a dress allowance' when purchased ready made in the shops One has really, nowadays, to pay more particularly for, the work used in making the garment, and the time thus employed, than tor the actual material itself. It would be found so much more profitable to turn one's skill as a needlewoman to practical account in the fashioning and embellishment of our clothes, than to waste it in embroidering cushions or counterpanes.</p>
        <p>Even the simple dressing jacket depicted in my first sketch offers scope for clever fingers in the conventional designs of the border, which is the only trimming which adorns it. The [material used is pink flannel of a delicate, but very becoming shade, and the embroider- ed pattern is worked in pale green silk on a white ground. The style of the dressing jacket is very simple, but also exceedingly pretty, and partaking very much of the style of a Japanese, kimono. The sleeves are close- fitting at the upper part of the arm, increasing in dimensions towards the wrist, where they fail freely, not being confined by any cuffs or</p>
        <p>IILETTE.</p>
        <p>wrist bands. The jacket fastens at one side, and a cord of green silk with a couple of hand- some tassels midway crosses the upper part of the figure. Viyella could be used if preferred, although it is hardly so warm as flannel, while some of the charmingly-patterned Zena- na cloths wpuld make up into particularly cosy garments. Flannelette is, of course, very much less expensive, but is much lighter, almost requiring a lining to render it warm enough. Quilted satin, lined with silk or a thin woollen material, makes a very pretty anti very cosy jacket. Very charming indeed is the pretty evening toilette given in my second sketch, and the style is one which not only must recommend itself to anyone looking out for a pretty mode for a new gown, but would prove helpful, I feelsure, for the adaptation or renovation of an old dress. Sleeves in which there is a gap showing glimpses of the upper arm between the shoulder strap and the sleeve proper, are quite one of the smartest features of this season's evening toilettes, and an old sleeve can easily be so altered and modified to pro- duce this effect while a series of full frills or flounces should depend from the lower part of the sleeve to about the wrist, The bodice and skirt are of finely pleated material, and a</p>
        <p>quite simple in design, a panel of la :e or em- broidery being inserted in the front of the bodice. There are many pretty materials to be had for evening wear, and dainty silver and gold embroidered tissues which give quite a faipv-like appearance to silk and chiffon dresses. Beaded nets, in various coloured beads ranging from gold and silver to many coloured sprinklings, are used for draping bodices or covering them in blouse form for evening wear. One model gown had it draped on the hips in pannier fashion, with the desired result of imparting an air of origin- ality to the toilette. Another of pale pink satin had a trellis work of gold which was most effective, especially as here and there tiny pink and white roses were introduced. In purchasing headgear just now it is as well to confine one's selection to something which is smart but simple, as it is yet too early to attempt anything very elaborate in the way of millinery. A particularly good sample of what I mean is portrayed in my last sketch, the only trimming of the chic felt hat con- sisting of some bands of velvet and a large feather porn porn. Fur is also much employed as a trimming on hats, and is sometimes intermingled with somewhat incongruous materials such as fragile lace and diaphanous chiffon. One does not see so many furry surfaced felts as a short time ago, although some of those in the marquise shape were truly admirable and needed absolutely no trim. ming whatever.</p>
