      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>MISSING DIVA.</head>
        <p>A MISSING DIVA.</p>
        <p>BY JEAN MIDDLEMASS,</p>
        <p>Author of "A Life's; Surrender," "In Storm and Strife," "The Case of David Lisle," "The Yellow Badge," "Love is Lord of All," Bco.</p>
        <p>"In that box to your right is Lady Mauleverer, a charming blonde, and in the next to her are the Prince and Princess of Louisville. Rich? I believe you. LOok at her diamonds 1" "And the superb-looking, dark beauty in pink ? "</p>
        <p>• "Is the Duchess of Marleville. What a full house! Quite a triumph for L'Alouette. How she is adored Always a full house when she is to appear. You have heard her, of course ? "</p>
        <p>_ "No. This is my first appearance. I have just arrived from South Africa."</p>
        <p>" Ali ! "</p>
        <p>And the man who apparently knew by sight all the celebrities with whom the Grand Opera Houk) was packed, looked his companion over as though querying whether he was to be avoided or cultivated. They were not old acquaintances, had scarcely even caught each other's names when they had been hurriedly mentioned by a club friend, who, passing to his own stall, had seen that they were going to sit side by side. "And you have never seen L'Alouette ? " asked Sir Claude Edwardes, his survey being over and the decision arrived at that at all events for that evening he would cultivate the stranger. "Never. I heard of her for the first time when I saw her name on the programme," and the colonial smiled a little superciliously as he noted the surprise expressed on Sir Claude's face. He was not, lowever, prepared for the rhapsody his ignorance evoked. "Never yet seen her! What a happy man to have such a treat in store as a first sight of L'Alouette ! She is a sorceress that enslaves every- one, men and women alike. When she sings there is never a vacant seat in the house." "Fair or dark ? "</p>
        <p>"Dark, and such a voice! But you will not have long to wait; it is half-past eight. I wonder why the curtain does not go up. People are murmuring—exactly—punctuality is a virtue. The musicians are all whispering in the orchestra. What can have happened? The upper boxes are getting excited. The agitation is spreading t.o the stalls. Something must be wrong. Ah, the manager!" "Ladies and gentlemen, I must implore your kind patience for a short time. La Diva has not arrived. I have sent a messenger to her house to inquire the cause. If Madame Santiaga " "L'Alouette ! L'Alouette ! " shouted the whole house in unison. Excitement was in the ascendancy; suspense prevailed. The manager looked implor- ingly around, but it was useless to speak another word in the tumult that now prevailed; so with a gesture, he was once more lost to view behind the curtain.</p>
        <p>A terrible half-hour of disturbance and dis- quietude was passed. Since the public would not accept Madame Santiaga as a substitute, no further attempt was made to impose her. They must wait with as much patience as they could command. Some ladies fainted, some grew hysterical, and many of the boxes became empty. The police vainly tried to subdue the agitation, but their interference only served to increase if, and by eleven 'o'clock—all the elite of the assemblage having departed in terror—a free fight ensued, Which was only stopped after much damage to property, by a strong official reinforcement, and, truncheon in hand, the building was cleared. Among the last to leave were Sir Claude Edwardes and his new acquaintance. As they stood together outside the Opera House, the colonial said he would not have missed the sight of the wild excitement for the world; it was far more worth seeing and hearing than all the Alouettes in creation. And," he added, "now we must follow up the story, and discover what the deuce has become of her."</p>
        <p>Sir Claude was nothing loth, and, as he seemed to know everyone, Was soon busily engaged in Making inquiries, but With little result. No one held the key of the mystery. L'Alouette had left home at the usual .hour, with the apparent inten- tion of going as usual to the theatre; but she had not arrived, nor did anyone know where she had gone. In the course of the investigation some account of her domestic relations naturallly transpired. L'Alouette, whose name was Luline Etchcouf, was a sweet, Attractive little beauty iabout twenty-two, who, when in London, lived with some Americans in the Bloomsbury district. That was all that was known about her; the rest was a mystery. Since her sixteenth birthday- she had been about so much —half over the world, in fact—that when asked her nationality she invariably answered that she was a cosmopolitan—partly because that was true, and partly because she did noc choose that her ante- cedents should be raked up for public amusement. Not even newspaper interviewers could arrive at the truth. She spoke three or four languages with perfect fluency, but none of them with perfect grammar; hence it was quite impossible for anyone to decide in which country she claimed a birthright. L'Alouette's forebears were of the peasant class —working in the fields for their daily bread. They had dwelt for generations in a picturesque village at the foot of the Pyrenees, and the language of L'Alouette's infancy—the pure Basque—was the only one to which she never treated her professional associates and fashionaCe admirers, having a foolish, perverted idea that she would be slighted if her lowly origin was suspected. Silly little Luline ! She made herself utterly miserable lest the worthy, honest, hard-working couple, whose child she was; should, by any inad- vertancy, be made to take a place in her new and successful career. Yet she loved them both very dearly, and often during the many reactions which perforce succeeded an hour of triumph, she would cry her heart out because the dear mother was not there to sympathise, or the strong, handsome father, who might have been a king, so noble was his bearing, was not by her side to shelter and protect her. Little did those by whom she was surrounded guess how the feted brilliant Alouette would every now and again long and yearn for the Basque village, not only that she might be pressed to the hearts of the worthy couple who dwelt there, but that she might experience the freedom of the old childish days, and be 'regaled once more by the sight of the handsome young stripling who had been her companion in many a daring adventure. How he had begged her not to be tempted away from them when an American impresario and his family had come over from Paris to visit the beauteous village, and, filled with admiration for the sweet notes of L'Alouette's voice, had, when he heard her trilling like the lark, whose name he gave her, among. the mountain passes around her home, offered lr a musical education in a large city. Fernand had begged and implored her not to go, but Luline was obstinate and ambitious. "To make a name for herself, to become a great singer—this monsieur said it was possible; so, of course, she would go. Was she to remain a little country mouse all her life solely to please Fernand? " Yet many and many .a time during the last five years, she had longed for Fernand; and when triumphant success had been attained, she seemed to long for him still more—but he never shewed any sign. Only once had she been home during these years of training, and then he was absent, had gone away to a chateau near Paris, to do some work for it nobleman who lived there; for Fernand was by trade a stone-hewer, and such a clever manipulator that he might almost be designated an. artist. More about him L'Alouette could not learn: Her people were illiterate and vague, and when she left them to return to her work and her triumphs, she heard no more about Fernand, . since neither her father nor mother could write, and the few missives she received were indited either by the schoolmaster or the parish priest, who never mentioned Fernand's name. Yet she was often troubled about him, and, as time went on, she longed for him more and more. It almost seemed as if the constant tributes of admiration and adoration she received, paled before an overwhelming longing for Fernand, and the simple-hearted, earnest affgtion which had erst been hers. The only confidante Luline had was the young American, with whose mother she had been placed by the impresario who took her from her native village. This girl, Julia Darling, was two years younger than herself, and was as devoted to Luline as she was to her. Everyone who knew L'Alouette knew of this girl-friendship, and amongst others, Sir Claude was fully cognisant of it. "Let us go at once to Taverton-street and find out what the Darlings know," he said to his com- panion, to whom he felt strongly drawn by his ATee-Pd;ng interest in the case. "Late, isn't it no • professional peophi never go to be 4 until the small hours." . _</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>The Opening of Parliament.—The presence of the King and Queen gives to the opening of Parliament a soeial importance it scarcely had in the latter years of the late Queen's reign. It is true that the landedaristoc- racy now form but a small part of Society, but they are still a very important part, and a Royal opening of Parliament means in many instances an earlier return to their great London houses than would otherwise be the case. The full muster of peeresses wholare privileged to attend the opening of Parliament, is one of the results of the presence of their Majesties, and wearing fall-dress evening toilettes, with a gorgeous display of jewels, they give an added splendour to the scene in the House of Lords on these occasions. But lhough the opening of Parliament is generally regard as the inaugural event of the fashionable season, it is not until after Lent that Society returns in full strength to the Metropolis. In Parliamentary circles there is some anxiety as to the course events may take, lest political affairs should militate againsta successful social season, but it only shows how mixed Society has become, now that the fashionable drapery firms attach more import- ance to a revival on the Stock Exchange, than to any other influence affecting their prosperity. Modes at Weddings.—Saturday continues to be far and away the most popular day for weddings, and next on the list come Tuesday, and Thursday. Monday ranks as the next best day, then comes Wednesday, while a Friday wedding is a rare event indeed. The most fashionable hour for the ceremony is half-past two, and undoubtedly it is a very conyenient one,as it does away with all hurry and bustle in the early morning, and moreover permits of a light lunch being partaken of before the ceremony. Not a few recent Society brides have elected to carry a prayer book, bound in white vellum, ivory, or mother-of-pearl, instead of a bouquet, but one misses the familiar, finishing touch to the toilette, afforded by the flowers. The number of brides- maids would seem to increase annually, for this season it has been no uncommon sight to see a brilliant train of twelve fair girls, and in one or two instances, the number has risen above, that.</p>
        <p>is, like the water we filter, simply a harbouring place for germs, and more likely to be the cause than the preventive of disease. Apparently it ought to be dis- infected daily, but then so ought our boots, and it would almost seem that the only way to escape the manifold dangers that surround us, would be to live in a germ-proof house and never stir out of Woe as it is everything we eat, drink, or do,is conderaned in its turn by the medical journals. Hay Oven Cooking.—ln the winter, the poorer classes of Denmark and Norway have a device for cooking porridge for the morning, which saves much trouble and firing. Before the evening fire is allowed to go down, they bring the meal and water to the boil, in a suitable pot, and this is then placed in the hay oven. This is nothing more than a large box stuffed full of hay, in which the pot is embedded, and covered in tightly with a cushion also filled with hay. With the heat kept in, the porridge goes on slowly cooking, and is turned out quite hot for the morning meal. Food already cooked can also be kept hot for an indelLiite time by similar means, and seeing how dear coals are, it is surprising that the poor in this country do net adopt a similar system in order to have the benefit of hot meals. Laws for Housewives.—The municipal authorities are constantly framing minor Acts for the public good and convenience, but it is usually left to householders to find out as best they can what the new regulations require. Everyone is supposed to know the laws of the land, but that is only a fiction, as the housewife remark- ed when she was fined seven shillings beeause her maid had shaken out the front doormat after sight o'clock in the morning. It seems that in London this is an offence punishable by a fine, but it is doubtful if one in ten thousand housewives know it, and it is only too prob- able that they are equally ignorant of the divers pains and penalties for permitting servant girls to clean the outside of windows if above a certain height from the ground. MADGE.</p>
        <p>Fashionable Materials.—Cheeks and Plaids, also tar- tans ere much in fsvour, and those women who have secured a dress length of any of these fabrics at the re- cent sales, should have it made up forthwith. It should be remembered however, that the charms of all such matenals are considerably heightened by a trimming of plain cloth, whioh should match exactly one of the colors in the check, plaid, or tartan. The wise purchaser will select small checks or broken plaids which are very much more becoming and as the home dressmaker will quickly discover more economical. Sun-ray Pleating.—For gowns, theatre coats and cloaks, tea, and dressing gowns sun-ray pleating, is much employed,and proves invariably a success. Crepe- de-chine is a delightful fabric so manipulated, as also is chiffon velours, chiffon voile,and mousseline-de-soie, but nun's veiling, voile, spotted net, or chiffon cloth all look well, sun-ray pleated. For a skirt the fabric has to be seamed up into a largesquare.then the corners are rounded off, and a hole is made in the centre for the waist. The larger the square, and the hole in the centre, the fuller the skirt will be, but eight yards of double width material makes a very good skirt indeed. All establishments that undertake sun-ray pleating will, for a very moderate sum, cut and seam up the garment, if duly instructed in the style preferred, and this is the wiser course for the amateur who is not sure. of her ground. If wrongly cut and seamed, the pleating is sure to be wrong. The Pouched Blouse.—There has been much difference of opinion concerning the merits of the pouched blouse or bodice, but unless greatly exaggerated, there is no doubt about the mode being a becoming one. And it is to last, at any rate, while the straight-fronted corset remains, and happily, there is no sign of this comfort- able and much more healthy cut of corset being on the wane. The hip yoke has disappeared. an item the home "' Joseph Walker, a signalman, residing at Dallam Cot- dressmaker will not deplore, as she rarely, if ever, tage, off Winecick road, appeared at the Warrington managed to get it to sit properly. Police Court on Monday, in answer to a summons Tea Drinking.—Excessive tea drinking has been con- charging him with failing to notify three cases of small- demned as second only to alcoholism, in the harmful pox to the Medical Officer of Health. effects it has upon tliose'who indulge in it. When Mr. Stanley Wilson prosecuted on behalS of the pretty nearly everything else has been condemned as Health Committee and said that in consequence of in- more or less dangerous, it would be strange indeed if formation which reached the Inspector of nuisances, the tea escapad. But in the case of tea there is unquestion- Medical Officer and Inspector Flood visited the defend- ably something more than faddist theories. Excessive ant's house on Thursday of last week. They found tea drinking was thought by a Royal Commission to be the defendant's wife and a son, aged nineteen one of the causes of national physical deterioration, recovering from severe attacks of smallpox. The now so much talked about, and it is said by eminent house, it appeared, had ten occupants—defendant, his authorities, to also account for many of the cases of wife, seven children, and a lodger. Three of the chit- total loss of memory which appear ,almost daily in the dren were attending school and five of the men were papers. One however, can only imagine such a serious following their employment. They had, in fact, been outcome to be the result of drinking stewed black tea working during the whole of the time that the disease by the quart. As light refreshment, afternoon tea is had been in the house. In Dr. Gornall's opinion the one of the most delightful diversions of the day, but defendant's wife and son had bean suffering hem small- Dr. Hutchinson in a recent lecture on "The Food of pox for some weeks, and they were at once removed to the People" whol