      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>RHEUMATISM CAN BE CURED.</head>
        <p>RHEUMATISM CAN BE CURED.</p>
        <p>Anyone who doubts that Rheumatism can be cured should ask Mrs. E. K. Stokes, Hither street, near Wrentham, Suffolk. This is what Mrs. Stokes says: "I was a martyr to Rheumatism and was several months in hospital. I would get a little better for a time but would soon be laid up again. I was weak, my limbs ached, my feet were sore, I had to go up- stairs on my hands and knees, drawing myself up by my hands along the rail. I was almost helpless and was drawn out of shape. "I began taking Dodd's Kidney Pills, and continued until I was completely cured. Mv work is now a pleasure to me, and I feel better thanl have for years." Another lady who claims that Dodd's Kidney Pills will cure Rheumatism is Mrs. Pollard, Lodge Lane, Bolney, Sussex. She says : Until I took Dodd's Kidney Pills I never knew what it was to have my body free from pain; if the Rheumatism was not in my hands, it would be in my feet, for it was always somewhere, and nothing I took ever seemed to shake it off or give me any relief, until I used Dodd's Kidney Pills. They have worked wonders in my case, and I feel like another woman."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>SMALLPDX OUTBREAK. Heavy Penalty for Failing to Notify Cases.</head>
        <p>THE SMALLPDX OUTBREAK. Heavy Penalty for Failing to Notify Cases.</p>
        <p>"I am his wife. I will never leave him. I will not return to the old life at the theatre."</p>
        <p>"Fernand's wife! You, the feted tuline, the queen of song, wife of a simple peasant ! " And the sorrow expressed on Julia Nasmyth's face was very genuine, while the tears rose into the eyes of the rough, usually unsympathetic Joe, as he felt much kindliness for the girl, who 110 cast on one side ambition, wealth, and all the luxuries of life, for the sake of this peasant-lover. "But it is impossible, Luline ! You cannot even pretend that you are happy ! " cried Julia, who, the more she thought about it, the more she was annoyed, not only at L'Alouette's temerity in can- celling jier engagements, but that for her oft sake she should have thus, ruthlessly cast away for ever the luxuries and benefits with which for so long her wonderful talent had surrounded her. "You think I am unhappy," answered the ex- diva, springing up excitedly. 'You think, perhaps, lam a poor, miserable peasant, who tills the land and draws water, for my daily maintenace. Well, come and see for yourself. Come with me to St. Germains ; I was going to the station when I met you. Come and see my Fernand, and judge for yourself whether my present life or the public career I have left behind me is preferable." Julia consulted her husband by a glance. He bowed his head in acquiescence as he said: "If madame will permit we will accompany her home. Perhaps her husband will absist in solving the difficulty of coming to an arrangement with the manager of the opera-house, who has been so dis- advantageously deserted." Straightforward Joe Nasmyth was always desirous to right a wrong, even when, as in this instance, sentiment had been awakened.</p>
        <p>A few Yainutes later and the trio were speeding away to Germains, which they reached just as the sun was shedding its last lustrous rays over the grand old forest. t'Alouette, who seemed to Julia to have sud- denly re-assumed some of the important manner in which she had usually wrapped herself in the days of her great triumphs, called a carriage, but did not give an address to the driver. She was apparently too well known for it to be necessary. After a drive of about a quarter of an hour the carriage stopped at a pretty chcilet which had been built at the edge of the forest and seemed to be almost embedded among majestic trees. A big man in loose blue trousers and a velvet coat came forward to greet them. He had dark, wavy hair, which, according to English ideas, should have been cut far shorter than it was, for it lay almost upon his shoulders; but he had a strong, virile countenance on which manly beauty had set an indelible mark, and there was nothing of the peasant, either in his appearance or his mien. Nevertheless, he was Fernand, the worker in stone, who, a giant in his art, had lined his pockets with gold, and dared to rob the world of L'Alouette's talents as a cantatrice.</p>
        <p>.He was pleased to see his wife's friends, especially when he knew that the lady was Julia, of whom he had heard so much. And he told them o, simply and genially, while they, on their part, were so impressed and surprised by the unexpected majesty of this man whom they had been prepared to despise, that they seemed almost incapable of expressing any feelings at all. That is, not for some time, for after awhile the geniality of this son of the people seemed to thaw all frigidity between them. That Luline had cast in her lot with Fernand's when, after the lapse of years she had met him again at her father's funeral, seemed no longer to be a wonder or a scandal; only, as practical Joe Nasmyth said: "The world must no longer be left in the dark about her fate, and the impresario must be con- ciliated and fairly dealt with. If a sum of money was needed for this purpose he was ready to come forward, for of what use was it to create wealth if help was not available when a fried was in need of assistance ! " Fernand, however, would not hear of this. "We have only been keeping silent so long," he said, "that I might collect a sum sufficiently large to .offer as an equivalent for the loss of L'Alouette. '</p>
        <p>"The sum Fernand has arranged to give is far too large," cried Luline, excitedly. "He must not be permitted to deprive himself of repose and luxuries for so undeserving a reason."</p>
        <p>"You set your talents ad services at a very low figure, eh, madame ? " Joe Nasmyth inquired, with a laugh. "On the contrary I consider that Mr. Wilkins has been amply repaid by the large sums I have brought into his coffers every time I have sung for him, and if I choose to depart and make my own happiness he has no cause to complain." "I am afraid you will not find it so," said Julia. "However, we must communicate with him and make the best terms possible. Now we must go or we shall be belated ere we reach Paris."</p>
        <p>no, you will stay here. Your luggage must be fetched; my maid and yours will arrange it. You will stay, Julia, in our chdlet, you will." And Luline's arms were around her friend's neck. There was no refusal possible though a compromise was made.</p>
        <p>"They would send for some things and stay a few days but they would not give up their rooms at the hotel."</p>
        <p>So it was settled, and a right happy time they enjoyed. No thought of irate impresarios or managers being permitted to mingle with the enjoyment of the double menage until one day, the very man himself from whom they felt that ere long some communication must reach them, was seen, coming in at the gate. That storm was in the air Luline felt certain, and, seized with a sudden fit of cowardice—though she had until now appeared to be so brave—she turned to flee. Fernand, who had just come in from his workshop, put his arm round her. _ _ -</p>
        <p>• I. 1•./ • "Have no fear, aerie. lam by your side and our good friends will mediate." And well was it that Mr. and Mrs. Nasmyth were present, since the impresario had a great respect, not only for Joe's thousands, but for his commonsense. Without kis presence the matter might not have been so easy to arrange. Mr. Wilkins, however, was a better-hearted fellow than an outward coat of bluster and irritability would have made people suspect. He heard all that Joe Nasmyth and Fernand had to say about the money they were ready to pay on Luline's behalf, and he watched her, meanwhile, as she stood pale and trembling by Fernand's side, occasionally hiding her shamed face on his arm. This new aspect of the proud, self-important diva amused the American—perhaps it touched him. Still, he did not intend to let her Qff scot free, nor did he wish to be out-done in generosity by these two men.</p>
        <p>_ "pardon me, gentlemen," he said, "but it is L'Alouette who has broken her contract. She alone can compensate."</p>
        <p>• There was an uncomfortable movement, and Fernand's _arm tightened round his wife's waist.</p>
        <p>Was this resaute American, who considered that she belonged to him, going to carry her off by force.</p>
        <p>He smiled when lie saw the sensation his words had evoked, and held out his hand to Luline.</p>
        <p>"I think we can manage it, petite, without making you quite miserable, but you know you deserve a punishment." Luline trembled.</p>
        <p>"You will come to London as soon as we re-open the opera season, which will be in about a month, and you will sing three times in your favourite parts, .after which I will return the papers- you have signed, and you shall go back to your idyllic life in this superb old forest. The money I shall obtain for these three representations will, in some degree, compensate me for my pecuniary loss, while your re-appearance will show the public that L'Alouette is a loyal and true woman as well as a great singer." There was silence for a moment when Mr. Wilkins had finished speaking, then Luline dis- engaged herself • from her husband's arm and said: "I will do what you wish, monsieur. I owe you reparation, and I thank you for making it possible to me. You will forgive me, will you not, and believe that love is stronger than ambition, and life among the forest glades is happier than in the glare of the footlights? " So the affair was amicably settled. The im- presario, at Fernand's invitation, shared their mid- day repast, and, judging from the merriment of the little party assembled round the well-spread board, no one would have imagined that a shadow had so lately been athwart their joint interests. Luline kept her engagement with punctuality and loyalty. Never before had she sung so gloriously, or awakened more public enthusiasm. Sir Claude Edwar'des was beside himself with rapture. Even Fernand, who had accompanied her to England, and now heard her sing in public for the first time, was almost sorry that, for his sake, • she had retired from so brilliant a career. •</p>
        <p>But Luline herself reloiced when the curtain went down on her last appearance. She was long in for the chide in the St. Gerrnains forest where she rettrect rortnwitn, aria where the impresario is a very frequent and very welcome guest, vainly though he constantly tries once more to tempt her on to the boards.</p>
        <p>flun END. 1</p>
      </div>
