      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>VLADIVOSTOK AND CIIEMULPO.</head>
        <p>VLADIVOSTOK AND CIIEMULPO.</p>
        <p>This illustration gives a good idea of the character of those two important Far Eastern ports from which so many startling ninjours have issued, Vladivostok and. Chemulpo.</p>
        <p>Vladivostok is one of the finest harbours in the Far East, and Russia's chief seaport and naval station in the Pacific Ocean, and lies at the end of a long peninsula running into Peter the Great Bay, at the southern extremity of the Maritime Province. The entrance is divided into two narrow channels by Dundas Island. The harbour, which is surrounded by hills on all sides, runs for half a mile in a northerly direction and then turns east for another mile. , Though situated in the same 'latitude as Boston, N'ladivostok is ice-bound for four months of the year—from December to April ; but a powerful ice-breaker keeps a passage open for the shipping. There is a floating dock, accommodating vessels up to 3,000 tons, and a graving dock, measuring 625 feet by 'jo, with a depth of 30 feet. The town, which has a population of 30,000, is built on the north side of the harbour, and has several fine buildings. There are profes- sional schools, a naval hospital, and barracks, with space for poo,ooo men. A railway runs northwards to Karbarovsk, ,on the Amur, a</p>
        <p>ctance of 475 miles, and another line runs east to join the Trans-Siberian Railway at Harbin.</p>
        <p>Chemulpo was opened to foreign trade 20 years since, and to-day it possesses imposing - looking shops and a train service which con- nects it with the capital, Seoul, about 24 miles away. There is a Japanese, a Chinese, and a Korean quarter, and a general foreign quarter in Chemulpo. The Japanese is the best situated and the most promising. The • number of Japanese is about 6,000, while the Europeans number only about ioo. In Che- mulpo, as in all the ports of Korea which are</p>
        <p>ol,en to foreign trade, there is a branch of the Imperial Korean Maritime Customs, an offshoot of the service which exists in China, under the administration of Mr. McLeavy Brown. The Emperor has lately sanctioned the grant of one million yen from the Customs revenue for the provision of aids to naviga- tion. Thirty-one lighthouses are to be built, the two earliest being placed upon Roze and Round Islands, off the entrance to the Ham River, upon which Chemulpo stands. The -worst point about the port is that the tide runs out some seven or eight miles, making the landing of troops there a difficult matter.</p>
        <p>THE BISHOP OF NASSAU.</p>
        <p>The news of the death by drowning Of Dr. Henry Norris Churton, Bishop of Nassau, Bahamas, has been received with the tmost regret in England, for he had a wide circle of friends here. Dr. Churton,who was the fourth on cf Archdeacon Churton, of Cleveland, was bornin 1843 and educated at Eton and Uni- versity College, Oxford. In 1868 he was ordained to the curacy of Stoke-on-Trent, and subse- quently served curactes at East Retf or d and Christ Church, Wes tßromwich. From 1879 to 1883 he was Vicar of St. John the Evan- gelist,_West</p>
        <p>Bromwich; from I 88 4 to 1891 curate of St. James's Chapel, Avonwick ; and from 1892 to 901 he was diocesan treasurer and missioner of Nassau. From 1893 till 1901 he was ex- amining chaplain to the Bishop of Nassau ; lie was Archdeacon of the Bahamas from 1898 till 590 : and he was consecrated Bishop of Nassau in St. Paul's Cathedral on July 54th, 1902, being elected to the See in succession to his brother, who' resigned.</p>
        <p>RIS CHURTON.</p>
        <p>f ' • 4,1 .:`,• • .; THE LATE DR. HENRY *OR</p>
