      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>CHAMPION WRESTLER.</head>
        <p>THE CHAMPION WRESTLER.</p>
        <p>The much - talked -of wrestling match between Hackenschmidt and Madrali which aroused unusual excitement in London ended in a fiasco on Saturday night. There has been a great revival in the old sport of wrestling of late, dating from the time when Georges Hackenschmidt . came over from Russia two years ago and vanquished all rivals. . It culminated tV,To months ago when Ahmed Madrali the Sultan's own private wrestler arrived from Turkey and threw down the gauntlet. He, too, vanquished all Opponents with remarkable ease, and only Haekenschmidt was left to be met for "the championship of the world." Consequently, endeavours were made to arrange the match, but there were many difficulties in the way. Chief of all was the question of rules, Hacken- Schmidt refusing to wrestle in any other style than the Grmco - Roman, which he always adopts, while Madrali has all his life Wrestled in the catch-as-catch-can fashion. However, sooner than have no match, the latter gave way, and it was arranged for the two champions to wrestle in the Grieco- Roman style, which precludes gripping below the waist and tripping, for liz)o a-side and a large purse, at Olympia, on Saturday night last. There was a tremendous rush for seats,</p>
        <p>ARMED MADRALI. GEORGES HACKENSCHMIDT.</p>
        <p>some of which went for very high prices, and the great hall was packed with an excited crowd. Madrali, the favourite, is the bigger man of the two, standing 6 ft. tin, in height and weighing 15 st. 7 lbs. ; while Hacken- Schmidt is only 5 ft. 9 in. and a stone lighter, but the latter is a perfect Hercules in development, even beside his rival. Both stepped into the ring in perfect condition a few minutes after nine o'clock. Then came the fiasco. Almost at once the "Terrible Turk" got a grip of the "Russian Lion," who finessed for a second and then brought off a remarkable fall; forcing Madrali fairly and squarely on the ground. The whistle blew 'forty-four seconds after the start, crediting Hackenschmidt with first fall, the match being best two out of three. Then it was seen that the Turk was in pain, and it Was discovered that his left elbow had been dislocated. It was subsequently set by Dr. Jackson Lang, the official doctor t the National Sporting Club,. but, of course, it was</p>
