        <p>THE SWEDISH ANTARCTIC EXPEDITION.</p>
        <p>botanist, Mr. DR. NORDENSKJ OLD. Skottsberg ; a doctor and bacteriologist, Dr. EkelO if; a magnetician hydrographer, Dr. Bodman ; and Lieutenant Duse, of the Swed- isly Artillery, as cartographer. Making Mount Bfansfield on Louis Philippe Island head- quarters for a re-union, Dr. Nordenskjold with Lieutenant Anderson and two sailors left the rest of the expedition and pushed south for a couple of hundred miles on sledges, return- ing in due course to Mount Bransfield. Mean- while, in December, 1902, the unthrtunate Antarctic had got shut in impenetrable ice in Terror Bay and went to the bottom. The men, with all they could save, made their way across the ice to the volcanic isle of Paulet, sixteen days' journey, and there they en- camped, sending a small party to meet Dr. Nordenskjold at Mount Bransfield. Last November he and his party were rescued by the Argentine steamer Uruguay. After a search they found their comrades on Paulet Island, and all were in due course landed safely at Santa Cruz.</p>
