      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>" FISCALITIS."</head>
        <p>" FISCALITIS."</p>
        <p>defined by black and red spots. In the centre is a little wooden loaf, surrounded by a wall, and the object of the game is for the Protectionists to get four men over the wall, one of whom must be Mr. Chamberlain) The Free Traders endeavour to prevent this and, take their opponents draught-fashion, meanwhile, the pieces may only move one spot at a time, and never diagonally; and the four Free Traders have the advantage ol being able, outside the Tariff wall, to take their opponents in any direction, while the Protectionists, with the _exception of their leader, may only take forward. The Protec- tionists have first move and can only leap the tariff wall from an outside red spot to an inside black spot.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>WEATHER PLANT.</head>
        <p>THE WEATHER PLANT.</p>
        <p>• The poor old barometer is in danger of being dupplanted by a much more useful in- strument, provided by Nature herself. The barometer has been a good friend for many years, advising umbrellas and -fulfilling many other useful functions: but it has never been sufficiently pro- phetic. How- ever the new</p>
        <p>system promises</p>
        <p>tO predict t_h e</p>
        <p>weather for</p>
        <p>many days</p>
        <p>'aheadrgiving timely warning</p>
        <p>of when to</p>
        <p>expect rain and</p>
        <p>when drought. This new system</p>
        <p>been</p>
        <p>PROFESSOR NOWACK.</p>
        <p>11\ \ 11</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>worked out by Professor John __</head>
        <p>worked out by Professor John __</p>
        <p>Nowack</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>nearly twenty years' study of</head>
        <p>nearly twenty years' study of</p>
        <p>a tropical</p>
        <p>"weather</p>
        <p>plant:"</p>
        <p>wonderful</p>
        <p>botanical di s-</p>
        <p>covery he claims</p>
        <p>is under certain conditions. so sensitive that the leaves and twigs move in the most extra- ordinary way: and it is from the action at the leaves and the way they point that he is able to forecast what is going to take place in the atmosphere. From London he can operate within a circle of 3,000 miles in any direction ; and he is able to issue charts a month ahead showing all seismic and atmos- pheric disturbances within this radius from a single station, instead of many as at present. A week ahead Professor Nowack can issue a daily chart, forecasting the weather in all districts with absolute accuracy, even down to fog and its density, which puzzles other meteorologists. He shows the paths of gales and cyclones and locates the time and place of earthquakes. He has put his system thoroughly to the test and now considers it perfected after twenty years ,of study and experiment. In 1892 he sent the Royal Meteoro- logical Society his charts for thirty-five years from 1883 to 1918 : and it is his theory that certain disturbances, owing to natural pheno- mena, run in cycles of that duration.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, February 6, 1904. WARRINGTON DRAUGHTS LEAGUE. FOOTBALL</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, February 6, 1904. WARRINGTON DRAUGHTS LEAGUE. FOOTBALL</p>
        <p>---- During the past week four matckes were played in USSET'S PORTABLE BlLLlARDS.—Scientifically con- the League Competition,at the rooms of the first named IQ clubs, the results being as follows : .I_, structe4 perfectly accurate, excellent workmanship, WARRINGION D. &amp; C.C. PA DGATE INSTITUTE. superior finish, bit. £4 10s.. 6ft. £6155., 7ft. £7 ss. Obtain wi. ns from local agent the Special Catalogue of GEORGE G. BUSSXY Wins. Draws. &amp; co., Billiard Manufacturers, London. 0 F. Dunne 2 W. Warburton IJ. Dixon IC. Roberts 2 J. Nattrass 1 E. P. Dunne 1 G. Holcroft 0 2 J. H. ,Dunne 0 J. W. Pomfrey 0 0 *Absentee - WARRINGTON v. BROUGHTON RANGERS. 2 At Warrington, before 8,000 spectators. Warrington had to substitute Mahoney for R. Bate. The Rangers early attacked, and fine passing between the Jameses, Wilson, and Hogg was successfully dealt with by Fish. . _ _ .</p>
