      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>Cairo-street</head>
        <p>Cairo-street</p>
        <p>Warrington 4 2 2 0 Fletcher Russell's 4 2 2 0 . Monks Hall &amp; Co's 3 1 2 0 . Padgate Institute 2 0 2 0 ... Crosfield's</p>
        <p>SIMULTANEOUS DRAUGHTS' PLAYING.</p>
        <p>There was a good muster, of the members of Rich- mond's Draughts Club at the Coffee Rooms, B idge- street, last Thursday evening to meet Mr. F. Dunne, who had engaged to give them an exhibition of simul- taneous draughts playing. Ten boards wets arranged, and the players took their seats to oppose the champion not without exhibiting signs of trepidation in teveral instances. Mr. Dunne allowed his opponents the first move .on five of the boards, he himself starting the games on the remainder. The play went briskly 00, the singe player walking round all the time from beard to board, and only occesionally halting for a tew moments at one or other of the boards to take a 'long shot," beneath which his opponent's game crumbled away like a house of cards. Play was continued for abcut an hour and an half, the score at the conclusion reading fifteen wins to Mr. Dunne and three games drawn. Two of the drawn gamca were secured by Mr. W. Toml.'nson and one by Mr. M. Go:,nan. Following is the deta;:ed score : Wills. Draws. Wins. 2 F. Dunne 2 /, 1 7P 2 ~, 1 Pt 1 PP 2 , 1 ./ 0 ~, 1 IP 1 ~ 0 PP 1 PP</p>
        <p>~. 25 20 10 ..._• 4 There was only a moderate attendance at Runcorn to .. 24 29 9 ... 4 witness this match. Farmer started for Runcorn and .. 14 22 5 ... 2 Swinton at once became aggressive, but Wallwork spoiled 6 16 8 0 a good opening by passing the ball forward. Then a .. 10 44 8 0 fine piece of passing between Molyneux, Hampson, and IWallwork ended in the latter scoring a try, but Preston's ' kick struck the post and rebounded. On the resumption Runcorn attacked, and Jolley, Richardson, and Butter- worth got to the Swinton end, and a moment later Warder was stopped when almost over. Swinton then got possession, and forced Runcorn back. The home for- wards at length broke away with a dribble, and Dixon was caught over the Swinton line before he could return, but from the ensuing scrummage Molyneux relieved. Molyneux again got possession,and passed to Hampton, who ran three-parts the length of the field and scored. Preston kicked a • goal. Half-time—Swinton 1 goal, 2 tries (8 points), Runcorn nil. The second portion of the game was greatly in Run- corn's favour. Richardsonand Flanagan each had hard luck in not scoring, but at le agthJolley got possession, and passed to J. Richardson, who dodged through and scored. Farmer failed at goal. Resuming, Swinton were penalised,and Jolley placed a goal. Runcorn were now only three points behind,and made desperate efforts to score. At length Butterworth sent the ball to Dixon, who made a faulty kick sending the ball over his own line near the corner flag. There N%...s a/race for posses- sion, and Flanagan successfully grounded the ball. Farmer failed at goal, and a keen and exciting game ended in a draw. Result :—Runcorn 1 goal, 2 tries (8 points), Swinton 1 goal 2 tries (8 points). Teams . Runcorn—Harrison, back; Jones, Butterworth, Warder, and Flanagan, three-quarter backs; Jolley and J. Richardson, half backs; Walker. Moulton, Farmer, Washburn, Tomlinson, A. Richardson, Houghton, and Dobson, forwards. Swinton—Dixon, back; Gatrell, A. Valentine, Wall- work and Hampson, three-quarter backs; Molyneux and Booth, half backs; Evans, Preston, Pomfret, Cheet- ham, Yates, Cealtrup, Jones, and Barnett, forwards. Referee, Mr. W. McCutcheon, Oldham. Touch judges, Mr. A. Jones, Warrington, and Mr. A. Critchley,Widnes. SALFORD v. WIDNES.</p>
        <p>At Weaste, before a good attendanGe. In the first half Salford had all the play, Allen, Thomas, Lomas, and Tunney scoring; whilst Lally dropped a goal for Widnes. On resuming, the game again went in favour of the home team, Allen scoring, Lomas failing. Salford eventually ran out easy winners by 1 goal and 5 tries (17 points) to 1 dropped goal (2 points). WIGAN v. OLDHAM.</p>
        <p>At Cential Park, Wigan, before fully 12,000 specta- tors. Wigan pressed for the greater part of the initial half. Carpenter, for Oldham, made several attempts to get through without success, and no score was regis- tered up to the interval. On resuming, the visitors forced Wigan to defend, but Mason relieved. Fluctuat- ing play ensued, and Leytham scored on time, the kick failing. Result :—Wigan, 3 points; Oldham, nil. HULL v. HUDDERSFIELD.</p>
        <p>At Hull, before 6000 spectators. Parry scored for Hull, and Harrison failed. Muxworthy kicked a penalty goal, and Moseley scored a try, Huddersfield leading at half-time by 5 pdints to 3. Huddersfield had much the best of the game throughout the second half, and,it was only Hull's good defence which saved the situation several times. Huddersfield scored again, and the place kick was a failure. Result—Huddersfield,l goal 2 tries (8 points); Hull, 1 try (3 points). LEIGH v. HUNSLET.</p>
        <p>At Leigh, before 3,000 spectators. In the first half the Leigh forwards played grandly, and beat their opponents. Messer scored from a cross kick by Bennett, Johnson failing at goal. Hunslet attacked in the second half, but were opposed by tenacious defence. Under- wood (Highfleld) turned out for Leigh, and Hunslet played Burnell, of Otley. Result—Leigh, 1 try; Hunslet, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>SEQUEL TO A TRAP ACCIDENT. – ENGINE DRIVERS AND THEIR DUTIES.</head>
        <p>SEQUEL TO A TRAP ACCIDENT. - ENGINE DRIVERS AND THEIR DUTIES.</p>
        <p>Before His Honour Judge Bowen Rowl3nds, K.C. at the CVar7ingtort County Court on Thursday, Mr. S. E. Pardy, of Winwick Priory, claimed £lO 16s. 6d. from Messrs. Robert Boni and Company, G!azebury, for damages to a trap, alleged to be due to negligence in omitting to shut off steam and bringing a t:actiou engine to a stindstill wen requested to do so. Mr. Madden (instructed by Messrs.Arthn: Ezownand Company) apreat al for the plaintiff and Mr. Dootson, of Leigh defend3d. In opening the case, Mr. Madden snid the plaintiff was consulting agent for Lord loilford. On December Bth, he was driving into Wal:ington with his two daughters in a trap. When near the borough boundary they met a traction engine coming towards them. Mr. Pardy held up his hand for them to stop but they still came on with the enne, tire houe swetxed round, the wheel caught fie carbsione, and the left shaft was broken. Some men tried to hold the horse tad it again swerved nod broke the shalt. Mr. Pardy and one daughter and the driver were thrown out. No claim was n ride for injuries though Mr. Pardy had bean laid up for a time. The plaintiff was then called and bore out Mr. Madden'sstatement. The engine, he said, was emitting a great quantity of steam. lila driver of the engine failed to bring the.engire to a standstill when requested by Ritness and the coaohmuu.—ln cross-examination,Mr. Pardy denied that he whipped the horse when it be- came restive. _ _</p>
        <p>John Dalton coachman for Mr. Pardy, stated that when he saw the locomotive coming he put up his hand twice. The driver did not stop and the horse when opposite the engine became restive and broke the shaft. Witness was thrown out of the vehicle. The horse had passed locomotines when standing cross-examination he denied Oat anyone used a whip. He could not say what part of the engine was emitting steam.</p>
        <p>Annie Milligan, a married daughter of the plaintiff, stated that she saw steam being emitted when they were passing. She was siAinglwith her back to the coachman sand did not see him put up his hand, but she saw her father's band up. She found herself standing in a field on the side of the roadway. She was much frightened but not hurt. Somebody came up and asked her if she was hurt, and she eplied "How dare you not stop." Kate Edith Pardy also gave evidence. For the defence, Mr. Dootson contended that Mr. Pardy was not an eKpert witness in regard to a loco- motive and there was no evidence to prove that the plaintiff had allowed excePsive steam to be emitted. His Honour: Although he is not an expert he says he is in the habit of meeting locomotives on the road- way and that this one etr ;tted more steam than usual.</p>
        <p>kr. Dootson thou3ht that if the plaintiff saw the locomotive 100 yards away he should have taken some steps to avoid what possibly might be an accident. If the coachman had got out and held the horse's head, no accident would have hamened. Instead of that the whip was used with the idea of getting the horse past the engine. Robert Bond, thrashing machine proprietor. Glaze- bury, was called and said there were two men in front of the engine and one at the side, He saw the horse shie when opposite the engine and stopped the engine. There vas no steam coming from the r,.-..fety valve as he was working 401bs below safety-valve pressure. He did not sea any signal given to stop the engine. John Bond, brakesman on the engine, Patrick Flattery and John Hand also gave evidence. His Honour said the plaintiff had succeeded in estab- lishing his case. The driver of a locomotive on the road was bound to stop on signals being given, and in this case, the plaintiff, his coachman. ,and one of the ladies had all sworn that a signal was given to stop. Hebelieved their evidence, and gave judgment for the la intiff for the amount claimed with costs.</p>
        <p>A BOON TO CRICKETERS.LGeorge G. Bussey and Co., of 36 and 38, Queen Victoria-street, London, E.C., have sent us their "Cricketers' Diary and Companion for 1904." This is the thirteenth issue of a sixpenny minia- ture that can be carried in the vest pocket. It is not so large as a card case, but it contains next season's fixtures, and so much concerning last year's averages, results, and records generally that there is nothing lgft to enquire about. Whether you play or not you should possess yourself of this wonderfuisixpennyworth.</p>
