        <p>LEEDS v. HULL KINGSTON ROVERS.</p>
        <p>At Leeds, before 12,000 spectators. The Rovers had four new players from Wales in their ranks. Leeds, while defending most of the first half, scored a try through a run by Evans, and Were thus 3 points ahead at half-time. Afterwards Littlewood kicked a penalty goal,. and Evans and Grace got tries, Littlewood im- provlng one. Result—Leeds, 2 goals 3 tries (13 points) ; Rovers, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>BATLEY v. KEIGHI.EY.</head>
        <p>BATLEY v. KEIGHI.EY.</p>
        <p>At Batley. The home side started, and Keighley I attacked. Jagger charged down a kick by Goodall, and scored a good try, Walker converting. Good combina- tion on the part of Batley resulted in Davies scoring, Sutcliffe failing to convert. Walker kicked a splendid goal. On change of ends, Keighley attacked, and Walker kicked his third goal. Davies soored for Batley Sutcliffe converting. Result—Keighley, 9 points; , Batley, 8 points.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>BRADFORD v. HALIFAX.</head>
        <p>BRADFORD v. HALIFAX.</p>
        <p>I kt Park-avenue, Bradford; before 15:600 spectators. in the first half Little placed a goal for Halifax, and Mosby did the same for Bradford. In the sectend half the referee had to caution both teams. The game became tedious and slow, until Laidlaw got over with good try. Mosby failed at goal. Result—Bradford, 1 goal 1 try (5 points) ; Halifax, 1 goal (2 points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>CHILDREN'S COUGHS. Mrs. BALLIN, the great authority on Children's Diseases, strongly recommends VENO'S LIGHTNING COUGH CURE.</head>
