      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>t.TTACIC ON A SISTER AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>THE t.TTACIC ON A SISTER AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>On Monday, at Runcorn, Thomas M`Donald, aged fifteen years, appeared on remand to answer a charge of inflicting grievous bodily harm upon his sister,Ellen M`Donald,aged eleVen years, of Grindrod-square. The girl stated that her brother was making toffee. Some scones had been got for his breakfast, and he said she had been picking currants out of them, and threw a table knife at her. The knife struck her on the eye.— Detective Mottershead said the girl's eye had been completely taken away at Liverpool Infirmary. The prisoner, when charged, said his sister had been plagu- ing him, and ne ran after her and threw the knife at her.—The mother, a widow, said he was a good, hard- working lad, and the sole support of the house. The girl plagued him,and called him nicknames.—The Bench described the case as a sad one, and bou'nd the prisoner over under the First Offenders Act.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>THEFT OF A JUG.</head>
        <p>THEFT OF A JUG.</p>
        <p>_ Margaret Littler Haydock, was charged al the War- rington County Sessions, on Wednesday, with steal- ing from the Hand and Banner Hotel, Newton, a pint jug. valued at sd, the property of Mr. Wood, the land- lord, on the 28th ult. Mr. H. J. Longton, solicitor, prosecuted and said the case might seem trivial but it was brought in consequence of repeated thefts of jugs and glasses.—Defendant said she did net steal the jug. She was simply taking it out with some porter for- her husband to drink.—She was fined 5. and costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>FIRST DIVISION.</head>
        <p>FIRST DIVISION.</p>
        <p>THE SLACK CONTRACTS.</p>
        <p>INQUIRY SUB-COMMITTEE'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>Water Committee's Grave Irregularities.</p>
        <p>ANGRY COUNCIL DEBATE.</p>
        <p>At the meeting of the TOWTi Council, on Tuesday evening, the following report of the committee appointed on Ist September, 1903. "to investigate the last three slack contracts for Winwick and Delph-lane waterworks," was read :—" 29th January, 1961. Your sub-committee have pleasure in reporting that in pur- suance of the resolution of the Council of the Ist Sep- tember last they have investigated the papers placed at their disposal with reference to the contracts for the supply of slack to Winwick anti Delph-lane pumping stations, and are of opinion that for want of official control grave irregularities have been permitted, viz., Receiving fuel and accepting the seller's weights with- out taking means to have same re-weighed and checked; accepting the delivery of fuel from a firm after the tender of another firm had been accepted by the com- mittee. The reports of the tests furnished to your sub- committee by the assistant water engineer state that the deliveries of fuel from the Warrington Slate Com- pany and Messrs. Richard Evans and Company are prac- ticallyequal. While exonerating the Water Committee of having knowledge of these, your sub-committee wish to express their opinion that regulations ought to be devised sufficient to prevent any repetition of such irregularities in the future." The report is signed by Alderman Pierpoint, and Councillors Craik, Forshaw, Lord, and Pemberton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>FOOTBALL SWEEP AT A PUBLIC-HOUSE.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALL SWEEP AT A PUBLIC-HOUSE.</p>
        <p>A Fine and a Warning.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Police Court on Monday, Alfred Wood, licensee of the "Boar's Head" Inn, was sum- moned for permitting a football sweep to take place on his premises on January 15th.—Mr. Wilson, deputy town clerk, prosecuted, and Mr. Browne defended. The case, as stated by Mr. Wilson, was that Sergeant Smith and Constable Clough called at the "Boar's Head "at 8.30, on the date named, and in the taproom they found about twenty men. Defendant and his SOR were standing in the centre of the group holding up a box which contained numbered discs. A man named George Bloomfield was acting as a kind of secretary and before him was a sheet of foolscap, bear- ing ninety names with numbers opposite. Opposite some names was an additional number in red ink. Sergeant Smith pointed out that it was illegal to have a draw on licensed premises and defendant's son said "We are only having a friendly sweep-stake on the match to-morrow." Defendant said that if the sergeant would look over the matter, he would promise that it should never occur again. The men, he added, had paid only threepenee each, and there had not been anything of the kind at his house before. On Saturday morning defendant interviewed Superin- tendent Monaghan at the Police Station, and expressed his regret at what had occurred, adding that he did not know he was doing wrong. Whilst the officers were in the tap-room, defendant's son held up the box and num- bers were taken out. This class of offence, said Mr. Wilson, had been growing considerably in the town of late, and it would not be permitted by the police in future. In reply to Mr. Browne, Sergeant Smith said football sweeps were common at the works in the town. He had not heard that on Saturday one man won £lO Os. 3d. —The Chief Constable pointed out that it was no offence to hold a football sweep at a works.—For the defence, ,Mr. Browne said defendant had lived in Warrington twenty-four years and had held the licence for six years without any complaint. Defendant had no know- ledge that he was doing wrong, as was evidenced by the fact that the sweep was continued in the presence of the police. Defendant's son had carried on sweeps at a works for some considerable time and he did not know that it was illegal to do the same thing on licensed premises. Defendant did not make a penny out of the sweep, the whole of the money being handed over to the winners.—Tbe thairman poiated out that the sweep might become an inducement to people to go to the house—After considering their decision in private, the magistrates decided to impose a penalty of 10s. and costs, in view of the fact that this was the first case of its kind which had been brought before them. They had the power to fine defendant £lO and costs and if there were any similar cases they would feel aeliberty to exercise their discretion Of inflicting the full penalty.</p>
        <p>Alderman FRANCOMB in moving the adoption of the ordinary minutes of the committee, alluded to the report of the Special Committee. He said, that after a full and he hoped a very careful investigation the Special Committee had come to the conclusion that the Water Committee had been guilty of receiving fuel and accepting the seller's weight without taking means to have the same checked. As he told them some months ago he did not think the chairman of the committee could be held responsible for weighing loads of coal at Winwick. If they paid a high-class official they should have full confidence in him. The day before they had had the men from Winwick before them to see if the committee or the officials deserved this censure. The head official at Winwick, in charge of the engines, was Greenway. He said "We weigh every load of slack that ever comes into this place with these exceptions " "ah ").--" If I am down in the well they do not keep the cart waiting till I can come up again, or if their is some difficulty with the engines I do not go; or if I have a man off. As a rule every cartload is weighed." fie (Alderman Francomb) had asked: "How do you find their weight out ?" and Greenway replied: "In every gase good weight for the Corporation." He thought, considering that all carts had to go over a weighing machine before they could tip their loads, it would make people doubly careful. Alluding to the second indictment: "Accepting the delivery of fuel from one firm after the tender of another firm had been accepted," Alderman FRANCOMB said this was such stinking fish that he was ashamed it had been brought up again. Explanations had been given as fully as possible. Mr. Deas, without any sanction, actually did take the tender from his office and give it to the Slate Company. He thought they had had quite enough of that. The report of the tests furnished to the Special Committee stated that the deliveries of fuel from the two companies were practic- ally equal. That was a most difficult paragraph to under- stand. He found that it applied to the quality of the fuel. From inquiries which he himself made at Winwick he had no hesitation in saying! that Evans' Wood Pit slack was as good as any in Warrington. Councillor DoDDS said he thought the Special Com- mittee making the inquiry had treated the Water Com- mittee with consideration and courtesy. Alderman PIERPoiNT said it seemed to him that any- one who took upon himself the duty of questioning what was done by a committee became an object of derision. The carrying out of this duty was imposed by the Council as a whole. They carried it out as com- pletely and kindly as possible. He was sorry the Water Committee thought fit to call their officials before them without in proper courtesyasking some of the members who had signed that report, to be present. They knew what officials were in the presence of a committee. They would say and do anything. The TOWN OLERK : # Thank you. Alderman PIERPODTT continuing, said the Special Committee should have been present. The evidence given before the Water Committee was absolutely op- posed to the evidence given before the SpecialrCom- mittee. They could not get out of that. They had a long interview with Greenway. He declared that he did net like to open his mouth as wide as he ought to have done. The matter was discussed as plainly as possible and there was no evidence to show that any loads had been weighed at Winwick. There was no documentary evidence to prove it—no books or any record. The evidence was conclusive that the loads were not weighed, or if they were weighed they were weighed in barrows full (laughter). With regardto the preferential treatment, they knew it was stinking fish "- but was it true ? Was it true that preferential treatment was given to one particular firm against another? Alderman FRANCOMB: Perfectly true! . Alderman PIERPOINT: We have been asked to report and we have a right to report and'you should not have taken exception to our report on such grounds. We stated that thequalities were equal because you person- ally said here that tho reason that you did not take Richard Evans' and Co.'s slack was because it was not in accordance with your requirements and not equal to that supplied by the Slate Company. Here is a report from your own Water Office which says that the slacks are practically equal. Therefore Mr. Deas' statement, or that of some other official in your employ was absolutely untrue and ought never to have been made. You made it in this Chamber on his authority or that of someone else. Alderman FRANCOMB: I hadjalways stated that the slack was as good as anyone else's. Alderman PIERPOINT : You publicly stated that one of the masons was that Evans' slack was not equal to the Slate Company's slack. These are all the thanks - we get for having carried out a public duty. We could ! The best work for Free Traders to consult is the have made our report much stronger. I am not going t "Financial Reform Almanack," 1904, containing state- to be punched down by any man in Warrington. I have meat of the case for Free Trade, and an analysis of the been used to fighting all my life, and I will keep on fighting to the end. proposals for Fiscal Federation between this country and ! the Colonies. Official statistics are given of our imports Councillor PEMBERTON said the report was . and exports for the past fifty years, dis‘inguishing not as strong as he would have liked to make between the different countries. The publication of it. This was a much more serious matter than the "Financial Reform Almanack " has been delayed appeared on the face of it. There was not a bust- this year in order that it could give the Board of Trade ness man in the Council would allow his own business Returns for 1903. The figures given in regard to British to be carried on the same as the Water Committee's Trade are thus the latest that can be obtained, and are business was carried on. There had not been a load of ' a year later than those of any other annual. The section coal over the machine for a fortnight. They brought contains a very full analysis of our imports of manu- a man before his masters and asked him certain lead- factured and semi-manufactured articles,and also of the ing questions. What was the poor beggar going to do? , exports of manufacttired articles, distinguishing the It was intolerable. That man ought to have been pro- f countries to which they are sent. The article upon, tected by his committee, and he would fight for him Free Trade contains the most useful of the tables gleen tooth and nail. It appeared as if they were making a in the Board of Trade Blue Book. The almanack also frivolous report. He strongly objected to the re- - contains articles on national expenditure and taxation marks of the chairman of the Water Committee. The cost of armaments, local taxation, poll figures for five Special Committee should have been present when these General Elections, facts and figures on all chief politi- witnesses were examined. The chairman had thought cal questions. fit to get information to criticise their report without hearing their side of the question. Councillor WILKINSON said it should be borne in mind that the allegations against the Water Committee were made in September and the Special Committee had only now sent in one of the weakest re )orts he eversaw in his life. They were not the men to let anyone off. He considered he report so weak that only the paper it was printed on ;rprevented it falling to pieces. And it had taken them 5 months for the job. Councillor JAMES EVANS said that at the meeting when the allegations were first discussed it seemed as if Mr. Deas was solely responsible for a good many of the things that had gone wrong. He did not think so now. The pumping station at Winwick had existed for a con- siderable time, and he thought a set of books should I have been kept to record everything received on behalf 'of the Corporation. What good was a machine without ! a record?' Everything did not rest even on the shoulders of the Water Engineer. It was unfortunate of the Cora- mittee to summon the Winwick officials before them, for they all knew what men thought when their bread and cheese was at stake. He should be very sorry to think that the officials of this Corporation would deliberately tell lies if Put in a corner, but at the same time this was a very unpleasant and unfortunate con- dition of things. Some of the members of the Special (Committee should have been present when these officials • gave evidence. Alderman FRANCOMB: Councillor Pemberton was I present the whole time. 1 Councillor PEMBERTON ; My point was that the whole of the Committee ought to have had notice that I the Water Committee— Councillor DUNCAN : He ought to have said he was ! present himself. 1 Councillor JOHN E yam said he never heard any charge ! before of fuel going to Winwick without being weighed, , and he considered it necessary to ask these officials to report upon-their conduct. There were three officials . stationed at Winwiek who could weigh a load of coal. They were not all down the well. It appeared to him i there were more men neglecting their duty besides the i Water Engineer. The Water Committee had no busi- ness to passlightly over such a matter as this. I Councillor LORD, as one of the Special Committee ' supported all that Alderman Pierpoint had said. With I regard to the delay where had the Water Engineer been ' all the time? They wanted information from him. The MAYOR: He has been very ill. Councillor LORD said they were asked to rush the ' report in a few days before the Council meeting. The 1 weighing machine at Winwick was rusted and did not look as if it had been used for months. Hundreds of loads must have gone over it without being weighed. 1 Councillor CRAIK also supported Alderman Pierpoint.l : They were told that the slack had not been weighed. ' I No record was kept and the whole thing occurred through the Water Engineer neglecting his duties. The Engineer at Winwick had been there for some years and never , had a day's holiday. He had not seen the Water Engineer , for months. 1 Councillor' DODDS said, as they were there to consider</p>
