      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>gptorks, 15u5ittegcseg, eft.</head>
        <p>gptorks, 15u5ittegcseg, eft.</p>
        <p>TO PARTIES WANTING A SOUND BUSINESS IN LIVERPOOL.</p>
        <p>BR W N AN D C 0 . ACCOUNTANTS. TRANSFER BIM:VMS AGENTS AND VALUERS. RSTABLISHED 1875 WILL BUY, SELL, OR VALUE ANY CLASS OF BUSINESS, STOCKS. OR MACHINE4Y. WILL FIND YOU A PARTNER WITH £5 TO £50009 NO SALE, NO CHARGE. SOUTH JOHN STREET, LIVERPOOL.</p>
        <p>Parliament met on A DISPIRITED ftiesday under con- GOVERNMENT. ditions which were certainly not excit- ing. The King's Speech was not a lengthy docu- ment. It set out a very small programme of legis- lation, so small indeed that if it was dropped to- morrow it would not be missed. There were some perfunctory allusions to Somaliland and Thibet. Mr. AUSTEN CHAMBERLAIN spoke of Somaliland in the debate as if it were one of tho3e countries to which we were always sending successful expedi- tions that were never explained. What we , are doing in Thibet the Government may know, but they keep the knowledge to themselves. The inter- est of proceedings in the House of Commons was damped by the absesce of Mr. BALFOUR, whose indisposition we trust will be very brief. He left Mr. AUSTEN CHAMBERLAIN to answer Sir HENRY CAMPBELL-BANNERMAN, and the performance irresistibly reminds us of the retort of the small schoolboy to a formidable adversary, "Just you wait till my big brother comes back." Sir HENRY CAMPBELL-BANNERMA.N described the political situation very pithily when he said that it de- manded an immediate appeal to the electorate. Whether Mr. BALFOUR'S asc 11 policy and Mr. CHAMBERLAIN'S fiscal policy are two distinct policies or really one and the same, the question ought to be submitted to the country in order to clear up the general confusion. To this Iqr.A.usTEN CHAMBERLAIN replied that Mr. BALFOUR had already defined the policy of the Govern,nent which amounted to nothing more than the proposition that oar fiscal system was in need of "reform and change." Here we have a glimpse of the tactics with which the Government will meet Mr. MORLEY'S arnenelment to the Address. This has already stirred the Ministerial Unionists to a great show of anger. Mr. MORLEY will ask the House to protest against a return to Protection and the taxation of food. "How dishonest and cowardly," cries the "Daily Telegraph." " This amendments is actually framed to catch the votes of the Unionist Free Traders." Why it should be an act of dishonesty and cowardice to ask those gentlemen to vote against Mr. CHAMBERLAIN'S proposals we do not know: but the prospect that twenty Unionists may support. ,1 Mr. MoRLEv explains this outbreak of temper The Opposition might have drawn their amend- ments, says the "Daily Telegraph " with beautiful simplicity, so as not to raise this horrid question of the taxation of food at all. In that case not more than half-a-dozen Unionists would have voted ' against their party; but because Mr. MoRLEY naturally treats Mr. CHAMBERLAIN'S part in the great revolution as more important than Mr. BALFOUR'S there is all this outcry from the dis- comfited tacticians who would like the issue to he narrowed now to Mr. BALFOUR'S formula for keeping his party together. Had the recent elections gone favourably for Mr. CHAMBERLAIN we should have heard a very different note from the Ministerial journais. Now they are all appre- hension. They are afraid not only of the Opposi- tion and the Unionist Free Traders and the Irish, but also of the Unionist members who do not propose to seek re-election, and may therefore be a little slack in their attendance. There is a sinking of Ministerial hearts, all round. It is a poor outlook for the very small programme of legislation.</p>
        <p>The Warrington Liberal Association is to be congratulated upon the efforts which are being put forth to educate the constituency on the vital political question of the hour. The next Parlia- mentary election in Warrington, whether it comes soon or late, must be fought on the question of Free Trade v. Protection and it is therefore of the first importance that the electors should have a thorough grip of the subject. The friends of Free Trade stand to gain more from a free discussion of the subject than the adv.ocates of Protection. The more closely the theories of the Protectionists are examined, the more visionary and unpractical do they become. There are already significant. signs that Frei) Trade will be the winning ticket in the forthcoming electoral contest and we do not think that Warrington is disposed to cast in its lot with the reactionists. At any rate it should not be the fault of the Liberal Association if the electors are not made well acquainted with the virtues of Free Trade. Two Liberal meetings have been held in the town during the past week at which the fiscal question has been the subject under discussion, and two further meetings are to be held next week in other parts of the town when the same topic will be spoken upon.</p>
        <p>Mr. Robert Pierpoint, the member for the Borough, has already declared himself a follower of Mr. :Chamberlain's policy of prefer- ential tariffs, with its obnoxious pro- posal of a tax on food, and although on second thoughts he may think he has been too hasty in going the whole hog with Mr. Chamberlain, he has now to take the verdict of the constituency upon the position he has taken up. Mr. A. H. Crosfield, the prospective Liberal candidate, if an avowed Free. Trader, so that the issue in Warrington is clear and straight. At the Liberal meeting on Monday evening, Mr. A. W. Wilson, in the course of a well-reasoned address on the fiscal question, pointed out that the subject is essentially a bread and butter one, and it is this aspact of the question which needs to be brought home to the workingmen of War rington, who form seven-eights of the electors Upon their verdict the issue depends, and we do not think that on weighing the pros and cons of the subject they will find that their interests will be best served by Mr. Robert Pierpoint, whose support of Mr. Chamberlain's policy is more cal culated to benefit rich landlords and wealthy manufacturers at the exp3nse of the rest of the oommunity.</p>
        <p>The February meeting of the Town Council is always the one at which increases 'of salaries are considered, and a good part of Tuesday's meeting was occupied in considering the nnmerous applica- tions from Borough officials for what almost every- body colloquially calls "a rise." In every case but one the proposed increases were small and un- important, but oa/y two or three passed through without discussion. The Council was not in its most generous mood, and a good deal of the opposition came from those Councillors whom we might term—without disrespect— the small men. To them, the man who is getting £2OO a year is living in princely style, and we very much deprecate the narrowness of view which such ideas indicate. Corporation officials are men holding very responsible positions and in most cases thousands of pounds per annum pass through their; hands. It is in the interests of the town that they should be adequately and even well paid, and there is no argument affecting the merits of an official's application in the oft repeated assertion that so much a year is a nice round sum and quite enough to live on. Coun- cillor Pemberton used exactly the style of reasoning which we are alluding to. He remarked, as a point to be considered in fixing the Market Superintendent's salary, that many of the leading tradesmen would have a diffi- culty in clearing £4 ss. a week. Well ! we are sorry for the " leading tradesmen " if this is true, but " it has nothing to do with the case," in the words of the song. There has just been the same sort of agitation for economy in Blackburn where the Council has recently appointed a Town Clerk at £lOOO a year, against the recommendations of the Labour representatives that the sum should be £BOO. The considerations which affected the Coun- cil there are those which have been strongly urged by the "Blackburn Times" in the following per- tinent language: "They fait that it is absolutely essential under the circumstances that they should have the strongest and best man they can get for the money they can afford to give. We repeat that we do not want a Town Clerk who will be influ- enced against his better judgment by the opposition of small men with little experience; men of a class of which we shall have plenty of representatives in the Town Council in the future if the biggest men continue to hold themselves aloof from public life, as they seem disposed to do at present." These remarks seem to us particularly appropriate for the consumption of Warrington readers. Any. how, we believe the increases of salaries granted on Tuesday were well deserved.</p>
        <p>_— The Water Committee has had so much of its own commodity thrown on it of late that one would imagine it to be decidedly chilled. Never- theless several members contrived to get rather heated at the Council meeting on Tuesday, when the report of the Special Committee into slack contracts was presented. Though couched in unexpectedly mild language, the report found that "for want of official control grave irregularities had been permitted"—and these were enumerated. There were no very startling disclosures with the exception that the management of aflairs at Winwick pumping station is proved to have been worse even than at Warrington. The most happy-go-lucky methods seem to have obtained here. Tons upon tons of slack were taken in and used without any system of weigh— ing or even recording delivery. The weighing machine appears to have been a purely ornamen- tal article, mail rust robbed it of even that trifling raison d'etre. Naturally there was much wide- eyed astonishment—and among members of the Water Committee too—at the state of things which had obtained. Then with regard to the preferential treatment of one firm of coal mer- chants over another which was supposed to have received the contract, the Inquiry Committee broke the last leg of excuse upon which the Water Committee had endeavoured to stand. The quality of slack from these two firms did not differ very materially. "Practically equal" is the verdict, and the Water Committee thus stands condemned on all counts. It is to be hoped that we have heard the last of this slack contract scandal, for to use Alderman Fran- comb's phrase it has indeed become "stinking fish." The Water Committee has a large and un- pleasant task before it, but the Town Council and the public alike demand that the house shall Lae put in order. It appears to us that there will have to be a fairly extensive purchase of new brooms before much good can be accomplished. We are quite anxious to see whether the Committee possesses the resolution to do what is required.</p>
        <p>The Mayor showed considerable impatience with the gentlemen who had placed numerous ques- tions on the agenda paper, and he was, at times, rather hard on the questioner. We have, never- theless, considerable sympathy with His Worship in the view he holds that the proper place for these questions is in Committee. The formulating of questions to be printed in the agenda paper is quite a recent innovation, and one that there does not seem any good reason for—except perhaps in the minds of individuals who desire a little cheap notoriety. The information wanted could easily be obtained elsewhere, and we do not see that the publi3 is any better for the monthly inquisition we have had recently. Councillor Tinnion said some of the questions were " very stupid and very frivolous." They certainly took up an amount of time which might have been more advantageously utilised.</p>
        <p>There was not much excitement attendant upon this year's Brewster Sessions at Warrington, but some interesting business was transacted by the Bench. His Worship the Mayor's application for a new licensed souse in Lovely-lane was the only one of its kind, and as everybody, we should think, anticipated, it failed. This district appears to be looked upon as a happy huntinc, ground by the brewers, and if they all had the temerity of Alder- man Bolton they would doubtless all apply for licensed houses in the vicinity. "The Brooklands " was only licensed after a lot of opposition, and Mr. Rigby Swift suggested yesterday that the new house 230 yards away would create a" healthy rivalry." We can see the austere magisterial smile yet ! Nobody doubted the rivalry, but the healthiness—from the point of view of the neighbourhood—of two licensed houses striving which can sell the most intoxicants, it requires the mind of a Rigby Swift to comprehend. Not even the inducement of two off-licenses and one full license to he surrendered would prevail upon the Bench. They were not to be bought— bartered with, perhaps we should say. We have never heard a weaker case presented. The only valid reason why the licence should be granted that we could see was that his Worship the Mayor desired it, and this did not suffice. One more attempt to exploit the respectable Lovely-lane dis- trict in the interests of the trade has failed. All credit to the magistrates for their firmness.</p>
        <p>A number of interesting points were raised during the consideration of the music licences,and eventually the Bench declined to renew the licences ' for music at publichouses. This appears to us a somewhat drastic step, seeing that there was no direct evidence to prove that such licences were objectionable or against the interests of the com- munity. It must not be forgotten that, after all the publichouse is the workingman's club, and in the great majority of cases he has nowhere else to go for his evening recreation and amusement. To deprive him of his " sing-song "is an arbitrary proceeding and litcelv to inflict a serious hardship on even the most respectable workingmen. We Can only conceive a serious objection to these music licences if they tend very largely to induce men to stay longer and drink more, or lead to the introduction of undesirable characters. Adequate police supervision should, however, be sufficient to minimise these difficulties, and we hardly see that there was complete justification for the wholesale withdrawal of the licences. It is, of course, possiblo even yet for publicans to obtain fourteen day licenses, but it is easily con- ceived that no man will care to apply often under these conditions.A year's experience will soon prove whether the deprival of musical entertainment is at all seriously felt by the workers who make legitimate and reasonable use of the publichouse.</p>
        <p>It is astonishing what little support such an institution as the Ragged School obtains in the way of subscriptions, and yet nobody could deny the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <p>valuable reclamatory work done year after year by Mr. Skelland and his helpers. Only this week the Ragged School has been engaged in a most praise- worthy effort to fill the stomachs and brighten the lives of some of the poorest slum children. By the generosity of Mr. R. A. Naylor' and Miss Naylor a treat was provided this week for several hundreds of these unhappy little vagabonds. There have been many pitiable sights on previous like occasions, but that which presented itself on Wed- nesday evening surpassed everything for misery and general wretchedness. Long before the time announced for the meal, hundreds of poor children crowded round the door of the Mission, many of them hatless, shoeless, stockingless. They stood there in the pitiless rain and bitter cold, clamour- ing only for shelter and a bit of bread. Over 400 were admitted, as many as could be accommo- dated. It was a vivid picture of the extreme dis- tress prevailing in the town, the effect of which is ' so keenly felt by these r‘or young lives. The school authorities are anxious to be able to pro- vide more of these teas and entertainments, and we hope those people who have any money to spare for charitable purposes will direct their efforts to- wards increasing the scope and usefulness of the Ragged School. No donation is too small. In fact it is wonderful what a lot can be done at very -small cost in the way of entertaining the children of the poor.</p>
        <p>The Ayr, Burghs have emphasised the lesson taught by the series of by-elections which have been fought under the conditions created by Mr. Chamberlain's plunge into the Protectionist morass. The seat which was won by the Con- servatives in 1900 by a majority of 590 has been captured by the Free Traders by the adequate majortiy of 44 on a record poll. Both sides strained every nerve to poll their full strength ; and it is satisfactory that while the Conservatives, even on an increased register, only added 76 to the previous highest point, the Liberals brought up their reserves to the extent of 710. This handsome victory' for the Free Trade cause will complete the demoralisation of the Government and its supporters'. Its organs in the press are either dumb, or are reduced to taking refuge in preposterous excuses. Not the least significant feature of the Ayr Burghs victory is the Indication it affords of the coming triumph of Free Trade in Scotland. Since last July three contested elections have taken place in the northern kingdom for seats previobelly held by Conserva- tives—Argyllshire, St. Andrews Burgh, the Ayr Burghs. In each case the seat has been wrested from the Protectionists. On the very eve of the meeting of Parliament they have received the last of a succession of reeling blows such as no Ministry has ever experienced before.</p>
        <p>A case has just been decided in the metro- politan courts which deserves the attention of small house-holders who use the penny-in-the-slot gas-meters. The gas company sued several con- sumers for the payment of small sums which represented the deficiency shown by the meters on the visit of their inspector. It appeared, how- ever, that the money had been abstracted by another person, who had represented himself to be the inspector for the gas company, and who had opened the meters with a key. Under their agreement with the consumers the company were apparently entitled to recover the missing money from the consumers, who were thus compelled. to pay twice over for their gas. Such was the ruling i of the magistrate, who no doubt acted strictly n accordance with the letter of the law. The deci- sion, however, seems to act very unfairly towards the consumer. Impersonation of this kind by petty thieves is daily growing more frequent, and against a clever rogue consumers have little or no protection. The agreement under Which they are made liable is an arbitrary document, which they are bound to sign if they Wish to obtain gas tinder the slot system—a System, too, by which they pay more per 1,000 feet for gas than does the ordinary consumer. The law in this case seems to bear very hardly upon Door people, for, looked at impartially, the plain interpretation of the decision would appear to be _that the gas company, which has a monopoly and Is.safe-guarded from competition in its own dis- tricts, is entitled to penalise its customers for the thefts of rozneß against Whom it cannot p-ote !ti Itsel. The effacli of such a system n certainly k not calculated to give poor peonlq confidence in an in- vention whiph ws irnrl f.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>LOCAL NEWS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>Coming Events. Mr. Tom Boothroyd's orchestra and other artistes will give a concert in the Parr Hall this (Saturday) evening in aid of the District Nursing Association. A varied and attractive programme has been arranged and, considering the deserving object, it is hoped that the event will be a great success. The annual tea party and entertainment in connec- lion with St. Paul's Church will be held in the Parr Ha!l on Monday evening. A dance will take place in the same building on Tuesday evening. Meetings promoted by the Liberal Association to discuss the fiscal question will be held in the Co- operative Hall, Winwick-street, on Tuesday evening and in St. James's school, Latchford on Wednesday evening. Mr. Joseph Howes, of the National Reform Union will deliver an address at each 'meeting and on Wednesday Mr A. H. Crosfield will also speak. On Tuesday evening Professor Sadler, M.A., L.L.D., will deliver a lecturA on "Secondary Education" in the Co-operative Hall, Sankey-street. The annual ball in aid of the Infirmary and Dis- pensary will be held at the Lion Hotel on Thursday evening. The annual congregational tea party in connection With St. John's Presbyterian Church will be held in the schoolroom on Thursday evening. After tea a concert will be held, Councillor Dodds presiding. The annual Convention in connection with the Warrington and District Christian Endeavour Union will be held in the Ragged School, Bank-street, on Saturday next. The annual ball in connection with St. Mary's Church, Latchford, vill be held in the Parr Hall on Monday, the 15th inst. The Scarlet Goblin. - - - - -</p>
        <p>Those of our readers who are wondering which pantomime to go to should quickly make up their minds in favour of" The Scarlet Goblin," one of the most gorgeous productions ever staged at the Roya Court Theatre, Liverpool. The last Monday matineel will be given on Monday next. Other matinees Thursday aid Saturday.</p>
        <p>Overhead Railway Accident. .1-7. As Samuel Lee, flatman,of Warrington,was standing near the edge of the Custom House Station platform, on the Overhead Railway, at Liverpool,on Monday,the Projecting part of the driver's van caught him on the head, knocking him down with great force, and in- flicting a long, deep, lacerated scalp wound. First aid was rendered by the ccmpany's servants, and the injured man was then removed to the Northern Hospital. British Women's Temperance Association. The members of the above society met on Tuesday afternoon in the Church Parlour, Bold-street. There was a very good attendance, over which Mrs. Ganaett Presided. Several new members were introduced, and the meeting was made very pleasant by words of encouragement from Mrs. Garnett and Miss Bessie Dutton. During the past month the officials and friends were busy getting a petition signed which was presented to the magistrates on the 19th, and was received with evident signs of approval. The .petition is in favour of men and not girls serv- ing in bars where intoxicating drinks are sold and hopes that as the present barmaids leave the licensees Will replace them with men. The petitioners were very kindly received by the Chief Constable who had made arrangements for their visit, and who read a list of the number of barmaids employed in Warring- ton, a copy of which was read at our meeting on Tuesday. On Monday evening several of the mem hers visited the Girl's Clbu, Silvea-street. This club has a membership of 79 poor girls who meet here four .evenings in the week and does a useful work.</p>
        <p>Personal. Mr. Roger Pierpoint, son of Alderman Pierpoint, has passed his final law examination. Mr. Pierpoint was articled to Mr. Arthur Browne, solicitor, Sankey- street. _</p>
        <p>Mr. J. W. Scott, who served his articles with Mr. J. Longland, 2, Egypt-street, Warrington, also passed his final law examination on the 11th and 12th ult Mr. John Lister, who succeeds Mr. Tennent as post- master of Warrington, commenced the new duties on Monday. For the last twenty-five years Mr. Lister has been superintendent of the Central. Telegraph Office, London. UNDER the CLOCK or GOOD BOOTS.—Advt. Prize Birds.</p>
        <p>- At the great All England Cage Bird Show held at the Crystal Palace on January 29th,30th,February Ist and 2nd. Mr. J. D. Leigh, Warrington, was awarded 2nd prize with a light yellow greenfinch mule. He was also highly commended with a yellow Norwich Canary. The best birds in the country were shown in each of the clasqes.</p>
        <p>ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS MINERAL WATERS.—Advt. Scalding Accident. An accident befel Edward Greenlees, 36 years of age, residing at 5, Derby-street, whilst following his employment at the works of Messrs Rylands Bros., Limited, Church-street, on Monday morning. He was examining the economizers, about 10,30. when one of the pipes burst, scalding him severely. He was re- moned to the Ihfirmary, where he was detained.</p>
        <p>ASK for CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS MINER&amp; WATERS.—Advt. Wycliffe Junior Cricket Club. A social promoted by the members of the Wycliffe Junior Cricket Club, was held in Wycliffe Hall, on Monday evening. A capital musical programme was submitted ; games were indulged in, and refresh- ments were served. Master H. Griffiths is worthy of special mention for his rendering of a song. The young people spent a very pleasant evening.</p>
        <p>PUBLIC BENEFIT. UNDER the CLOCK for Bas BOCTS in LANCASHIRE.—Advt. Girls' Club, Silver-street. A temperance meeting for the members of this club who number 76, was conducted on Monday evening by the ladies of the British Women's Temperance Association. There was a large attendance. Addresses were given by Mrs. Simpson, Miss Green and Miss B. Dutton. Sacred duets were rendered by Mrs. Whit- more and Mrs. Moore in a most iriapressive manner. Miss Dora Fairclough, who accompanied, also sang a song which was much appreciated. At the close a large number of the members who throughout seemed deeply interested, signed the pledge. Mrs. Davies, .naatron, superintended the proceedings.</p>
        <p>H. 31. ASHTON'S LIFE TO.NIC is the KING OF TONICS.-43, SANKEY-STREET, Warrington.—Advt. Poor Children's Treat. There was another happy gathering at the Drill Hall on Saturday evening when 650 poor boys had their annual treat. An excellent tea was provided an an entertainment, which was thoroughly appreciateda followed. The programme included instrumental selections by the Misses Warren and Pooley, humour- ous items by Mr. A. Grounds, and conjuring by Mr. Foster. Lantern views were exhibited by Mr. H. Taylor. On leaving each boy received an orange and a bag of sweets. A hearty vote of thanks was accorded to the subscribers, to the Ladies' Committee, Mr. Monro for giving the tea ,and Lieutenant-Colonel Edelsten for the use of the hall. •</p>
        <p>BEST BOOTS FOR ALL.—UNDER the CiocK.—Advt. Rechabites Entertainment. A tea and entertainment arranged by the members of the Prosperity Tent of the Independent Ordsr of Rechabites for the Hope of Sankey juveniles was given in 'the Sankey Bridges Wesleyan schoolroom last Saturday. Tea was served to about 50 people at 5.30. 'Among those present were Bros. Wright and Massey. After tea an enjoyable entertainment was given, the following taking part :—Violin solos by Master J. Pardoe and Miss Dolly Lawton; pianoforte solos by the Misses F. and D. Fellows and Master R. Lea ; songs by Miss Jackson and Messrs. E. Jones and C. J. Sheath; recitations by Messrs. W. Wilkes and W. Wright; also a sketch by Messrs. E. Graham and W. Wright. At the close a new member was initiated, and on Monday evening seven juveniles joined the Hope of Sankey tent.</p>
        <p>LADIES BEST GLACE BOOTS and SHOES, UNDER.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>CLOCK. BRID GE-STREET.-At. ' The Rational Association Friendly Society.</head>
        <p>the CLOCK. BRID GE-STREET.-At. ' The Rational Association Friendly Society.</p>
        <p>The first annual supper in connec-tion with- the Latchford Branch of the above society was held at the Black Bear Inn. Over 30 persons were present. After supper there was a smoking concert, Mr. A. Lingard, secretary of the Warrington branch, presid- ing in the unavoidable absence of Dr. Leadbetter. The Chief Secretary (lir. Duncan) gave a practical address, dealing very carefully with the many good points of the society which cannot be too well known, and understood even by Rationals. Mr. Thomas Davies, the branch secretary, presented a brief report of the branch for the past year, which showed a very great increase, both numerically and financially. At the close he proposod a most comprehensive vote of thanks to all who had helped to make the gathering a success, and needless to say it was most heartily. approved. -</p>
        <p>FIRE GRATES Of every description.--J. S. GAR- - - -r</p>
        <p>LAND, Furnishing Ironmonger; 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-street,Warringion.—Advt. A Warrington Steamer in Collision. In the Admiralty Court 'on Wednesday Sir Francis Jiune and Mr. Justice Barnes, sitting as a divisional court, commenced the hearing of the case of the Zillah Shipping and carrying Company of Warrington v. the Owners of the St. Millicent, which was an ap- peal from an order of the Liverpool County Court Judge. This case arose out of a collision in the Manchester Ship Canal which occurred on January 3rd, 1903. On that date the "Sarah Brough," be- longing to the Zillah Shipping and Carrying Company was going out of the Canal at Eastham,when owing,it is alleged to the bad seamanship on the part of those in charge of the St. Millicent, belonging to Messrs. Fisher and Company, of Newcastle, a collision took place. The "Sarah Brough " was seriously damaged and on the case coming before the County Court judge at Liverpool, the Zillah Shipping and carrying Com- pany obtained substantial damages. An appeal was lodged in the Admiralty Court, and came on for hear- ing on Wednesday. The further hearing was adjourned until Tuesday next.</p>
        <p>BASSINETTES AND -MAIL CARTS in latest art</p>
        <p>shades.—J. S. G ARL AND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-street, War r ington.—Advt. Presentation to the Secretary of Cairo-street Congregation. The annual business meeting of the Cairo-street congregation was held on Monday evening. Mr. T. Birchall, J.P., presided, and at the close of the busi- ness, he intimated that a pleasant little function had now to be proceeded with. He asked Mr. F. Monks, J.P., to make a presentation to Mr. J. W. Davies, the Congregational secretary.—ln doing so, Mr. Monks referred to the valuable services which Mr. Davies had rendered during the past six years, and said it was the desire of the congregation to recognise it, in some tangible form. He had pleasure in handing Mr. Davies a silver card case :and a silver sovereign purse as a small token of the Congregation's appre- ciation of his services and of the esteem in which he was held.—The Chairman also added a few remarks and said he had known three generations of Mr. Davies's family at Cairo-street but he did not think any of them had performed such excellent service on behalf of the congregation as Mr. J. W. Davies had done.—ln accepting the gifts, Mr. Davies thanked the speakers for their kind remarks and observed that during the time he had been secretary he had eta- ' deavoured to the best of his ability to perform the duties in a satisfactory manner. If he had succeeded that was all he wished for. He sincerely thanked them for their kind presents.—The gifts were of solid silver, chastely designed, and bore the monogram of Mr. Davies.</p>
        <p>CRAWFORDS'</p>
        <p>ITSCOTCH SHORTBREAD The " Cr awf ord " Quality.</p>
        <p>HIGHLAND. Thick Cakes. LOTHIAN. Thin!Sections, sugared on top. AYRSHIRE. Thin oblong Cakes, sugared on top. Sold by Grocers and Bakers everywhere.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, February 6, 1904</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, February 6, 1904</p>
        <p>Fatal Fall from a Ladder. At 11.45 on Wednesday morning, John Evans, a windour cleaner employed by the Window Cleaning Company Friars' Green, was cleaning a bedroom wind6w at 56, Wilderspool-road. He put his foot on an iron spout which gave way beneath him, precipitat- ing him to the ground. He was picked up and it was found that his head was seriously injured, Dr. Collinf was sent for and ordered his removal to the Infirmary where he died soon afterwards from a frqc- t tired skull. The Stockton Heath Tramway._ _ _ _</p>
        <p>- The Board of Trade have sanctioned the safety arrangememts at the Swing Bridge, Stockton Heath in connection with the extension of the tramway system to Victoria-square The Tramways Committee have given instructions for tenders to be obtained forthwith for the construction of the line. The Corporation have acquired all the property required for the widening of Old London Road as far as the Mulberry Tree, and the inhabitants of Stockton Heath may look forward to the line being ready for use by midsummer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>Forthcoming Visit of Madame Clara Butt.</head>
        <p>Forthcoming Visit of Madame Clara Butt.</p>
        <p>We would -draw the special attention of our readers to the announcement in our advertising columns that Madame Clara Butt(Mrs, Kennerley Rumford), Mr. Kennerley Rumford, and a party of other distinguished i artistes will give a concert n the Parr Hall on Thurs- day, March 10th. This will be the event of the season in local musical circles and early bookings are advised. Mr. R. H. Pearson has charge of the local arrangements, and the plan of reserved seats opens at his warehouse, Bold-street, to-dab. H. M. ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC iS the AING OP TONICS.-63, SANKEY-STREET, WarringtO n.—Advt. The Danger of Paraffin Lamps..</p>
        <p>A fatality which seems to illustrate the danger of paraffin lamps has been reported to the Warrington police. Mrs. Mary Ellen Harrison (31), of 43, War- burton-street, was retiring to rest on Saturday night shortly after eleven o'clock. She was carrying a paraffin lamp upstairs when the glass fell off, and the burner, which was defective, is supposed to have caused an explosion. Mrs. Harrison's clothing was ignited, and before her husband could extinguish the flames she had sustained very severe burns about the upper portion of the body. P.C. Chrimes, who was in the neighbourhood, rendered first aid, and the unfortunate woman was conveyed to the Infirmary, where she died on Tuesday afternoon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>Market Tenants Association Soiree.</head>
        <p>Market Tenants Association Soiree.</p>
        <p>The 7th annual soiree and ball in connection with the above association was held in Parr Hall on Thursday evening and proved to be a very enjoyable function. The attendance numbered about 300 and dancing was indulged in until about 2 o'clock on Friday morning to the strains of Mr. J. T. Barton's orchestra. The Ball Committee to whom great credit is due for the excellent arrangements made consisted as follows : Councillor Arthur Peacock, Messrs. J. Pemberton, W. Hewitt, J. Halliwell, I. Bowers, A. Roberts, H. Turner W. H. Wallington, W. H. Pearce, Albert Peacock, A. T. Pemberton, with Mr. J. Atkinson as secretary. The duties of M. C's, were discharged by Messrs. William Hewitt and James Halliwell. The catering which gave every satisfaction was in the hands of Mrs. Atkinson, G-olborne street. FOR CLASS AND CHINA. of every description try J. S. GARLAND, Furnishing Ironmonger, 51, 53, and 55, Bridge-street, Warrington.—Adyt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>Concert in Bold-street Schoolroom.</head>
        <p>Concert in Bold-street Schoolroom.</p>
        <p>A successful concert was held in Bold-street Wesleyan schoolroom on Thursday evening in con- nection with the Busy Bee Sewing Institution. The proceedings were presided over by Mrs. Carnson. The programme was of a miscellaneous description and was greatly appreciated. Miss Amy Pigot was very successful as an elocutionist and her recitations were a feature of the evening's entertainment. Mr. J. G. Osborne made a very promising debut as a soloist and was warmly applauded for his fine rendering of "The Skipper" and "The Bedouin love , song," both of which were well suited to his voice. Songs were also efficiently sung by Miss Spruce, Miss Wilson, Miss Kershaw,and Miss and Master Spruce were heard together in a duet. The instrumental items included a pianoforte duet by Miss M. Weaver and Miss Francis Parkinson, a piano- forte solo by Miss Osborne, and an instrumental trio by the Misses Osborne and Ward. Recitations were also given by Miss Bessie Plinston and there were action songs by members of the Busy Bee Society. A pleasant evening's entertainment concluded with the usual vote of thanks.</p>
        <p>St. John's Literary and Social Society. On Tuesday evening at. the weekly meeting of this Society, Mr. Chas. Lyon, of the " Liverpool Daily Post," and "Echo," gave an intereAing address on the production of daily newspapers. Mr. Lyon said the present was an opportune time for the discussion of such a subject ; great progress was being made in newspaper methods and those that were new a decade ago were already old-fashioned. Warrington was also a fitting place for such discussion when they recollected that Caxton set up his printing press in the town and in later times the famous Dr. hiackie was con- nected with Warrington. Referring to the early days of newspapers the lecturer read extracts from the "Aberdeen Journal "of 1783, which he said con- tained no sporting news or advertisements, nor any stock exchange or gambling notices, but on the other hand did contain graphic descriptions of public execu- tions and advertisements of state lotteries. Mr. Lyon explained and illustrated, in a highly instructive and interesting manner, the various means of collecting news, and the operations of type-setting, stereotyping and printing, showing how the very latest news is obtained and supplied to the public by means of stop- press editions of the evening papers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0064" n="art0064">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>ALLEGED THEFT OF A PURSE AT GOLHORSE.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Occasional Court on Monday, before Mr. Thomas Halton, a St. Helens man named Thomas Callaghan was charged with stealing a pure containinglss. and a number of pawn tickets, the pro- perty of Charles Fryer. Legh-street, Go/borne, with whom he had been lodging for several weeks.---From the evidence it appeared that prosecutor's wife and Callaghan's wife went out on Saturday night leaving Callaghan in the house. Subsequently Mrs. Fryer missed her purse from the house and informed the police, uallaghan being arrested.—He was remanded for a week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>YOUTHFUL SHOPBREAKRRS,</head>
        <p>YOUTHFUL SHOPBREAKRRS,</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County- Petty Sessions on Wed- nesday, William Taylor and Richard Trigg, ten years of age, residing in Cherry-street,Warlestown, were charged with breaking and entering the shop of the Bankruptcy Clothing Company, Market-street, Earlestown, and stealing an overcoat and two whistles of the value of us. 3d.—They pleaded guilty.—The ovidence showed that prisoners had entered the premises by removing a pane of glass from a back window. Trigg afterwards gave the overcaat to a boy darned Carmody, stating that he had walked to St. Helens, and that a woman who was leaving a tramcar dropped the coat from a parcel. Taylor, however, said they had taken it from the prosecutor's shop.—Taylor now stated that Trigg entered the shop whilst he Taylor) stood outside.— Trigg admitted breaking the window.—The magistrates ordered the boys to receive six strokes with the birch.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>ANOTHER CHARGE OF SHOPBREAKING.</head>
        <p>ANOTHER CHARGE OF SHOPBREAKING.</p>
        <p>Martin Moran, of no fixed abode, was charged at the Warrington County Petty Sessions on Wednesday, with breaking and entering the pawnshop 31, Market-street, Earlestown, on January 14, at about one o'clock in the morning, and stealing therefrom thirteen watches, one set of gold studs, and three dress rings,valued £4 7s. 6d, the propGrty of:James Callender.—An assistant said he left the shop secure at 8 o'clock on January 13th and at 2 a.m., on January 14th he was called up by the police. He found that the shop window had been broken and the articles named in the charge were missing.— Prisoner was remanded to Newton Petty Sessions to be held on Thursday next.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>A DROWNING MYSTERY.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday" Te-f;o-iii-of Anne Butterworth, aged twenty-four years, daughterof Thomas Butterworth, of Bold-street, Runcorn, who had been missing si nee December 29 last, was taken from the Weston Canal. The deceased was a domestic servant employed at Liver- pool. DAMAGING ASTON ESTATE.</p>
        <p>William Antrobus, a young man, was summoned to Runcorn Police Court on Monday charged with cutting fifty hazel sticks growing in Bird's Wood, Aston, the property of Mr. A. H. Talbot, J.P., of Aston Hall. Mr. H. N. Linaker, who prosecuted, said much damage was done in this manner. Defendant was fined 20s. and costs (Bs. 6d.), or a month's hard labour.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN GAS COMPANY. The report of this company, declaring a dividend o f 10 per cent., was adopted at Monday's annual meeting. SENT FOR TRIAL FoR STEALING A CHOP. A woman named Riley vras,at Widnes,on Mon- day, sent for trial 1:1,211e next Liverpool Assizes, for stealing a chop, value flvepence, from the shop of Robert Taylor, Farnworth-street, Farnworth, Widnes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>LICENSING SESSIONS.</head>
        <p>THE LICENSING SESSIONS.</p>
        <p>PROPOSED NEW HOUSE IN LOVELY-</p>
        <p>LANE.</p>
        <p>The Application Refused.</p>
        <p>NO SPIRIT LICENCE FOR THE "GREEN MAN."</p>
        <p>Music in Public Houses—Drastic Step by the Bench.</p>
        <p>The annual Licensing Sessions held at the Borough Police Court yesterday were the more notable on account of the drastic measures taken by the Licensing Committee in respect of music licences for public- houses. An inspection of the licensed premises by the committee had previously, been made, and in consequence the holders of music licences received notice to attend the Sessions. The Court room was filled with members of " the trade," but the attendance of the general .public was not quite as large as usual. The magistrates oh the Bench in- cluded the following members of the Licensing Com- mittee :--Mr. F. Monks (chairman), Mr. C. White, Mr. T. Birchall, Mr. J. Geddes, Mr, H. Roberts, and Dr. Fox. The other justices present were:—Aldermen Francomb, Hutchinson, and Smethurst, Councillors Roberts and Thornton, and Messrs. E. J. Guest, R. T. Fairclough, F. W. Monks, J. W. Hayward, and S. Wright.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>CHIEF CONSTABLE'S REPORT.</head>
        <p>CHIEF CONSTABLE'S REPORT.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable's report,, dated January 31st was as follows :—I have the honour to present to you my eighth annual report relative to the business of the Brewster Sessions, together with certain figures and information connected with the same, for the year ended December 31st, 1902. The population of the borough according to the last census was 64,242. The present estimated population is 67,000. There are in the Borough 86 alehouses, 37 beerhouses, and 83 off-licensed houses (including wine and spirit licences), making the total 206, a proportion of one licence to 311•36 inhabi- tants. In Table 3of this report are shown the corres- ponding figures relating to 15 other important Northern towns, the average of which is 1 licence to 301*(15 inhabi- tants. There are ten clubs registered in pursuance of the Licensing Act of 1902. During 1903 the magis- trates have sanctioned the transfer of the licences of 14 ale-houses, 5 beerhouses, and 9 out-door houses, total 28 as (against 33 in 1902). The charges of drunkenness in Warrington during 1903 amounted to 308. The previous year they numbered 339, showing a decrease of 31 during 1903. The average of "Drunken Charges" in Warrington for the past de- cade is 32240. For the year 1903 the average number of "Drunken Charges" in 15 large Northern towns is 6.96 per 1,000 inhabitants. The corresponding average in Warrington is 419 per thousand. The 308 persons dealt with for drunkenness in Warrington during 1903 were classified and disposed of as follows :—There were 2'32 males and 76 females; of these 292 were convicted and 16 discharged; 219 . were local residents, and 89 were strangers. Two persons were each convicted 5 times, 1 person 4 times, 5 persons 3 times, and 10 per- sons twice each, so that 49 of the charges represent only 18 individuals. On January 26th, 1903, the Ale- house Licence of the Green Dragon, Bridge-street, was transferred to the Brooklands Hotel, Lovely-lane, and in consideration of this, the licences of the Ropemakers' Arms, beerhouse, Crossley-street, and the Sceptre Inn, Winwick-street, were allowed (by their owners) to lapse without renewal. The Green Dragon premises have been demolished. CONDUCT OF LICENSED HOUSES.</p>
        <p>During the year two alehouse keepers have been prosecuted for permitting drunkenness on their licensed premises. One was fined, and the 2nd was ordered to pay costs.</p>
        <p>At the date of this report, a summon; IS pending against one alehouse keeper for permitting gambling on his licensed premises. Nearly 800 visits of supervision during the year, have been made to licensed houses by the chief officers of the Force, and their reports, gener- ally speaking, have been of a satisfactory zharaster. I have, moreover, personally supervised these premises, and visited them as often as possible, with results, in the main, confirmatory of the reports submitted by my officers. On 12 occasions it was Recessary to formally caution licensees for irregularities which, while not actually legal offences, were likely to conduce to in- fringement of the Licensing Laws. A record of such official cautions, as in former years, has been carefully kept for reference, and I have seldom or never had to caution the same individual twice. The irregularities referred to were such as playing dominoes, rings, darts &amp;c., where actual gaming could not be proved; or piano- playing and singing too frequently heard on premises not licensed for music. The police, moreover, as far as possible, assist licensees by calling attention to drunken persons seen entering licensed premises, or prostitutes found resorting thereto. SPECIAL REPORT ON MUSIC LICENCES.</p>
        <p>I have the honour to submit the following report for your information. It is abridged from the detailed statement read at a former meeting of your Committee. There are 40 places within the Borough licensed for music, &amp;c., under the Public Health Acts Amendment Act, 1890, Part IV.. viz. :-21 alehouses licensed for music and singing, and one alehouse (the Lion Hotel) for music and dancing ; total, 22 alehouses. In addi- tion, 6 beerhouses are licensed for music and singing, making the number of places which are licensed for the sale of intoxicants, licensed also for music, 28. Besides this there are (unconnected with liquor-licensed pre- mises) 12 large rooms licensed for music and dancing, such as the Parr Hall, tke Circus in Queen-street, the two Co-operative Halls, the Gymnasium, and several schools and club-rooms. ALEHOUSES AND BEERHOUSES WITH MUSIC</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>LICENCE.</head>
        <p>LICENCE.</p>
        <p>I have recently caused measurements to be taken of the music rooms in alehouses and beerhouses, and par- ticulars to be compiled explaining the manner in which the various music licences are used, and the occasions upon which the holders have music, &amp;c., upon their several premises. Summarized the return is as follows: —Average dintensions of music rooms: Length, 21ft. Gin. ; width, 14ft. Bin. ; height, Bft. 101 in. Eight rooms are below the average length, 8 below the average width, and 5 below the average height,. The largest room ("King and Queen." Padgate-lane) is 43ft. long, 15ft. 6in. wide, and 10ft, in height. The smallest room ("Lower Seven Stars," Bridge-street) in 10ft. long, 9ft. 6in, wide, and 7ft. high: Of the total 28, 17 houses (13 alehouses and 4 beerhouses) are licensed for music, &amp;c., over their entire respective areas. Ten houses (8 alehouses and 2 beerhouses) are each licensed for one room only, and 1 alehouse ("King's Arms," Buttermarket-street) is licensed for two rooms, one of which, a detached club-room at the rear, is not used, being an a dilapidated condition. The average measure-, ments, given above, refer only to the houses where some particular room or rooms are licensed for music. They do not, however, include the Lion Hotel, as it is the only alehouse licensed for music and dancing, and so differs from the rest. It is provided in the Public Health Acts Amendment Act, that any house or room licensed for music, &amp;c., shall have displayed over its entrance the incription : "Licensed in pursuance of Act of Parliament for music, &amp;c." In 15 alehouses and 4 beerhouses the police found this condition observed, but 7 alehouses and 2 beerhouses had not the required signs displayed. Except in a few cases, where the houses are restricted by the special terms of particular licences, the tenants of all these 28 liquor-licensed houses might (at present) legally hold musical entertainmentedaily and nightly during their legally fixed business hours, except on Sundays, mood Fridays,. and Christmas Days. In many of the houses paid pianists are employed either regularly or occasionally. In one or two houses the waiters sing; songs for public entertainment, and receive extra re- muneration for this.</p>
        <p>APPLICATION FOR A NEW MUSIC LICENCEI</p>
        <p>FOR AN ALEHOUSE.</p>
        <p>T have received notice in due form that John Caldwell Rigby, of the Queen's Hotel, Sankey-street (an alehouse owned by the Warrington Corporation), intends at the forthcoming Licensing Sessions to apply for a Music Licence for his premises. On behalf of the police 'res- pectfully submit that there is no necessity for such a licence at this house. INEBRIATES' ACT.</p>
        <p>With reference to the Inebriates' Act of 1898 I have once more to report, with regret, that there is yet no local provision for the accommodation of persons who might be committed from this Court under the powers of the statute.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>CENTRAL STATION REFRESHMENT ROOM.</head>
        <p>CENTRAL STATION REFRESHMENT ROOM.</p>
        <p>On the application of Mr.Browne,temporary authority was granted to Mr. Clegg Thomas, secretary of the Cheshire Lines Commttee in respect of Central Station refreshment room. The licence was formerly held by th 3 late Mr. David Maldrum, manager.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>ROYAL COURT HOTEL.</head>
        <p>THE ROYAL COURT HOTEL.</p>
        <p>Mr. Browne said the Bench would. remember •that there was an opening leading from Dr. Ferguson's yard to the brew house attached to the Royal Court Hotel. It was proposed to block up the passage and open a new roadway five feet six inches in width from Rylands street to the brew house. It would be used merely for the purpose of carting hops, malt, coal and other mat- erials to the house and would not be used at all by customers of the hotel. The Chief Constable said the new roadway would be an important improvement from his point of view.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>"BRICKMAKER'S ARMS."</head>
        <p>THE "BRICKMAKER'S ARMS."</p>
        <p>Mr. Browne said that at the last transfer sessions but one, plans were passed for the rebailding of the "Brick- maker's Arms," School-brow. Messrs. Greenall, Whitley and Company proceeded to buy the adjoining property, but they received a notice from the Corp Oration of the new building line which they had adopted and which would require the company to set back the proposed</p>
        <p>itew premises 24 feet. This made it absolutely impossible for them to carry out the plans and -the company had therefore acquired an adjoin- ing plot of land. They asked the Bench to renew the, licence for the old house until the new house had beet, built. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. William Owen submitted the plans for the new house. He pointed out that there would be no club room as originally intended and the house would there- fore be a two storey instead of a three storey building. In reply to Councillor Thornton, Mr. Browne said the new site was nearer Hopwood-street than the old one.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>DECISIONS.</head>
        <p>DECISIONS.</p>
        <p>The magistrates retired and after consultation the Chairman announced that all the ale-house licenoes and off-licences would be renewed. The beerhouse licences were renewed with the exception of the "Star and Garter." The alterations at the Royal Court Hotel and the "Brickmaker's Arms" were agreed to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>"GREEN MAN" BEERHOUSE. A HARDY ANNUAL.</head>
        <p>THE "GREEN MAN" BEERHOUSE. A HARDY ANNUAL.</p>
        <p>Mr. Arthur Browne applied on behalf of Benjamin Kenyon,for a full alehouse licence for the" Green Man." It was unnecessary, he said, to tell the magistrates that this was an old application and one that had been made for many years. He thought it was one of those appli- cations that did not lose anything by its continued repetition, because as time went on it showed that the wants of the neighbourhood required„ and would justify the Bench in granting, a full licence. It was not a tied house, and Mr. Kenyon had many times been asked for spirits by customers. He thought if anybody were entitled to a full licence in that district, he was. The Chairman: You have nothing new to add. Mr. Browne: Except that a lot of new houses have been built there.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable: The figures are precisely the same as to inhabitants and houses as last year. Mr. Kenyon was then called and said he was asked almost every day for spirits. When he made the appli- cation last year it was supported by a petition signed by 1,000 inhabitants in the district. Mr. Browne intimated that he could call witnesses, but he did not think it was necessary.</p>
        <p>The magistrates reserved u",,;ii—degision. THE MAYOR'S PROPOSED NEW HOUSE. A BAIT TO THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>Mr. Rigby Swift, barrister 'instructed by Mr. Long. land, solicitor, then applied on behalf of Mr. Richard Sanson for a. provisional grant of a licence for a public-house which was to be erected at the corner of Delamere-street and Lovely-lane. The applicant, he said, was a joiner living in Hall-street, and had for many years past been the holder of an off-licence. He was a married man in the prime of life and in every way suitable to be the recipi- ent'of a licence. The house which was proposed to be built would cost between £2OOO and £3OOO. It would be built on land belonging to Mr. William Bolton. It would be in every way a comfortable house, intended for the purpose of carrying on a bona-fide public- house business. There was no pretence that they were going to raise a first-class hotel. Speaking of the needs of the neighbourhood Mr. Swift said it was a comparatively new part of the town and had been developed in recent years and was still being developed with considerable rapidity. There were now withinan area of 400 yards of the house some- thing like 1434 houses and of these 20 had been built since the last annual licensing meeting. The whole of the houses represented a population of 7,500 people of whom a considerable number had come to live in the neighbourhood since the last application was made. New works had recently been opened and naturally they brought more people into the vicinity. In the area of 400 yards there was only one fully-licensed house—the Brooklands which was 238 yards away from the pro- posed new house. In the other direction there was the Green Man beerhouse which was 367 yards away. There were also two off-licensed premises—Throssell's and Potters—and the other public houses were the Bowling Green and the Ship, both of which were considerably over a quarter of a mile away. There were therefore only four licences for a population of 7,500 whereas in Warrington generally (and Warrington certainly had no cause to be ashamed of its position, as it appeared from the comparative table prepared by the Chief Con- stable thatWarrington was not very high on the list, the proportion of population to each licence was 311.36. _ _ _</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable at this stage, gave his figures as to distances and population. He also pointed out that the Ship Inn was 537 yards away, the Railway Inn, 550 yards, the Railway Tavern 730 yards and there were also 7 off-licences within a radius of 730 yards.</p>
        <p>Mr. Swift: Within a radius of 400 yards there are only four licences of any kind whatever. Continuing, he said a little time ago the magistrates granted a licence for the Brooklands hotel and he ventured to think that they recognised the necessity of a licence being put in this new part of the town. He thought it was quite clear that the new hotel had not by an means exhaust- ed the necessities of the neighbourhood. An element of healthy rivalry would be created by the opening of an- other house and that would be no disadvantage to the public or the police. If necessary, he could call many respectable residents who would tell the magistrates that the present facilities were not sufficient for them. The Chairman: Do you propose to give up any licences?</p>
        <p>Mr. Swift : Tam coming te that now. Continuing he said he knew the magistrates would endeavour to sc, distribute the houses throughout their district that there would not be a predominance in one part and an inadequate supply in another. If the Bench granted the licence and it was confirmed Mr. Bolton proposed to give up an off-licence at the corner of Priestley-street and Cartwright-street. An off-licence, it seemed to him was a thing which might not improperly be given up in Warrington, because there was a very consider- able proportion of them in the borough- -83 in comparison with 86 alehouse licences In addition Mr. Bolton proposed not to apply again for a renewal of the full licence of the "Exchange" Inn, Golden squareand the effect would be that the licence would be transferred from Golden-square, where licences were numerous, to Lovely-lane. By granting the application the Bench would be doing more to mitigate any evil there might be in the drink traffic without adopting what some people said was an injus- tice—the taking away of a licence without granting any adequate compensation. The Chairman inquired if it was not proposed to give nit the off-licence in Hall-street. Mr. Swift (after consulting with Mr. Longland) : If the Bench have any feeling about the licence in Hall- street—that we are not offering enough without it—we will give it up, bat it was not part of the original application. Mr. Season gave formal evidence, but the magistrates decided not to hear any witnesses as to the needs of the district. The decision was reserved. MUSIC IN PUBLIC-HOUSES. LICENCES REFUSED.</p>
        <p>The magistrates then proceeded to consider the renewal of music licences. 3tr. Browne said the Bench had caused notices to be sent out to the holders of music licences to attend the sessions and he had been instructed to appear on their behalf. He pointed out that some years ago some of the applications were made simply for protection. Under the old regimb it was thought by the late Chief Con- stable that any .person having an instrument of music in a downstairs room ought to have a licence. In those days applications were made solely with the idea Of protection. even though the instrument was only used on one night per week -or on an odd occasion. He con- tended that such use of a piano did not require a licence within the meaning of the act. Licences were not required for music when the rooms were occasionally used for the holding of suppers, &amp;c. The Chief Constable said he should certainly not put ' a severe construction unon the meaning of the word occasionally. Even if the music licences were taken away and gatherings were only held once a month or six weeks he did not suppose the police would take any noticeof it. In a Case where there was no music licence and music was regularly carried on, they would cer- tainly bring it under the notice of the Bench. Mr. Browne: I quite agree with that.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constahle said. a npnbar of music licence- holders had given notice that they did not intend to apply for a renewal.</p>
        <p>AN ABSENTEE.</p>
        <p>Thename of Alfred Owen, Smiths' Arms, Legh-street, was called but there was no response, and it was inti- mated that a renewal was not being applied for.</p>
        <p>WOULD REFUSALS BE TO THE TOWN'S DISADVANTAGE'?</p>
        <p>Mr. Browne: I have been told this morning that music licences were taken away at Ashton-unde7-Lyne and it was found that it worked to the disadvantage of the town. The magistrates. subsegusatly grantedlicences for one night a week. The Chief Constable: If it does the same here I certainly don't obiect to your worships adopting the same course; but I don't think it will.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>WHAT IS A PROFESSIONAL?</head>
        <p>WHAT IS A PROFESSIONAL?</p>
        <p>Peter White, of the Old Ball Inn, applied for a re- newal of his music licence, stating that the piano was only used on Saturday nights. The Chiefronstable :Do you employ any professional? Applicant: One man conies to sing on Siturday night, and I give him a shilling or two. The Chief Constable: Have you not told the police that you pay a pianist so much a week? Applicant: Yes, I do. The Chief Constable read the fourth condition of the music licence, which was that no professional artistes should be engaged.</p>
        <p>Anplicant said the men followedordinary occupations during the day. Frank Berry, Glassmaker's Arms; Charles Cook. Gardener's Arms; Robert Eyes, Gladstone Arms; and Robert Anderton, Cross Keys, attended to apply for the renewals and were not questioned by the Chief Constable. Thomas Chantler, of the Ade!phi Vaults, said he only had music occasionally. - -</p>
        <p>On the advice of Mr. Browne he withdrew his application. No one attended to ask for a renewal of the "Borough Arms" licence, aiid Mary Travis, of the "Volunteer" Inn, stated that she did not require a licence. A BLIND PIANIST.</p>
        <p>Elizabeth Plinston applied for a-renewal of the musie licence for the King's Arms," Buttermarket- street.</p>
        <p>Mr.. Browne said Mrs. Plinston had paid a blind man to play the piano during the past six years. In answer to the Chief Constable, Mrs. Plinston said she paid•the pianist £1 a week and a vocalist 75.16 d. James Gorse, aged 27 years, who said he had been blind from birth, informed the Bench that if he were not allowed to play at the" King's Arms," his means of livelihood would be gone.</p>
        <p>THE VALUE OF A MUSIC LICENCE: Robert Perdue, of the" Higher Angel," Buttermarket street, said the man who supplied the music at his house worked at Messrs. Rylands' during the day time. In answer to the Chief Constable, applicant said his living almost depended upon the music licence. Super- intendent Monaghan had recently advised him not to admit certain persons to his house, but he denied that he ever admitted them</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>_THE QUESTION OF " ARTISTES " AGAIN.</head>
        <p>_THE QUESTION OF " ARTISTES " AGAIN.</p>
        <p>_ Thomas Walsh, of the "Crown and Sceptre" ad- mitted that he paid a pianist and two vocalists but pointed out that there were different interpretations of the word "artiste."</p>
        <p>Mr. Browne said the applicant was under the im- pression that artistes were persons specially "got up" for singing and dancing. He did not think the clause in the licence covered merely paying a man for singing or playing. The Chief Constable: He thought it only covered the ballet girls who wanted covering. (Laughter.) Mr. Talbot added that he did not suggest that the music room was not as well conducted as it could be.</p>
        <p>Thomas Cooper, of the Blue Bell Inn, next applied and said he only had music one night a week and did not pay anyone. George Lake, of the Corporation Inn, and Thomas Malkin, of the Royal Exchange, did not apply for re- newals.</p>
        <p>"THE HOUSE WOULD BE NO .USE TO HIM." George Lang, of the" Rose and Crown," said he paid a pianist 10s. for three nights' services per week, and also paid a waiter who sang occasionally. His house would be no use to him if he had not a singing room.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>JUSTIFIABLE DISTINCTION.</head>
        <p>A JUSTIFIABLE DISTINCTION.</p>
        <p>John McClinton, of the Royal Oak, said he paid a pianist but he like the other applicants did not con- sider that he employed an artiste. The Cl-ief Constable: I think the distinction in most cases is justifiable. (Laughter.) Mr. H. Roberts: He pays a pianist who is not an artiste. (Laughter.) The Chief Constable: I think we all understand that. William Brown, of the "Lower Seven Stars," with- drew his application.</p>
        <p>Peter Clirimes, of the "Roebuck V Inn, applied for a renewal.</p>
        <p>IS AN AUTOMATIC PIANO AN ARTISTE?</p>
        <p>Harriet Williamson, of the "GOlden Lion," Knutsford- road, stated that she did not pay anyone to provide music.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable: There is a piano that plays when you put f!, penny in the slot.</p>
        <p>Applicant said that was all the music they had. Mr. Browne: Is it an artiste? (Laughter.) The Chief Constable: In comparison with most of the other instrumental performances it may be called music in the highest sense. (Laughter.) The fact of the piano being automatic does not make the slightest difference if the music is there habitually. John Davies, of the Baths Hotel, and Joseph E.Shaw, of the "Red Lion," did not apply for renewals. Samuel Wakefield, of the Central Hotel, applied through Mr. Browne for a renewal. Frederick Clare, of the '• King and Queen "withdrew his application. THE DECISIONS.</p>
        <p>Afterthe Bench had consulted in private the Chair- man said the applications of Messrs. Kenyon and San- son were refused. The music licences for the follow- ing places would be renewed :—Patten Arms Hotel, Lion Hotel, St. James's School, Tofts' Assembly Room, Conservative Club Assembly Room, Co-operative Halls, Winmarleigh Oafs, Parish Room Latchford, Masonic Hall, Gymnasium, Parr Hall, and Workingmen's Club, Market place. All the other applications were refused. If any licensee wished to have a special music licence he could make application, and the magistrates could grant one for 14 days. They thought it , better for applications to come in that form than to renew the licences for twelve months. The Chief Constable said a licence for 14 days could be applied for any morning when the Court sat. This concluded the business of the Sessions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>" GRIMALDI."</head>
        <p>" GRIMALDI."</p>
        <p>St. Mary's Amateur Dramatic Society's</p>
        <p>Performances.</p>
        <p>Warrington has had its full share of amateur drama tic performances this season and the members of the St. Mary's Society fittingly concluded the series on Mon- day, Tuesday and Wednesday by a revival of Dion Boucicault's drama "grimaldi" which did them infinite credit. One's pleasure ir congratulating the enthusias- tic members of this comparatively youthful organiza- tion is enhanced when it is known that their efforts are always put forth, and willingly tco, on behalf of deserving causes. It was at the request of the Board if Management of the Infirmary that the performances of " Grimaldi" were arranged, and the public were afforded the dual privilege of wit- nessingan admirable representation of art enthralling play, and ajding an institution waich has powerful claims upon all classes of the community. Although on Monday the attendance was not very large, there was a marked imnrovement on the two succeeding evenings, and it is hoped that from the finalcial point of view the expectations of the promoters will be realized. "Excelsior" would be a suitable motto for St. Mary's amateurs, for they always aim high. In " however they, found a play eminently suited to the talent at thei disposal. The leading characters had the advantage of a long-standing acquaintance with their parts, for the society produced the drama some years ago in aid of the Indian famine fund. The striking success then achieved justified the repetition, and the members looked forward to it with confidence. The dranuttis persoetae was as follows:— The Duke of D— Mr. John New as Grimaldi. an old actor Mr. W. Kilduff Lord Shafton • M. Chas. J. Newton Maltravers Brother Officers Mr. Samuel Ryan Selwyn Dawdle J Mr. Walter Warren Wopshot Mr. George Newton Leech Mr. James Gatlev Hopkins Mr. Alban Kilduff Hastings Mr. .1. Cross Mortimer Mr. C. Fagan Waiter Mr. James McKay Call Boy Master Wilfred Hardiman Nressenger Boy Master James Dolan Violet Miss Nora Fleming (Griinaldi's adOpt?.d daughter) Julia. an actress Miss Fanny Hayes The Countess of Beanmaris.. Miss Delia Gleeson (Mother to Lord Arthur) Lady Belle Lettres - Miss A. Warren</p>
        <p>Miss Stingwell - LaW-tO. Miss Highlev Proper Mrs. Hopkins At the first glance, the cast seem,: a heavy one, 1311 t some of the characters had comparatively little to do. The audience foilowed with interest the story of the oh equered career of the old actor and his adopted daughter who is destined to a brilliant success on the stage. There are the inevitable complications and tragic incidents, but despite the machinations of the villainous and crafty Maltravers all ends !happily in the marriage of Violet to Lord Shafton Mr. W. Kimuff was thoroughly at home in the exact- ing title pile. His acting was marked by peculiar power and feeling and won general commend- ation. He was seen at his best during the period of susnense behind the scenes at Nottingham I theatre when Violet made her debut and also when the I play reached its climax in the denunciation and enforced confession of Maltravers at the Manor House. Miss Norah Fleming, now of the Brooklands Amateur Thespian Society, gained high honours as Violet. She realized to the full the possibilities of the part, and in- fused pathos and emotion into her portrayal. She further added to the pleasure ofthe audience by several sweetly rendered songs, and the duets with Mr. Kilduff were an enjoyable feature. Mr. Chas. J. Newton, as Lord Shafton, contributed much to the success of the play. He hada difficult part, but he played it well. He acquitted himself with special credit in the touching scene in Grimaldi's lodgings whenjoe declared his inten- tion of departing for the Soudan, so as to save the old man the pain of parting with his adopted daughter. The villain of the piece is Maltravers and the representation was inthe capable hands of Mr. Samuel Ryan, who fully maintained his reputation. He dis- played his abilities to particular advantage in the weird Manor House scene. Miss Fanny Hayes was well- placed as Julia, her saorn for her popular rival knowing no bounds until the last scene, when the honour of the professien has to be defended. Miss Delia Gleeson comported herself with fitting dignity as the Countess of Beaumaris and Mr. J. Newns was a courtly Duke of D-Tt must not be supposed that the play is entirely made up of tragedy and excitement. There are flashes of humour, and for them the audience was indebted to Mr. George Newton as Wopshot and Mr. Walter Warren as Selwyn Dawdle. The scene in which Wopshot found himself in the unfortunate posi- tion of "making love for another" was extremely amusing. Mr. Warren made a great hit as the officer who was always willing to applaud any expressions as being "just what I was going to say." The numer- ous minor pads were taken in good style. In the best regulated societies little hitches will occur, and there was a ripple of merriment on Wednesday when Mal- fraying made a futile effort to fire the pistol and wound Lord Shafton. There were ominous clickings of the trigger but no shooting, and the villian had to abandon his wicked design. Each evening the orchestra of the Warrington enter- taining Society gave selections, Mr. W. H. Kendrick conducting. The dnties behind the scenes were well discharged. Mr. E. Rimmer was stage manager and Mr. G. Coggius ably assisted him Mr, Harry Boyle manipulated the limelight.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>4 Rugby Football. By " Ovalist."</p>
        <p>Warrington seem to have struck a bad patch, in the words of the Yankee prospector. For the second time this season they have been beaten twice in succession, and appearances point to the likelihood of the record being raised to three consecutive defeats by Saturday night. There is no glossing over the fact that the form of the wirepullers has sadly deteriorated. A fortnight ago we were all discussing the possibility of their winning the championship. Now we shall be well satisfied if they finish in the first half dozen. Such are the kaleidoscopic changes in League football. lieighley and Broughton Rangers have upset War- rington's little apple-cart with a vengeance,and unless very strong measures are taken this week-end liunslet will knock another wheel off.</p>
        <p>To give Warrington credit they generally rise to the occasion on their own ground, but they have conspicuously failed on three occasions this season. viz: against Hunslet, Oldham, and Broughton Rangers. In other seasons, when they have had an inferior record, they generally came out on top in these games. There has been an awkward unexpected- ness about Warrington's failures this season, and their inability to secure safe points at home has been their undoing. On Saturday they succumbed to Broughton Rangers by two tries to one, though they had pre- viously won at Wheater's Field, and the local disap- pointment was keen. Broughton deserve full credit for their win, but it was Warrington's shockingly bad play which lost them the match.</p>
        <p>The largest gate of the season assembled, and a round 150 was taken at the gates. A good share of this would have fallen to the players had they only done themselves justice, but it is quite obvious that the bulk of the forwards were out of condition—a state of things for which they are entirely to blame. No less than eleven permits to play were obtained by the Warrington Club on Friday, and this fact alone testi- fies eloquently to the misdeeds of the men who failed so signally to uphold the honour of the club on Satur- day. Footballers are only human and subject to humanity's many failings, but the club has a right to expect better service from its players than they have given of late. -- The Rangers came over full of determination to win and the men were obviously in condition to carry out that determination. There was none of "that tired feeling" the advertisements talk of, apparent on their side, ' whereas some of the Warrington men could not raise a gallop in the last quarter. Wilson and Co., put in all they knew, and just succeeded in overcoming the home defence. The best defence, however,is attack, and this Warrington were lacking. What attacking they did was without sting, and but for one effort by Fish which resulted in a try to Davies, they were easily repulsed. The blame is mostly at the door of the forwards and half backs. In the scrum Warrington were beaten in a fashion wh; ch was really a disgrace, and by an eight not one bit more powerful in physique. At half Davies alone played well and he was incapacitated for a good por- tion of the game or the result might have been different. Mahoney, who deputised Bate, played the worst game he has ever done for the Warrington club, and his effarts were continually in the wrong c.irecticin. Warrington might not have had a threequarter line, considering the few attempts made to get the quartette in motion. Altogether the tactics of the homesters were bad, hopelessly bad.</p>
        <p>On the Rangers' side there was plenty :of initia- tive and resource. S. James and Wilson were always active and clever, and it was entirely due to the ex- ertions of these two men, supported bv the forwards, that enabled the Broughtomans to win. -up to half- time Warrington appeared likely to keep their 3 point lead, but they gave way completely in the later stages, and the strong Broughton attack was pushed home. There was a great contrast in half back play. Warrington tried too much on their own, and were never successful in breaking away,whilelthe Rangers' pair merely stood well back and fed the threeluarters. The same tactics on the home side would hare paid for the threequartlr line was the last hope,and it was shockingly served I know the Warrington halves have instructions to beat their men, but when they fail here they should try the other and simpler method of getting the attacking line going.</p>
        <p>Taken all round the game was by no means a poor ono, for both sides played open football. Wilson and Hogg did some very attractive passing and running, while Harry and Widuleson were both smart. W.James was a mere passenger, and, like Fish, he is not in condition. Both mea, are bulkier than they should be. The two full backs played excellently, and Hallam and Poole have nothing to reproach themselves with.</p>
        <p>Oldkam's defeat at Wigan, and successes by Brad- ford and Leeds, made the competition still more even, Just now the two Yorkshire clubs have the be't chance. The table up-to-date is as follows :</p>
        <p>For. Against. P. W. L. D. G. T. P. G. T. P. Pts Oldham 23...15- 7... 1...25 37-161... 7 14-56...31 Bradford Salford 92 .14. 6: 2...39 52-234-11 20-82...30 Lewis 20...14... 4... 2-15'34-132 9 17-69...30 Beghton.R 22...13... G... 3...15 51-183...21 17-93...29 Hunslet 22...14:.. 7: 1 .28 30-146...15 20-90...29 Hull K't'n R 22..12... 9... 1...17 27-115...11 23-91...25 Warrington 20...11... 7... 2...20 32-136..12 19-81...24 Swinton Halifax 2l. .10 3: .15 14— 72...17 16-82...19 Wigan Runcorn ... 21... 8...12... 1...23 21-109...25 34-152..17 Hull Widnes 23. 6..14. 3..14 15— 73...24 49-195..15 Batley Leigh Hudderstrld 21... 6...15... 0...10 27-101...22 40-166..12 Keighley 20:..' 4...12... 4...21 10— 72-28 41-179...12</p>
        <p>Warrington' § visit to Hunslet to-day will* not ill their supporters with hopefulness,, for the record of the team, in the Leeds suburb is not pleasant reading. The forwards will have to display vastly different form than of late, or they will be pushed off the earth, so to speal. They have every inducement to try hard' and I hope they will. Warrington have nothing to fear in theaunslet back division.</p>
        <p>Davies will be an absentee, not having recovered' from. the blow he receiv&amp;l in the Rangers' match. Bate and Mahoney thus operate at the base of the scrum, There are also two changes fzirward, Cooke and Lunt, displacing Edmondson and Naylor. The full team is: Hallam, full bask; Fish, Isherwood, Dickenson, and' Hockenhall, three-quarters; Bate and' Mahoney, half backs; Boardman, Fielding, Thomas, Cook, Lunt, Swift, ,Tolley and Morrison, forwards.</p>
        <p>Harris turns, out for the "A " team to-day against Rochdale Rangers, the smart Junior Leaguers. The other three-quarters will be Short (centre) and Kenyon.</p>
        <p>Messrs.Eustance and Co.have generously presented a Silver. Challenge Cup to the Works League Competi- tion, and it is now on view in their windows. It is a handsome two handled cup of modern design, richly engraved and embossed, standing on ebony plinth and surmounted with embossed cover supporting a model of a Rugby Football Player. The inscription on the Cup which is of solid silver throughout, is as follows. "Warrington Rugby Football Club, NH. Works League Challenge Cup, presented by Messrs. Eustance and Co., 1904."</p>
        <p>- St. Peter's Church. On Monday evening the annual tea party and enter- tainment in connection with this church were held in the schcol, John street. Nearly 209 members of the congregation sat down to tea, the following ladies pre- siding at the tables :—Mesdames Percival, Bamforth, Lingley, Joseph Taylor, John Burdett, Yeomans, Skelland, Webster aid Gleaves,—Councillor John Roberts presided at the concert which followed, but before the programme was commenced a very interesting ceremony took place. Mrs. Percival being presented on behalf of the members of the Mothers' Meeting with a handsome dinner service. The Chairman made the presentation and Mrs. Percival acknowledged the gift in a neat little speech.—Re- viewing the work of the parish, the Chairman remarked that he did not know of a parish which had got a better staff of workers than St. Peter's had. ,He was thankful to say that their esteemed Vicar was now in good health. (Applause.) The Vicar, after remarking upon the generosity of the Mother's Meeting in makin,, Mrs. Percival a present, gave some advice as to what should be done in the parish during the present year. He thought they could easily raise about £25 in the parish towards the new Liverpool Cathedral scheme.—The programme, which wasadmirablyarranged by Mr. F.Webster consisted of selections by a quartette party, solos by Miss A. Cook, Miss Fairhurst, Mr. W. R. Roberts,and Mr. P. George, and humorous songs by Mr. R. Sumner. Mr. F. Webster, accompartied.—The Rev. D. R. James proposed a vote of thanks to the artistes, Mr. Steel, seconding. The Vicar /1150 ' thanked Councillor Roberts for taking the chair.</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>••••■l-;i</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>NOW IS THE TIME.</head>
        <p>NOW IS THE TIME.</p>
        <p>Do you enjoy perfect health? If not,why not? The simplest way for man to secure immunity from disease is to follow, as far as practicable, the laws of nature, and if illness overtakes him, to take as remedies only such preparations as are supplied by nature. The only natural remedies are vegetables and the chief vegetable remedy of the present age is vegetables, EVUIS' Quinine Bitters, the Vegetable Tonic. GWILYM EVANS' QUININE BITTERS. Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters is a preparation now extensively taken throughout the country by patients -suffering from debility, nervousness, and general ex- haustion, and if any value be attached to human testi- mony the efficacy of this medicine is established. Its claims have been tested and proved by the medical profession and others, and corroborated by the written testimonials of eminent men. OUGHT TO BE IN EVERY HOUSE.</p>
        <p>Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters contains not only a suitable quantity of quinine in each dose, but the active principles of the following well-known herbs—sarsapar- illa, saffron, gentian, lavender, burdock, and dandelion. At this season of the year no one should be without Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, the Best Remedy of the Age for various ailments. GWILYM EVANS' QUININE BITTERS. Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters is recommended for indigestion in its different forms, such as sick head- aches, heartburn, cramp, flatulency, sense of fulness and oppression after eating, drowsiness, and pains in the region of the heart. It is, without exception, the best remedy for depression of spirits and melancholia. Being a vegetable "Pick-me-up,' it is strongly recommended for nervous diseases, such as undue anxiety, despond- elcy, fainting, fits, neuralgia, and nerve pains generally STRONGLY RECOMMENDED. Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters has successfully treated (after all otherknown preparations had failed) severe cases of affections of the chest, such as common colds, bronchitis,asthmatic colds,shortness of breath,influenza, etc. It purifies and enriches the blood. It is a healthy and safe tonic. Should be taken by all who are deli- cate,as it strengthens the system and promotes good health. People suffering great weakness, either after an illness, or through long confinement in ill-ventilated rooms, or any other cause, find Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters most valuable as a great strength reviver. GWILYM EVANS' QULITINE BITTERS. Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters is sold everywhere in bottles 2s. 9d. and 4s. 6d. each or will be sent,on receipt of stamps, carriage free, direct from the Sole Pro- prietors :—Quinine Bitters Manufacturing Company, Limited, Llanelly, South Wales. BEWARE OF IMITATIONS.</p>
        <p>When you ask -fo-r.Gwilym Evans' Quinine Bitters, see that you get it, with the name "Gwilym Evans" on the label, on the stamp, and on the bottle, without which none is genuine.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE THE MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON MAN'S ING RAT ITUDE-E BEZZLEM ENT</p>
        <p>'FROM AN EMPLOYER.</p>
        <p>George Mills, a married man residing in Cumberland street, was charged at the Warrington Police Court on Tuesday with embezzling 40s received byhim on account of his employer, Mr. John Bayley Isaac, junior, pro- vision dealer, Iforsemarket-sireet.—He pleaded guilty— Mr. Unsworth, solicitor, prosecuted and pointed out that Mr. Isaac had heen very loth to bring the case be- fore the magistrates. The nature of the embezzlement was such,however,that he felt he would not be doing his duty to the public if he did not institute the proceedings as a warning to others. He was not anxious to be vindictive in ftny sense. In August last prisoner went to Mr. Isaac and told him he was" on the rocks." He begged very hard for a situation and Mr. Isaac took compassion on him and engaged him. A few weeks ago the prosecutor had cause to suspect that there was something wrong. Prisoner was, in fact, caught red-handed in the act of taking a small sum from the till, and he was at once dischaviged. After he had left Mr. ISarIC found that bills which he thought were owing had been paid. Mr. Watkins, formerlyimanager of the Conservative Club paid prisoner several sums amounting in all to £3 6s. 4d., since October, and not a penny of it had been accounted for. Prisoner seemed to have been able to prevent the "accounts rendered" from going out or the theft would have been discovered sooner. Prisoner, who made his first appearance, said "it was all through drink."—He was fined £5 with the option of a month's imprison- ment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>Warrington Borough Police Court.</head>
        <p>Warrington Borough Police Court.</p>
        <p>Minor Cases. AlONtoAY.—Before the Mayor • (presiding), Messrs. Geddes, Birchall, R. T. Fairclough, J. W. Haywvd, and Wright ; Aldermen Smethurst and Hutchinson and Councillor Thornton. LOAFING NEAR BRIDGE-FOOT.—Walter ElliSjabollrer, 21, Portland-street, and James Nicholson, bricklayer, 5, Ellesmere-street, were summoned for having obstructed the free passage of Bridge-stre9t by standing there on the 27th January.—The Chief Constable said that for 'SOM3 time Messrs. Greenhalgh and Company, furniture dealers, had been very much annoyed and suffered pecuniary loss in consequence of loafers standing in crowds near the shop window. Bridge-foot had from time immemorial been the resort and gathering place of loafers. Messrs. Greenhalgh's employees had had to go out and request the men to go away from the front of the shop, but no notice was taken of what they said. The complaints to the police became so titter that the officer on duty at the other side of Warrington Bridge was told to keep a special watch on the men. When he remonstrated with them the men went away but when he had turned his back they at once returned.—P.C. Cox gave evidence bearing out this statement.—Mr. William Nichol, said that by standing about the shop loafers prevented respectable ladies and gentlemen from looking at the windows.—Defendant 8 were ordered to pay the costs 3s. 6d.</p>
        <p>_ MiscuLaNY.—For having committed a breach of the peace by being disorderly, on the 24th January, William McLoughlin, 23, Morris-court, was bound over in the sum of £5 to keep the peace. It was stated that on the date in question defendant was using disgraceful language in the centre of a large crowd off Church-street. —Catherine Clancy, 25, School-brow, was fined ss. and costs for having used obscene language.—A carter, named Edward Litherland. 58, Fox-street, was fined ss. and costs for having caused an obstruction in Bridge- street, by leaving his cart there for an unreasonable time on the 23rd January.</p>
        <p>TVESDAY.—Before Dr. F..sm. (.in the chair), and Arder-</p>
        <p>man Hutchinson.</p>
        <p>SHOP DOOR THEFT.—EIiza Higham, 3, Dial-yard, pleaded guilty to a charge of stealing a pair of boots, value ct 2s. 11d., from outside the shop of Messrs Park and Bowden, clothiers and boot dealers, 59, Horse- market-street, oh Monday.--Inspector Phillips stated that the boots were seen outside the shop at twelve o'clock on Monday. About three o'clock the woman went to Mr. Tyrer's pawnshop in Winwick-street and offered the boots in pledge. The aiktant, Walter Bracegirdle, was suspicious, and question 4if the prisoner, who stated that she bought the boots in the market. She gave a wrong name and address, and went away. The assistant went after her, and she was given in charge</p>
      </div>
