        <p>Wycliffe P.S.A. Society. I The usual meeting ws held on Sunday afternoon, and. Iwas presided over by Mr. D. Owen. Mr. Hunter,of Man- chester, was the speaker, and spoke upon '• Memory." At the Council meetine on Tuesday Councillor DODDS As a basis for his address, he took a passage of scrip. drew attention to the 'following resolution of the ture "I will remember Thee." Memory, he said Water Committee:—" That the Deputy Chairman and was a wonderful faculty, a great blessing to Mr. Councillor Duncan be appointed to meet a repre- have, but a groat Misfortune not to have. Our sentative of the Pearson and Knowles Coal and Iron hearts ought to abound with thankfulness to God Co. Ltd., and forthwith place the order with that Corn- , for a good memory. How did men remember God? pany for repair of the engines at Winwick." 1 It was often harder to remember good than bad. This He said he would willingly propose an amendment could be overcome by a willing submission to God's that this contract be put to tender as it ought to be, if guidance. Many people remembered God about.them,but someone would support him. He thought it was an did not submit to His ruling. They keptlGod in the irregular thing to take a job ta any firm and give it to back ground in calm days, but when the storm came, them without a tender. There were at least two other : they fancied God had forgotten them. In dangers men firms in the town who could have done the job. He did think of God, but it was better far to tbink of thought there appeared to be something behind this. i God in bright as well as dark days in happy times as The TOWN CLERK: The order has been placed and : well as in times of adversity. God beyond all our con- the work commenced. , ception thought of them. In Him they lived and moved Councillor A. PEACOCK: Probably Councillor Dodds Let them always remember Him by prayer, faith and knew that. I work. In the depths of sin aud depravity men migbt Councillor DODDS: I think it should 