      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0087" n="art0087">
        <head>TOWN COONCIL</head>
        <p>TOWN COONCIL</p>
        <p>ANOTHER PROTRACTED SITTING.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Warrington Council was held at the Town Hall on Tuesday evening, the Mayor (Alderman Bolton) presiding. There were also present:—The Deputy-Mayor (Alderman Fair°lough), Alderman A. Bennett, 'R. W. Francomb, T. Hutehinson W. Pierpoint, J. Smethurst, Councillors T. H. Ashcroft' T. Barton, W. Brough, T. Burton, J. G.Craik, S. Davies, J. Dodds, D. S. Duncan, James Evans, John Evans, L. Ellison, W. J. Forshaw, S. Gough, A. Horroiks, T. J. Hulse, J. Lord, J. Maginn, A. Peacock, J. Pemberton J. Roberts, W. Rogers, F. Stott, H. Thornton, D. Tinnion, J. Wilkinson, W. J. Wilson, with J. L. Matte (Town Clerk), S. Wilson (Deputy Town Clerk), J. Fair- hurst (Borough Treasurer), T. L. Longdin (Borough Surveyor), W. S. Haddock (Gas Engineer), and J. M. Murray (Director of Education).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0088" n="art0088">
        <head>WATpH COMMITTEE</head>
        <p>WATpH COMMITTEE</p>
        <p>A good portion of the minutes of the Watch Corn- mittee was taken up with resolutions relating to the tenders for police clothing.—lt was resolved that the report of the Stage Plays Sub-committee on their ex- amination of places licensed for the performance of stage plays be printed and submitted to the Council. The MAYOR moved the confirmation of the minutes and said the report of the Chief Constable was a very elaborate one. Parr Hall seemed to be the worst de- linquent, but the matter referred to in the report would be inquired into. The DEPUTY-MAYOR seconded.</p>
        <p>Alderman PEERPoiNT alluded to the condition of Parr Hall, and said he hoped at the earliest possible moment some provision would be made for goods that had to be stored in the corridors, and that a proper warehouse would be provided. On the next occasion when there was an exhibition special attention should. be paid to lighting. Naked lights should not be al- lowed. Fortunately no accident happened, but one might some day happen with open lights. The MAYOR.—i will call a meeting next week to con- siderithe matter. The minutes were then passed. • COUNCILLOR DODDS AND INCOME TAX PAYMENTS.</p>
        <p>The Financeand Estate Committee submitted letters from the Local Government Board. agreeing to the borrowing of £1,440 for Sankey recreation ground and of .£6,500 for providing a working balance for carrying the Education Act, 1902, into effect. The DEPUTY MAYOR moved the confirmation of the minutes.</p>
        <p>Councillor DODDS seconded, and alluded to two accounts amounting to from £1,300 to ,E 1,400 passed at the last meeting of the Finance Committee in favour of the Inland Revenue and representing income tax on the profits of the Water and Gas Departments. He ex- pressed the hope that the statements of these accounts complicated and intricate as the various classes of interests involved made thera,might be so arranged as to preclude the giving away in income tax of more money then was necessary • money whieh the rateyayers could well do with, and which, if saved, would help the rates very much indeed. (Hear, hear). The minutes were agreed to. SMALLPDX.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0089" n="art0089">
        <head>WANT OF ADMINISTRATIVE BUILDINGS.</head>
        <p>WANT OF ADMINISTRATIVE BUILDINGS.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Health Committee the Chairman reported that an inspector of the Local Government Board had interviewed him and the Town Clerk and Medical Officer with reference to the outbreak of small pox, and, after inspection of the Sanatorium, had urged the advisability of the Corporation erecting the administrative ward there. It was resolved that the Surveyor obtain tenders for the erection of the block. The Board also urged upon the Council the desirability of their providing, in pursuance of their powers under Section 15 of the Infectious Diseases (Prevention) Act, 1890 (which is in force in the borough), premises for the temporary shelter of persons whose houses are being disinfected. It was resolved that the Hospitals Sub- committee be requested to consider and report on the provision of temporary shelters. Alderman SMETHURST proposed the adoption of the minutes, and said he was pleased to hear that the small- pox outbreak was decreasing a little. (Hear, hear.) Councillor JAMES EVA.NS seconded.</p>
        <p>Councillor LORD asked how it was that the adminis- trative buildings had not been erected, seeing that tle money had been borrowed, and whether the money had been used for any other purpose than that for which it was set aside.</p>
        <p>Alderman SMETHURST replying, said that when the Sanatorium was built it was thought the outbuilding, which was used as a farmhouse, would be sufficient for their requirements. They had no idea that smallpox would break out; and if it had not broken out they would not have heard anything about the new accom- modation. He believed the money was still in hand. He might say that the Borough Surveyor was using up a great deal of the old material, and had saved them hundreds of pounds in this one instance. The building of the block would go on as soon as they had got clear of the smallpox patients. 4</p>
        <p>• - Councillor WrmussoN remarked that such questions as they had just been dealing with made them feel that they did not know where they were. (Laughter.) They were not in the habit of using money for other pur- poses than those for which it was obtained. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>Councillor PENIBFIRTON said the point was whether the work had been let. Had the contract been given or not?</p>
        <p>Alderman SMET HURST : Tenders have not yet been obtained for the work. The money for the work is in hand and the work will be proceeded with as soon as possible. The minutes were then approved. STREET PAVING. COUNCILLOR GOUGFI'S QUESTIONS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0090" n="art0090">
        <head>"VERY STUPID AND VERY FRIVOLOUS."</head>
        <p>"VERY STUPID AND VERY FRIVOLOUS."</p>
        <p>Councillor GOUGH asked the Chairman of the Paving and Sewerage Committee the following questions :—(1) Why is Jockey-street, which is a front street in the borough, being paved with macadam instead of being paved with setts or cubes? (2) Does not Alderman Heaton (thp Chairman of the Paving and Sewerage Committee) own all the property on one side of this street? Are aldermen and councillors exempt from the provisions of the law as to the street-making, or is there one law for aldermen and another] for the property owners who are not members of the Council? (3) Are Councillors Arthur Peacock and John Wilkin- son paving part of Dudley-street, and is the work under the supervision of the Borough Surveyor? Does he see that quantities and qualities of materials are being used in accordance with the required specifi- 'cations of the Corporation? How is it that property owners wile are not members of the Town Council are foroed to have their streets made through or by the Corporation? (4) Is the contractor who is making Algernon-street and other streets near to it, using Port- land oement in making the concrete? If not, what test will the cement bear that is being used, after the work has stood seven days? Will this work bear the test of 450 lb. to the square inch? And can the Borough Surveyor take up a square yard to test in the presence of members of the Council on a day to be named? (5) Shen are the Paving and Sewerage Committee going to provide a weighing machine at Howley Storage Yard,so that members of the Council may get a correct account or knowledge of what material the Corporation use during a year, what material there is in stock, and whether the Corporation gets weight for the money it pays? Councillor TINNION said he was exceedingly sorry the Chairman of the Paving and Sewerage Committee was not present. He was far frqm well, and had been in bed for a short time. They would be glad to know however, that he was making progress towards re- covery. He was sorry because the Chairman would have been able to answer and to understand Councillor Gough's questions better than he could. He really could not understand them but he would do his best. (Laughter.) He did not know whether they were questions or conundrums or what they were, but he did know that a large proportion of them were very stupid and very frivolous. (Laughter.) The MAYOR: You are not bound to answer them unless you like. Councillor TINNION : I'll do my best. When ques- tions are asked like this, it is a pity not to answer them. At the same time it is a stupid way of seeking information. - - -</p>
        <p>--6-0-u-n-c *LI fo-t: GOUGH : I could not get it any other way. Councillor TlNNloNsaid with regard to the first ques- tion he did not exactly know what was meant by a "front street," but he supposed that Councillor Gough meant to ask why Jockey-street was being repaired with macadam at the present time? The street was one made up and taken over from the Rural District Authority in 1893, when they took over that street with all its disabilities, since they could not call upon the owners to do something different to that which they had already paid for. With regard to the second ques- tion, Alderman Heaton had told him that morn- ing that he only owned one house on one side of the street. In No. 3 he pointed out that an error had been made in names. However, Councillor Arthur Peacock and Mr. Joseph Wilkinson were paving part of Dudley-street. They went to the committee and asked if they could pave this street. There was nothing to prevent any owner from paving his own street,but unless it was done to'the satisfaction of the Borough Surveyor the street would not be adopted, and the owner would be forced to maintain and scavenge it for all time. With regard to No. 4 question, he saw that the paving material to be used was described in the specification as being capable of bearing a tensile strain of 300 per square inch. As to a pressure of 4501 b. he was not aware that the concrete would be required to bear such pressure nor how it would be applied nor who would do so. With regard to No. 5 question he pointed out that there was aweighing machine at the entrance to the Electricity Station that was only a few yards away from the Howley Sewerage Yard and which was used for Corporation material. It would be folly to lay down another one there. AN INIPROVENIENT AT LATCHFORD.</p>
        <p>The minutes of the Paving and Sewerage Committee related principally to the apportionments of the cost of forming and paving streets.</p>
        <p>Councillor TINNION moved the adoption of the minutes.—Councillor ROBERTS seconded. Councillor ELLISON pointed out that the committee proposed to flag the footpath from Captain Waring's house to the Swing Bridge on the Knutsford-road. He urged that the work should be deferred, and that nego- tiations should be entered into with the Ship Canal Company to purchase a strip of land at this part to im- Prove and to make the road less dangerous. After some discussion the MAYOR stated that the Borough Surveyor had informed him that the cost of thesuggested improvement was very trifling, and if the matter were left in the hands of the Committee they would attend to it.</p>
        <p>Alderman PIERPOINT, who had brought the matter before the Committee said he should be perfectly satisfied with tke original scheme. The minutes were confirmed. A MURDERED KINGFISHER. ALDERMAN PIERPOINT AND THE _ _ "SCOUNDREL WITH A GUN." .</p>
        <p>AldermanßExxErr moved the minutes of the Museum Committee, which were of a formal character. Councillor ELLisoN seconded. Alderman PIERPOINT said he noticed among the gifts a "Kingfisher from Orford." This' was a com- mittee that should set a good example, but, he would like an explanation from the Chair- man as to this item. Was it a case in which a beautiful bird was flying about and in which some seoundrel with a gun shot it? He felt that the committee were helping to break the law, and the bird should be sent back to the vandal who sent it. (Hear. hear.) The Mayor.—But he has had a vote of thanks from the committee for sending it. (Laughter). Alderman Pierpoint.—Pshaw ! Ridiculous ! Mon- strous !—The Mayor.—The committee might take into consideration what you say and might diseourage such gifts in the future.—Alderman Bennett said he was favourable to the kingfishers, but in the vicinity of towns like Warrington it seemed hopeless to preserve them, and he would rather that an enlightened naturalist should give the Museum Committee the benefit of his murderous instincts—(laughter)—than that others should destroy them from pure wantonness.—Alderman Pierpoint.—He ought to be put in gaol. (Laughter).— The minutes were then adopted. HALFPENNY BATHING TICKETS.</p>
        <p>Councillor WILSON asked the Chairman of the Town Hall Committee whether the committee would consider the desirability of encouraging the art of swimming among women and children by granting halfpenny bath- ing tickets.—The Mayor said that the committee were willing to consider anything that was for the benefit of the residents of the borough,but questions of that kind were asked there and never came to anything ; instead, it was wasting the time of the Council. The committee were ready to consider every reasonable matter brought before them.—Councillor Wilson: lam not wasting the time of the Council. lam a new member, and lam asking the committee to consider it.—The Mayer: Well, you are wasting printer's ink, since the question is printed on the minutes.—Councillor Wilson: To teach young children habits of cleanliness is not wasting valuable time.—The Mayor: We cannot discuss it here and questions of this sort are only asked to effect notoriety and have no valise. _ _</p>
        <p>ENLARGEMENT OF THE COUNCIL CEIAMBER. The Town Hall, Parks, and Baths Committee have passed a resolution to the effect that the Council Chamber be enlarged, and that additional committee rooms and offices be provided, together with a new public entrance to the Council Chamber, and that the Surveyor submit a sketch of the plans of the proposed alterations.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR moved the adoption of the minutes. Alderman SM ETH URST seconded.</p>
        <p>Alderman BENNETT asked if it was only sketch plans of proposed alterations to the Council Chamber that the Borough Surveyor was to prepare. The MAYOR replied in till affirmative,and added that the Council Chamber as it was at present and as the Council was at present constituted was against the health of the councillors. It was built for use at a time when the meetings only lasted perhaps half an hour; •now they lasted three or four hours. (The Deputy Mayor : "Shame," and laughter.) There was the health of the members to be considc:ed and from a healthy and sanitary standpoint something ought to be done. The Surveyor was to draw up sketch plans so that the Chamber might be brought into a healthy condi Lion.</p>
        <p>Before the minutes were adopted Alderman Pi- POINT suggested suggested that one or two meetings might b 3 held in the Coroner's court at the Polka Buildings which was not used much, so that they might sae the contrast. (Laughter.) '</p>
        <p>THE MARKETS COMMITTEE AND THE FAIRS. The minutes of the meetings held on November 30th and 7th and 14th December having been referred back for further consideration, it was resolved that the minutes be againsubraitted to the Council for approval with the exception of those relating to the abolition of the caktle and pleasure fairs.—Alderman Hutchinson moved the adoption of the minutes.—Councillor Rogers seconded, the minutes being confirmed. THE PRICE OF GAS. SLOT METER USERS PAYING 7D. PER 1,000 MORE.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Gas Commiteee on January 22nd, letters were read from Councillor Wilson and from [Councillor Stott, secretary of the Trades and Labour ICouncil, asking the committee to take into considera- tion the reduction of the price of gas charged to slot meter consumers. In accordance with the instructions of the committee the Engineer presented the following report with reference to use of slot meters:—"The total number of slot meters in use on the 31th March last, was 5,574. Taking the average consumption of gas through these meters for lighting purposes at 6,000 cubic feet per annum, the following figures give the receipts and expenditure per slot meter supplied : Receipts: Extra cash received over and above the 2s, 6d. net per 1,000 cubic feet charged to ordinary consumers on sale of 6,000 cubic feet of gas at 10d. per 1,000, total ss. Expenditure: Loss of meter rent, Is. 6d.; say 10 per cent on 165.; extra cost of slot meter ever others, Is. 7d.; shorter life of slot meter as compared to others say 5 per cent. at 445., 2s 2d ; extra cost of collecting, id ,• total, 5s 4d. Showing a net loss of fourpence where the consumer burns 6,000 cubic feet per annum, and a corresponding greater loss where a less quantity of gas is consumed. I The figures given above for extra cost and less life of meter owing to the great strain set up in the coin mechanism are under estimated on the earlier form of meter. As we gained experience in the working of these, meters, improvements have been made and the newer' form of meter is now lowering these costs. A large' number of the slot meter consumers use the cooker, which is'supplied free of charge by the department. This raises the average consumption to 8,200 cubic feet per meter. The extra amount thus received does not more than cover the repairs and maintenance." The DEPUTY MAYOR moved the adoption of the com- mittee's minutes, and said he thought the report with reference to slot meters met what had been asked by Councillors Wilson and Stott. • Councillor WILSON considered that the slot meter consumers ought to have the gas cheaper. Councillor SToTT thought it was hardly fair that slot meter consumers should have to pay sevenpence more per thousand than ordinary consumers. He had always understood that the*Gas Committee encouraged the use of slot meters, so that poor people could have gas instead of using the dangerous low flash oil in paraffut lamps, but to him it seemed that they were not trying to encourage the poor to use gas seeing that they were charging more than they were the other con- sumers, who could afford to pay their bills half-yearly. He did not think any profit whatever should be made upon gas. There were many large ratepayers who did not use gas, but who generated electricity for their own use; and, therefore, if they made profits on the gas and let it go to the relief of the rates,those large ratepayers benefited at the expense of the gas users. That made it that the •working classes, having to pay high prices, assisted to relieve the rates of those persons who did not use gas. It would seem that the Gas Committee were inclined to let matters rest where they were, and that being the case it would be his duty at some future date to move that the price of gas be reduced. He thought it ought to be reduced, as it was very unjust to rob—he could use no better term—a certain section of ratepayers to relieve the rates of others. The MAYOR said the committee were prepared to do anything they could for the betterment of the slot meter users as soon as they saw their way clear. Councillor PEMBERTON also thought it was unfair to make the slot meter users pay more than ordinary con- sumers.He thought the working classes ought to be con- sidered. They. were making large profits on the gas. He was referring to the profits, and what might be done with them, when Councillor TINNION said the point on the minutes ought to be kept to. Councillor PEMBERTON: Councillor Tinnion objects to the working man getting his gas as cheaply as he can. Councillor TINNION : No, I don't ; but I abject to Councillor Pemberton's gas. (Laughter.) The MAYOR said the committee would try and deal equitably all round, and they would do the best they could in the matter, and as soon as they could see their way to reduce the price to the slot users they would do so. -- - _ -</p>
        <p>- Councillor JOHN EVANS said the cost of the slot</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <head>NEW ERA IN HOME BAKING.</head>
        <p>A NEW ERA IN HOME BAKING.</p>
        <p>A ne4 era in home baking has dawned for those who are using Brown &amp; Poison's new raising powder called "Paisley Flour." Housewives all over the country are finding that with it cakes, pastries, &amp;c., can be made deliciously light and tasty and so digestible, and this with ease and a certainty of good results unknown be- fore. Those who bake regularly at home and those who do not, possibly owing to disappointment with their first efforts, should buy a 7d, packet of" Paisley Flour" at once from their grocers and try, say, the recipes for "muffins," " little dinner loaves" or "jam sandwich." Home baked scones and cakes have a freshness and sweetness of flavour not always associated with bought articles. If you are not satisfied with" Paisley Flour," the grocer will refund your money at Brown &amp; Poison's expense. Apply to Brown &amp; Poison Dept. Ml 9, Paisley, enclosing Id stamp for their "A " Cook Book. It con- tains a variety of recipes for simple and dainty fare.</p>
        <p>meters (£2 each) was a big consideration, and it was absolubely impossible to let those consumers have the gas for less money than they were getting it at present. The DEPUTY-MAYOR said the slot users were getting both fair play and fair value. It was no benefit to the committee for slot meters to be used. Further, they wen: constantly having meters smashed openand robbed andthey had borrowed £7,000 or £B,OOO which they had had to pay for the meters. If they reduced the price of gas,the rates would go up. The minutes were put to the meeting and carried. MR. W. S. HOWARD AND HIS DUTIES.</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON asked the Chairman of the Gas Committee the following questions :—(1) Has Mr. W. S. Howard, cashier, acted as deputy-registrar of births and deaths for Warrington, and for how long? (2) What amount of time during the day has he devoted to this work, especially during the last twelve months ? (3) Was this done with the permission of the Corpoya- tion, and if so, on what date was the resolution passed?</p>
        <p>- The DEPUTY-11A.YOR replied as follows to the ques- tions :—(1) Yes; 23 years. Mr. Howard voluntarily resigned the office in October last. (2) Mr. Howard has only acted during the illness or absence of the Registrar. He so acted abont 15 or 16 times during the last twelve months. The time devoted by him was about four hours a day, two hours of which were given in his own time. The nature of Mr. Howard's duties permitted of no neglect, and if necessary Mr. Howard kept up his work after the ordinary office hours. (3) Yes December 31st. 1897. Mr. Howard hal previously obtained the permission of the old Gas Company.</p>
        <p>THE SHIP CANAL BILL. ALDERMAN BENNETT AND THE DOCK. ANOTHER ABORTIVE ATTEMPT.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Rivers Committee on January 22nd it was resolved"that a petition be presented against the Manchester Ship Canal Bill, 1904, and the Town Clerk instructed to take such steps as he may deem advisable to safeguard the interests of the borough. The representative of the Corporation on the Upper Mersey Navigation Commission reported that there was no objection made to Messrs. Crosdeld's application for the construction of, telpher line, Sze., across the river, provided the height was the same as Runcorn Bridge. The charts were produced, showing the channels to be in very good order; the constant dredg- ing by the Ship Canal Company, at Warrington, and the Dock and Harbour Board, at Liverpool, must be a great help in keeping them in good order. It was considered that the application made by the Ship Canal Company to erect sluices at Bank Quay may be detrimental to the channels, and it was considered advisable to take powers to petition against the Bill." The MAYOR proposed, and the DEPUTY-MAYOR. seconded, the adoption of the minutes. Alderman BENNETT said he was very loth to inter- pose a matter which the Council regarded as of little or no importance, but which was really one of the most important questions they could discuss. The Maxoß : Keep to the minutes. Alderman BENNETT said that although on the last occasion the Council did not treat him with proper fair- ness, the Press did, and reported what he said pretty fully, which made it clear that the matter- The MAYOR: What are you talking about? (Laughter.) Alderman BENNETT: Ifyou will have a little patience you will see. lam pleased to see from the minutes that a petition is to be presented against the Ship Canal Bill. The MAYOR: I should like to say that this is one of the things which it would be very advisable to say nothing about. (Hear, hear.) 'Alderman BENNETT: What lam going to say will lae a benefit to everybody if you will only consider it. It is a matter on which we have a right to make sug- gestions if we wish, and I wish to propose that even now it is worth while considering whether or not it would be advisable at some future date to have a dock at Warrington. The TOWN CLERK: The Bill does not deal with the question of a dock at all, nor the petition. . The MAYOR: The petition is just to give us a locus stand/ as a precautionary measure if we want it. Alderman BENNETT: I understood that a Bill before Parliament could be chopped about any .way so as to make it a benefit and suitable to the people who had an interest in it in some way or other. The MAYOR: Now, Alderman Bennett, you know all the difficulties in connection with a dock, and you have had my explanations previously, and [yet you continue to drag the matter in. Councillor DODDS thought Alderman Bennett might be allowed to state his point. Alderman BENNETT: If I had been let alone what I wanted to say would have been said by now. After further argument the matter dropped, and on the Mayor putting the minutes to the meeting they were carried.</p>
        <p>THE TR,AII FARES OF SCHOOL CHILDREN. Councillor WILWN asked the Chairman of the Elec- tricity and Trany,vays Committee "whether the time has not arrived for considering the question pf allow- ing school children to travel by the cars at halfpenny fares." Alderman SMETHURST said the Committee allowed school children to travel at less than a half-penny fare at the present time, if they purchased a monthly ticket. These tickets could be bought at the rate of 4s,a month and the trams could be used as often as the children liked.</p>
        <p>The MAYoR : We all knew that. Alderman SMETHURST: People taking monthly tickets get them at a less rate than a halfpenny fare. Councillor BURTON pointed out that it was not the practice of poor people to take advantage of the monthly tickets for school children. The NaYOR suggested that Councillor Burton should put any suggestion he had in writing and forward it to the committee. _</p>
        <p>Councillor WILSON contended that the Chairman of the committee had not affswered his question in the way he would like. The ,NIA-You : You cannot discuss the question. THE FUEL USED AT THE ELECTRICITY STATION. • THE ADVERTISING OF' TENDERS.</p>
        <p>Replying to questions put by Councillor Pemberton with reference to the quantity of fuel used in the Electricity Department, and the names and quantities supplied by each firm and the price,Alderman Smethurst read a detailed !statement. From this it appears that the Warrington Slate Company supplied 58 tons,W. and J. Richardson 8 tons, Chrutchley and Co., Limited 5024 tons, Pendlebury and Company, 983 tons, Cronipton and Shawcross 3 tons, Wigan Coal and Iron Company Ltd., 57 tons. Tenders were advertised for and, the com- mittee chose on December 15th, the following four firms for a supply of coal for testing purposes :—W. an d J. Richardson, Nottingham cobbles lie. 7d. per ton,Fletcher Burrows and Company, Lovers-lane nuts 10s. sd. per ton, Warrington Slate Company, Limited, unscreened Park Gate 11s, 7d. per ton, Pendlebnry and Company, Nottingham hard steam 12s. 4d. per ton. Up to the present time it had not been convenient to test the above coals, as the boiler housei,floor was up for some considerable period on account of alterations to trenches in connection with the new boiler and mechanical stoker, all coal being trimmed from the bunkers. When this work was completed it was neces- sary to make a thorough test of the stoker before the contractors left and the slack which the committee had been buying during the last few weeks was for that purpose. In conclusion, Alderman SMETHURST pointed out that the Babcock and Wilcox boilers were equipped with patent stokers and se far as the committee could see these stokers would mitigate the smoke nuisance and burn less fuel. They had been trying experiments with fuel at 55., 65., and 7s. per ton and they had got the best results from the highest priced coal. He would be very glad if they were able to as for contracts for the fuel they wanted. The large engine was now at work and was running satisfactorily.</p>
        <p>Councillo LORD seconcied. Councillor PEMBERTON moved an amendment that an account:for £lO6 3s. 11d, for coal purchased from Messrs. Crutchleys,be referred back. He slid so for the reason that he objected to the purehase of coal out of con- tract. He contended that thousands of tons of coal had been purchased by the Electricity Department without inviting tenders. Councillor JAMES EVANS seconded the amendment, and complained that all the ooal bought by the Elec- tricity Department had never been advertised for, but had been bought under the pretence of testing. He could not put it any other way. The MAYOR: We have a boiler that will only use one particular coal. ("Oh, oh.") Councillor JAMES EVANS asked why this had not been bought by tender like other coal._ _ _</p>
        <p>Councillor TH-O-R-N-TON suggested that the figures given by the Chairman should be banded to the reporters. The MAYOR: Do the reporters want them? (Laughter) Councillor THORNTON: Ido for one, aid I daresay many others do. _ _</p>
        <p>COuncillor LORD questioned the statement that the boiler used at the Electricity Station would burn only one class of coal.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: :The Engineer reported that he could not get as good results from other coal. Alderman SMETHURST, in reply, stated that the report of the engineer on the coal tests was read at the com- mittee meeting before Mr. Pemberton. The Engineer pointed out that the results obtained from other sup- plies were not as good as Crutchley's. Of course,in the opinion of Councillor Pemberton, the efficials were telling falsehoods. There was not a man in Warrington who could satisfy Councillor Pemberton. Councillor PEMBERTON : I rise te a point of order. object.</p>
        <p>Alderman SMETHURST : The report was given and you were present when the tests were made. Now you raise the _question.</p>
        <p>Councilfor PEI;IBF,RTON : I object to Alderman Smethurst saying that I consider the officials are dis- honest.</p>
        <p>Alderman SMETHURST : I didn't say you did. Councillor PENIBERTON : I say this continued pur- chase of coal without advertising for coal, is against the Standing Orders.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR : The Alderman says he didn't say what you have complained of, and that is an end of it. Alderman SMETHURST thought Councillor Pemberton ought to attend to the business of the committee more regularly. He had only been present at 16 out of 27 meetings.</p>
        <p>Councillor LORD: I object to the attendances being give?.</p>
        <p>- Alderman SMETHURST : It is getting a common. occurrence for councillors to write a lot of questions and ask fos answers.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: The questions are over. Alderman SMETHURST : lam glad if it has all ended in smoke.</p>
        <p>The amendment was not pressed and the minutes were then agreed to.</p>
        <p>BUSINESS ADJOURNED.</p>
        <p>Councillor MAGINs said he had come to that meeting to propose something for the benefit of the town, but the various members had so many petty differences to settle and so many recriminations to throw at one another that it had taken them until that late hour (9.40) to get through the business. Councillor STOTT suggested that a special meeting should be held next Tuesday but it was unanimously decided to adjourn the following motions until the next monthly meeting : By Councillor Mac-INN: "That it is desirable that a suitable bridge be constructed across the Mersey at Howley, and that plans, specification and estimate of a, single-span iron girder bridge, 60 feet wide, be pre- pared."</p>
        <p>By COll/ICillOr STOTT : "That in order to provide work for unemployed Warrington citizens during the present trade depression, the corporation shall imme- diately proceed with some of the contemplated street improvements, and any other work which can be com- menced without unnecessary delay." By Councillor PEMBERTON : That in future no fuel be used except it It purchased in accordance with the Standing Orclers of the Council; that Corporation servants shall devote their whole time and attention to Corporation work, and shall not be employed in any other work without the permission of the Corporation ; that any member of the Council shall have access to the minutes, invoices, vouchers and papers of all com- mittees." The Committee then rose.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>BILIOUSNESS.</head>
