        <p>The Chair Man in ' moving the adoption of that he had held 166 inquests on persons who were killed the report and statement of accounts said, directly by drio'c.Many °tithe slain were innocantchildren with regard to the progress, or lack of progress who had been accid_ !tal:y smothered by their parents that had been made in the construction of the bridge, when in drink. The Medical Officer cf Health for the engineer, Mr. Webster, reported that the weather Liverpool also pointed out that in one year 2,229 chil- had been very unfavourable for outsicL? work, rid thr :-, dren died before the age of one monilt. Mr. Taylor had caused a good deal of delay. &amp;dile slight delay proceeded t 3 speak of drunkenness amongst husbands had also been due to requirements of the engineers and the pit which had not been in the contract, and Mr. Webster 'able straights to which many of their homes were reduced in consequence. He referred had promised to make out an estimate of the delry due ta 3 to the number of seprations of marriage applied for, o this and to the weather. Upon that estimate and to the lack of common dccencies in the homes directors would decide whether the contractors were in the brought about by the drinking habits of the people. default, and if so, would take measures to protect company. The third cable had been fixed that day, arAl Pressure ought to be brought to bear, continued Mr. Tttylor, on the churches, and make them feel and realise the fourth would be hauled across the Mersey to-dav and their responsibilities and duties in this matter. Every anchored to-morrow. There would then remain thirty- church ought to be actively engaged in temperance four cables to be hauled across and anchored, and work, and trying to bring about an improvement in the Mr. Webster told the directors thaethe hauling of each homes of the people. (Hear, hear.) The magistrates cable would be a quicker job than the previous one; ought also to be appealed to. He believed ther