      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>BOLTED BREAKFASTS.</p>
        <p>"Only five minutes for breakfast this morning !" Isn t that your cry very often? Down goes the meal and away you hurry to business. We entirely agree with an authority who said recently that there is no more fruitful source of breakdown than this behaviour, unless, when it is regularly done, some safe but simple stomach and liver stimulant, such as bile beans be taken. When food is put into the stomach,the stomach cries out for blood to carry on digestion; and when you sit down after a meal, nature sees to it that the blood supply is reduced to certain parts, and increased at the stomach. When you gulp down a meal and hurry off, however, the legs need more blood, the muscles of the back need more, and the heart has got to work harder. You can feel it going " pit, pat," under the extra strain, and, if you could see it, you would find that the stomach is hardly able to call sufficient blood to its vessels to carry on its work.</p>
        <p>If you must hurry immediately after breakfast, why not give the stomach a little help ? A bile bean taken immediately after food, helps the stomach better than anything else. Don't think that in taking bile beans in this way you're dosing youvselves with harmful drugs. You wouldn't think of a bit of liquorice plant in that way. Bile beans are just the essences of certain Austra- lianherbs boiled down and concentrr ted. You can't chew four inches of vegetable st,,an as you go along, but you can take its essence when cetucentrated into a. bile bean, and it does you the same good. Mr. W. C. Chambers, of Cottrill-road, Dalston-lane, Hackney, London, says: "Bolting breakfast and hurrying off to business, swallowing dinner quickly and recommencing work immediately, at last brought on me serious indigestion. I felt a constant sensation of weight and pain at the stomach, had frequent attacks of vomiting, headache, and fits of trembling. Constipation also occurred, and an aching pain in the small of my back. My eyes became so weak that I could not bear strong light. The extra work which hurrying after food throws upon the heart, weakened that organ so much that my circulation became defective and my hands and feet were always cold. From medical advice I got no benefit, but soon after commencing with bile beans I felt an improvement. I persevered with them, and in the end they removed all my ailments. lam now free from indigestion and all its effects, and am restored to good health." Charles Forde's bile beans may be had from all medicine vendors.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>PROMOTION FOR A_COURAGEOUS POLICE</head>
        <p>PROMOTION FOR A_COURAGEOUS POLICE</p>
        <p>OFFICER.</p>
        <p>With the authority of the Watch Committee, the Chief Constable, Mr. Luke Talbot, has promoted Conqable J. Lewis, the police officer in charge of the Borough Police Out-Station in Winwick-- road, to the first class of Merit, with the First Class Merit Badge and corresponding increase of pay, in recognition of his (Lewis's) courageous conduct in attempting to stop a runaway horse in Winwick-road on Wednesday last, whereby he received personal injuries. It appears that at 12 noon on the day in question P.C. Lewis was on duty near Winwick-road railway bridge when he observed a horse attached to a float approaching town at a rapid gallop from the direction of Longford, without a driver. The officer pluckily made for the animal's head, and, catching the reins, was dragged some dis- tance when the reins gave way' causing him to fall under the horse. He rolled clear of the wheels but sustained rather severe injuries to the head and shoulder. He was temporarily stunned and was picked up by some gentlemen passing who carried him into a shop, called Dr. Adams, and assisted the officer to his house. Although not yet able to resume duty we learn he is making satisfactory progress towards recovery from the effects of his accident. Several eye-witnesses of the occurrence describe the Constable's action as an exceedingly courageous one We understand the horsb :and float belong to Mr. F. White, Bridge Foot Laundry, and were in charge of a man named Pane at Winwick on Wednesday. Pane had occasion to leave the animal temporarily when it bolted towards Warrington. Its career was ultim- ately checked near Haydock-street where it collided with a lurry, causing considerable damage to the float.</p>
        <p>NEWTON-I'N-IVIAKERFIELD AND THE CARNEGIE OFFER Considerable discussion took place at the monthly meeting of the Newton-in-Makerfield District Council, on Monday evening, with reference to the poll of rate- payers concerning the acceptance or rejection of the Carnegie offer of £4,000 and the adoption of the Public Libraries Act. The poll showed that 1,060 ratepayers were in favour and 848 against. Councillor Clarke alleged that the conduct of the poll was not satisfac- tory. The Clark (Mr. Cole) replied that the election had been properly conducted, and he was assured the collectors had done their duty. Ultimately a vote o thanks was passed to the chairman and clerk for their work. The meeting terminated in a discordant manner.</p>
