      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>CORPORATION OFFICIALS' SALARIES.</head>
        <p>CORPORATION OFFICIALS' SALARIES.</p>
        <p>NUMEROUS INCREASES.-ANIMATED DEBATES.</p>
        <p>There were several resolutions of committees approv- g of increases of salaries to Corporation servants oiscussed at the Council meeting on Tuesday. The first considered was that of the Finance and Estate Committee namely, that the salary of the chief assistant to the borough collector, Mr. F. Timperley, be increase( from 4150 to £175 per annum by three annual increments; and that the maximum salary of the office fixed by the Connell in 1895 be raised from £l5O to £175.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>MR. F. TIMPERLEY.</head>
        <p>MR. F. TIMPERLEY.</p>
        <p>Alderman FAIRCLOUGH, in moving the confirmation et the minutes of the committee, said Mr. Timperley was taken over from the Waterworks Company after Use purchase of that undertaktng. He had been in the nervice of the company and the Corporation 21 years, and had been of great service to them from his having thorough knowledge of the water and fittings charges. Ile also acted as n assistant collector to 1899; his Thaximum of £l5O per annum was fixed in 1895 to cover the above duties. In 1899 the Overseers' department ,n_and the collection of the Poor Rate were added to the 4-reasurer's department. This necessitated a re-arrange- Inent of the staff, and he was appointed counter clerk nd chief assistant to the borough collector, his duties 'Dein: considerably increasedand more responsible. The amount now receive-1 over the counter was about £140,000 a year, as against £51,700 in 1893. He might saY that by the taking over of the Overseers' depart- raent there was a saving to the ratepayers of about £6OO a Year, besides other advantages. Councillor DODDS spoke upon the same subject, and said the Council would probably not object to the increase of the small salaries, but the big salaries sbould stand still. The increase was approved. TWO UNOPPOSED INCREASES.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>MR. SANDERSON,</head>
        <p>MR. SANDERSON,</p>
        <p>The Paving and Sewerage Committee recommended that the salary of the foreman ganger,Mr. W. Sanderson 13e increased from 40s to 503. a week according to the arrangement made with him on his appointment. Councillor Tinnion explained that Mr. Sanderson ac- cepted service under the Corporation at less than he Previously received that he might be in Warrington. They were perfectly satisfieck with his services and it 'as a certainty that he could receive more than they gave from contractors in the town if he had to go. Commalor J. Roberts seconded, and the recommenda- tion was confirmed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>MR. HOWARTH</head>
        <p>MR. HOWARTH</p>
        <p>The Museum Committee recommended that the salary Of Mr. J. W. Howarth, sub-librarianlbe raised to £l2O a Year. Alderman Bennett, in moving the minutes,strongly urged the increase, Councillor Ellison seconded, and the recommendation was carried without opposition or discussion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>MR. ISAAC PARKER</head>
        <p>MR. ISAAC PARKER</p>
        <p>THE PRINCIPLE OF AUTOMATIC INCREASES. COUNCILLOR STOTT'S SUCCESSFUL PROTEST. The Town Hall, Parks, and Baths Committee recom- tended that the salary of the head gardener, Mr. Isaac Parker, be raised from £ll7 to £l3O per annum, rising by annual increases of £5 to a maximum of £l5O. The MAYOR moved the recommendation, and Alder- In SMETHURST seconded. Councillor STOTT said he did not object to an increase ueing granted to Mr. Parker, which would bring his salary to £l3O, but he objected to a maximum of £l5O 4sin. established. He believed in every man being paid a farr and standard wage, but they could get good gardeners and first elass men at muct), less than £l5O per a,unum, and men also who would undertake more and Larger work even thanthe head gardener of Warrington. 41.,..e objected to the promises of annual increases of £5. l'7lLe moved an amendment to the effect that the minutes "8 Passed with the exception of the words relating to ,_aattal incremeats, the recommended increase from £ll7 c'sa £l3O being thus left untouched. Councillor PEMBERTON seconded, and said he was °P.Posed to automatic increases which occurred annually rathout coming to the Council for consent., It was a ,d system, and he seconded the amendment quite I",..espective of the merits of Mr. Parker. „Elle MAYOR explained that maximums were fixed in 18 Way to avoid repeated squabblinoris in the Council questions of salaries came up. He pointed out that Mr. Parker last year had received £l5 in gratuities Ora large amount of work he had undertaken for the ~various other committees. He had had a great deal to o With the tree planting of Warrington, he had done Work for the Health Committee and ought to have his salary raised to something like a satisfactory amount Councillor LORD asked if he had received much assistance. The Ma.yoß: Very little. Alderman SMETHURST spoke supporting the motion nd pointed out that in addition to his ordinary duties ..Lrvlr.) Parker had laid out and looked after the Fever pital grounds which were nearly as large as a park A7ilst the Sankey Sanatorium was another extra place. a 'the present time Mr. Parker was engaged in making , t rwling green in the Victoria Park. He also alluded ° connection with the tree planting in the town, alld further pointed out that the Corporation obliged the 11blic with plants at certain seasons and the returns .Ircm this varied from £1 to £2 per week so that, he contended, in this way Mr. Parker made the larger part of his salary for the orporation. Councillor ASHCROFT supported the amendment, and said he was in favour of £l3O. Councillor WttarssoN supported the recommenda- tion and deprecated the suggestion that there were other Merl who would take the work at less money. Alderman PIERPOINT said be sympathized with the amendment, though he agreed with the minutes as far as the £l3O was concerned. After further discussion the Mayor, as chairman of the committee, agreed to make the alteration, and the minutes were adopted unanimously in the amended form.</p>
        <p>MESSRS. ELIJAH PLACE AND W. H WALLINGTON.</p>
        <p>"SQUALID LITTLE PETTIFOGGING DETAILS." The Markets Committee recommended that the salary of Mr. W. H. Wallington be increased from £lOO to 2120, rising by annual increments of £lO up to a maximum of £l5O per annum; and that the salary of Mr. E. Pface, as market inspector, be increased from £l4O to £l6O per annum. Alderman HuTcHtssoN, in moving the recommenda- tion, spoke at length upon the merits of the two appli- cants. Mr. Wallington was the assistant market inspec- tor, and was a young man who had been with the Cor- Poration for ten years. He commenced with them after Passing the difficult examination in London starting at 2.55. a week. Since then he had been advanced up to .E 2 2s. a week. He was a very energetic man, who knew I.als work well, and who was of very great assistance to re inspector. There was a great 'deal more work to ;10 than formerly, and he gave figures showing that in 1866 the market tolls amounted to £1,017; whilst ten Years later they were £1.945; and ten years later again ,284: and last year £5,065, He also pointed out that the work in other ways had very waterially increased, and urged these arguments also ilFrespect to the pro- 1513sed increase to Mr. Place. There was, he further mrged, no pension to be given to Mr. Place when he retired, and that was probably the last increase he Would ask for. ' He was an old tried servant who had Worked hard. Councillor ROGERS secondedand Councillor ROBERTS SUPBOrted. Councillor PEMBERTON said that whilst supporting strongly the increase of 2120 for Mr. Wallington, he lanSt move as an amendment "That the words rising annual increments of £lO to a maximum of £l5O per annum ' be struck out." Councillor STOTT seconded. The CHAIRMAN said he would agree to that. ,Councillor JAMES EVANS said such a course did not fpe the Council an opportunity of discussing the ques- tion. It might not accord with the feeling of some naetnbers of the Council. He would move the suspen- sion of the standing orders that they might discuss the question fully. • The MAyon • You can discuss it as much as you like now. •</p>
        <p>Alderman PIERPOINT suggested that the Council I Bhould vote on the amendmeint. Councillor PEMBERTON said he would like to point "t in answer to Councillor Evans that his amendment Wonld not interfere with the granting of the £lO in- ?.rease when the matter came before the Council on a !oture occasion. He objected to automatic increases, Out he bad not a word to say against Mr. Wallington's. Councillor JAMES EVANS said that was not his point ~Lt was possible the mover might have thought Mr. Wallington was entitled to £2O increase instead of £lO. he amendment was then put and carried. Councillor LORD moved an amendment that the Commended increase for Mr. Place be not confirmed. e thought Mr. Place's salary was quite high enough, Vul he was in duty bound to say that if Mr. Walling- °n was only worth £lO increase, Mr. Place was not Worth £2O increase. Councillor. PEMBERTON seconded. The statements Inade as to Mr. Place's salary were ,rather misleading and it was really .L:213 a year. He had £l4O as market I</p>
        <p>inspector and £73. as inspector of weights and measures. That was a nice living wage, and was a very good salary indeed. He also stated that duties were not so great, as the market was only open for two days in the week. Alderman HUTCHINSON : Pooh! Cotutoiilor PEMBERTON : Four, did you say? The MAYox: No; he's only troubled with a cough. (Laughter). Councillor PEMBERTON said many of their leaeling tradesmen would find a great difficulty in clearing *4 ss. a week.</p>
        <p>Councillor Asttcnorr supported the amendment. Councillor P. PBACOCK supported the recommenda- tion, pointing out that they should recognize the fact that Mr. Place had served them well for 38 years, and that they had had the best of his life working six days a week and not taking four days off in every six as Councillor Pemberton had stated.</p>
        <p>dounoillor PEMBERTON rose on a point of order, and said he did not say the market officials had four days off a week, but that the market was only open two days a week. _</p>
        <p>Councillor STOTT also supported the amendment. In these days, he said, when men were alleged ix) be too old at 40, it seemed out of place to apply for a rise at 70.</p>
        <p>Councillor ARTHUR PEACOCK supported the recom- mendation' and urged that the average rises given to lir:Place did not equal .an annual increase of £4 a year</p>
        <p>Alderman BENNETT said he did not think they should discuss these squalid, little, pettifogging details. He thought a thoroughly clear case had been made out for the increase. It was a monstrous thing that a man could tivactically pass his life in their service and be responsible for a flourishing concern like the market and yet for the Council to grudge him a twopence-half- penny increase. Alderman HUTCHINSON replied briefly . The amendment was lost by 17 votes to 11,the recom- mended increase being consequently granted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>Fels-Naptha</head>
        <p>Fels-Naptha</p>
        <p>Now and then a woman boils her Wash; her grandmother did.</p>
        <p>Why don't she wear a poke bonnet; her grandmother did? The modern way is to go by knowledge; go by the book. Feis-Naptha 39 Wilson street London E C</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <p>, -, i . - , -- ....... -.........-. - .... -- • . . ?.</p>
        <p>MR. W. R. WILSON.</p>
        <p>The Gas Committee recommended that the salary of the Assistant Gas" Engineer, Mr. Walter R. Wilson be increased from £l5O to £l7O per annum. In proposing the confirmation of the committee's minutes, the Deputy Mayor said that Mr. Wilson had been with them for about two years. He was an ex- ceptionally good man, and the committee had never had any trouble with him. Moreover, what spoke more highly for him, they had not had a single hitch with the'men since he came.</p>
        <p>The increase was agreed to. MR. THOMAS LONGDIIN".</p>
        <p>When the minutes of the General Purposes Committee came up for confirmation there was a discussion on the proposal to advance the salary of the Borough Surveyor, Mr. Thomas Longdin, from .£5OO to £6OO per annum. The Mxvoß moved the confirmation of the resolu- tionland Mr. Longdin had been in the service of the Corporation for many years, and it was a long time since he had an increase of salary. He had had many extra duties to perform,and the committee thought it better to recommend an increased salary instead of giving him gratuities. He Was one of the best witnesses the Cor- poration had in connection with the Ship Canal Bills. and in Parliamentary work, and the manner in which he had discharged his duties had given satisfaction. He (the Mayor), thought he was fully entitled to the increase. The DEPUTY-MAYOR seconded.</p>
        <p>Councillor LORD objected to head officials' salaries being increased by leaps and bounds. The present was not an opportune time for putting on £lOO a year. He moved, as an amendment, that the resolution be not confirmed. He had canvassed a lot of principal shop- keepers, —rithout using any infleence, and 20 to one were dead against tie increase. Councillor ARTHUR PEACOCK: They don't know his ability. ___</p>
        <p>Counctllor STOTT seconded, and said he had made in- quiries, and had found that Mr. Longdin's salary was above the average of these occupying a similar position. He did not object to a Corporation paying more than the average rate of wages to all employees, but if they wore going to set a standard for officials' salaries he thought in all fairness there should be such a standard for the wages of other employees. He could refer them to Corporations whese lowest rate of pay for employees was 6d. per hour. If the Council wanted to be generous and had £lOO to spare he would suggest that they selected twenty men, working under the Borough Surveyor, who received less than'...£l per week and give them an increase of 2s.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR: We are discussing the Borough Sur- veyor's salary and not the workmen's wages. .</p>
        <p>Councillor STOTT said he only wished to emphasise the inconsistency of increasing the salaries of men who were already receiving a fair anionnt for the duties they performed. The MAYOR said he had obtained the following in- formation with reference to the amounts paid to Borough Surveyors in other towns :—Barrow-in-Furness, popula- tion 57,586, salary £4OO to be £5OO in Jana. 1904. and two increments of £5O a year to £600; Birkenhead, 110,9 26, £750 ; Bootle, 58,556, £4OO, under considera- tion £500; Burnley 97.043, £500; Burton-upon-Trent, 50,386. £650, rising by £5O per annum to £750 ; Coventry, 69,877, £750; Cheltenham, 49,439, £600; Halifax, 104,936, £600; Hanley, 31,5922 £5OO ; Hudders- field, 95,017, £700; Ipswich, 66,630, £600; Middlesboro' 91,302, £500; Newport, 67,290, £500; Northampton, 87.621, £6OO • Reading, 72,217, £6OO ; Rochdale, 83,114, £600; Rotherham, 51,349, £275, £5O as waterworks manager ; Southampton, 104,911; £6OO ; St. Helens, 81,410,- £ 700 ; Wigan. 60,770, £500; Wolverhampt on, 94.187, £600; Yor, 77,914, £6OO. Councillor GOUGH supported the amendment and said the present was not an opportune time to increase salaries, as there were something like 500 people out of work in Warrington. Since 1890 the salaries of the staff at the Borough Surveyor's office had increased from £7OO to £l6OO, but the increase in the population of the borough during that period had been only 12,000. The MAYOR pointed out that the area of the borough had been considerably extended. Councillor GouGli said another man had been called in to repair the roads after the introduction of the tram metals. He did not want to make any remarks about the value of their Surveyor but he thought that if he were to ailptv elsewhere for another situation he would not get haft as much money as he was getting now. (Cries of "Oh!"" Oh !" and laughter.) Alderman PIERPOINT said he was sure every member of the Council would acquit him of any idea Of extrava- gance in regard to officials' salaries. What they had to consider was whether" the labourer was worthy of his hire." (Hear, hear.) Mr. Longdin had been in the ser- vice of the Corporation for between 25 and 30 years, and during the whole of that time he had done his work as he ought to have done it and never had the voice of complaint been raised against him. Whatever he (Alderman Pierpoint)might have said against officials— and he was told he had said a great deal that he ought not to have said—he had never had occasion to call into question the bona-fides of Mr. Longdin. (Hear, hear.) He had always done his work thoroughly, and to the entire satisfaction of his committee, and he (the speaker) believed from the bottom of his heart that he had done it in the best interests of Warrington. During the past few years when the conduct of public men and officials had been questioned they had never had the slightest breath of suspicion raised against Mr Longdin. liHe ha a great deal of sympathy with the mover of the amend ent, as he agreed that £lOO was a large increase. He sugbested that the amendment should be withdrawn in favour of a proposition that the increase be £4O per annum. That, he thought, would be passed witho4t an adverse vote.</p>
        <p>CouncilfOr•Donns pointed out that on the minutes of I the Education Committee, which had been confirmed that night, was a resolution to the effect that the Finance Committee should consider what should be paid to the Surveyor for his services to the Education Com- mittee. It seemed to him that the whole question might be deferred until the next meeting. The MAYOR: You cannot do that. Give him the £lOO from this Council and he will be satisfied. Councillor THORNTON said he was not favourable to small wages when a man was worth more, but still they must take into consideration that the ratepayers were strongly opposed to the increase. In the Surveyor's department in 1890 the grosssalaries of the officials were £782 2s.' and in 1891 the census of the population was 55,126. In 1901 they bad .£1,508 45., anth.with the £lOO rise as suggested, £1,608. Without the rise there would be a lift of £726 25.. and with the rise £826 2s. The population was now estimated at 67,000, so that the increase upon 1890 was 11,874. Therefore, with the £lOO advance, they would be paying more for the 11,873 people than they did for the 55.126. Those facts were taken from the official year book of the Corporation. In the department in 1890 they were five persons, now there were eleven. The MAYOR said when the Borough Surveyor was appointed, in response to an advertisement in 1878, architectural work was not included in the duties then set out, but he had carried out the works of the market roofs, fire engine station, hospitals, and all other work under the Corporation with the exception of the police buildings, technical schools and baths ; the works for which he had acted as architect having amounted in all to close on £lOO.OOO, all which, if an outside architect had been.engaged, would have meant £5,000 as fees, &amp;c. The Borough Surveyor had also introduced the present method of transmitting the excreta from the intercept- ing depots to the works at Longford, which system the very first year reduced the cost of removal of excreta after interest on cost of plant and buildings had been paid, £2,000. This system was introduced in 1885, and was still in everyday use, which during the 20 years it had been in service had been a very considerable saving to the Corporation. Councillor PETER PEACOCK WOUN prefer the Council increasing the Surveyor's salary by £lOO. When Mr. Longdin received his last advance in 1892 the assess- ment of the borough was £168,840. Last year it was £250,136. After all these years of service they ought to encourage him by giving him an increase of salary especially as he would' not enjoy a pension upon his retirement. . -</p>
        <p>Councillor PEMBERTON supported the amendment and contended no private firms would pay their officials like the Warrington Corporation did. Councillor PETER PEACOCR : Yes they do. Some in this chamber are receiving as much. Councillor PEMBERTON said Mr. Longdin had a lot of special work taken from him. At one time he had to inspect buildings ; now there is a building inspector. With such trade depression this was not a time to</p>
        <p>increase salaries, and the whole of the officials ,salaries put together came to is. 6d. in the R,and that was a big amount out of 7s. 4d.</p>
        <p>Councillor 'WAG-INN was surprised that the Councit should pass the smaller salaries without demur and yet object to increase Mr. Longdin's salary, especially as he was one of the most responsible servants of the Cor- poration. It had been proved by the list which had been read that night that they were not paying Mr. Longdin what was being paid in other towns for similar work. They had been told that private firms would riot advance their servants like the servants of the Corpora- tion were being advanced, but he thought a servant who was entitled to more wages would say to a private firm: "I won't stop with you. If lam worth more I will get it somewhere else. He agreed that that was an inopportune time to raise salaries, but they must not, forget that Mr. Longdin was one of their olden servants and an old man, and if he broke down to-morrow he would have no pension. He would not like to see their Borough Surveyor have to apply for a publio subscription to "help him out of a hole." The voting was al follows:— FOR. THH AMENDMENT.</p>
        <p>Councillors Ashcroft. Gough. Barton. Stott. Thornton. Horrocks. Lord. Duncan 9. Pemberton. AGAINST. Aldermen Fairclough. Davies. Smethurst. Wilson. Francomb. Peter Peacock,</p>
        <p>Hutchinson. Arthur Peacock. Bennett. Maginn. Councillors Hulse. Rogers. Wilkinson. The Mayor 15</p>
        <p>Robert</p>
        <p>Alderman PIERPOINT then moved a further amend-. men* that the salary be increased by £5O instead of by £lOO. He said no one had a more profound appreciation of Mr. Longdin's work and his abilities than he had, but 'after all they must) study economy at times, and he was bound to say that the advance of £lOO was the largest proportionate advance granted by the Council that night. He asked diem to consider qniatly and seriously whether they would not be doing justice to M. Longdin by marking their sense of appreciation of his serviees by an increase of £59 as they would by £lOO. The Corporation had paid him a salary that should have enabled him to lay aside a sufficient sum in lieu of a pension. Councillor CRA.IK seconded.</p>
        <p>The MAYOR said he thought they had discussed the question sufficiently, and that they might vote on the amendment without further delay. Personally he would prefer Mr. Longdin having the £lOO direct instead of £5O and something from the Education Committee and the Rivers Committee for his special services. If the Council thought in future years that Mr. Longdin was not worth £6OO they could reduce the salary. Only Alderman Pierpoint and Councillors Burton Duncan, James Evans, Craik, Thornton, Pemberton, and Gough voted for the amendment which was therefore lost.</p>
        <p>The committee's proposal to increase the salary by £lOO was then confirmed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>WARRINGTON PRIVATE STREET WORKS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON PRIVATE STREET WORKS.</p>
        <p>COIHICiIIOP Gough and the Local Government Board Inspector. OPPOSITION AT AN INQUIRY.</p>
        <p>At the Town Hall on Tuesday, Mr. H. Ross Hooper, M.A., M.I.C.E. conducted an inquiry on behalf of the Local Government Board into the application of the Corporation for a loan of £12,296 for works of private streets improvement under the Private Street Works Act, 1892. The officials in attendance were the Town Clerk (Mr. J. Lyon Whittle), Mr. Stanley Wilson (deputy town clerk), Mr. T. Longdin (borough surveyor) and Mr. J. Fairhurst (borough treasurer). Alderman Smethurst and Councillor Gough were also present. At the outseti Mr. Fairhurst pointed out that the rateable value of the borough was £273,113 and the out- standing debt was £1.122,974. The Council desired the repayment of the loan asked for that morning to be extended over a period of ten years. The INSPECTOR asked if there was any objection to the application. _</p>
        <p>Councillor GOUGH : r would like to know what the works are which it is intended to carry out. The INSPECTOR: I cannot guarantee to explain every- thing in the application, and I do not undertake to explain to everyone here the details of the application. If you will read the notice of the inquiry you will see the reason for the making of the application. Councillor GOUGH : I Cannot make any objection to the loan unless I know what the application for the money is for The INSPECTOR: The notice states the reason of the application. If you do not understand what the inquiry is being held for, I cannot see what your objection can be. ?our objection must be based upon something. Councillor GOUGH: I am a ratepayer of the borough. The INSPECTOR: Do you object? Councillor GOUGH : I want to know wliat the works are which are referred to in the notice of the inquiry. The INSPECTOR: I want to know whether you object or not.</p>
        <p>Councillor GOUGH : I will make an objection if the money is for certain streets. The INSPECTOR. I have nothing to do with that. Councillor GOUGH : If the money is to carry on the work in the principal streets I shall not make the slightest objection. -</p>
        <p>the INSPECTOR: I cannot hear any objection based upon a general principle. Objections must be specific. The Tows CLERK : I submit, with respect, that Mr. Gough must know the reason of the inquiry. Alderman SMETFIURST expressed surprise that Coun- cillor Gough should come there and say he did not know what the money was for. They originally gave three years to owners of property in which to repay the loans for private street works, but on account of loans not having been repaid, the Corporation was thousands iof pounds n debt. That was the reason they asked for a longer period than three years in which property owners could pay the loans. In face of the fact that it was only decided to apply for the loan after the matter had been carefully considered bythe Town Coun- cil. he was very Much surprised that Councillor Gough should come there to say he did net know what the money was required for. The Tows CLERK said the resolution of the Council with regard to the application was passed at a meeting at which Councillor Gough was presant. Councillor Gough voted for the resolution. Councillor GOUGH : I would like to ask whether any of these people— The INSPECTOR: lam not going to enter into any discussion. I will give you permission later to ask any question, but you may not cross-examine any witness until I have given you permission to do so. Evidtnce was then given in regaid to the works already completed and on which about £6,000 had been expended.</p>
        <p>At the Close Councillor Gough was asked if he wished to address any questions to the inspector. Councillor GouGH: I have been at many inquiries but never, when evidence was being given, was I not allowed to ask any questions. The INSPECTOR: lam conducting this inquiry. If you have any objection to make,kindly refer it to me. Councillor GOtIGH : I prefer to make what objec- tions I have to make, in writing to the Local Govern- ment Board.</p>
        <p>The INSPECTOR: Then you have nothing to say a this inquiry? Councillor GOUGH : You refused to allow me to question the witnesses, _</p>
        <p>The INSPECTOR: I invite you to ask any question of me first. If you will address any remarks you have to make to me, I will then consider the advisability of cross-examining any witness. It must first be addressed .to me.</p>
        <p>Councillor GOUGH : What observations I have to make, Twill make in writing to the Local Government, Board.</p>
        <p>The details of the second part of the application, Ivhich was in respect of twelve streets and passages about to be paved,were then explained. No further objections were offered and the inquiry concluded.</p>
