      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>SOCIAL SERMONETTES FOR WORKING MEN,</head>
        <p>SOCIAL SERMONETTES FOR WORKING MEN,</p>
        <p>BY JOHN TAYLOR. Author of "Search, Lights on Land, Labour,</p>
        <p>ana.Social Problems.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>TORY RECKONING DAY.</head>
        <p>THE TORY RECKONING DAY.</p>
        <p>"So comes a reckoning when the banquet's o'er, The dreadful reckoning, and men smile no more." _ —Gay.</p>
        <p>When a gay and festive fool wakes up the morning after a spree; and examines himself in the looking glass and finds that he has'got two lovely black eyes, and bruises and stipking plaster in abundance, and takes stock of his emptrpurse, and torn clothes, and his splitting headache, he comes to the conclusion that he must have had a high old time the night before. Where he went, or into whose hands he fell, or what he did, or who relieved him of his money, or how he found his waylhomei at last he has not the remotest idea in the world, but judging from the damages and losses he sus- tained,he comforts himself with the thought that some other fellow must have got it pretty hot in last night's scrimmages. Whenever I meet with a wanton reck- less simpleton of this typo mysympathies are invariably with "the other fellow,' who has to bear the conse- quences of his neighbour's drunken spree. Somebody's head has to get broken, and somebody's pocket has to be lightened by such ructions as these, and Tam always sorry for the innocent looker-on, who gets bounced,and bumped, and•plundered through no fault of his own. I do not care how much money a crazy spendthrift flings away so long as he is only getting rid or his own cash. I do not care how many headaches or heartaches he causes so long as he bears them all himself. But if he flings my money away and inflicts losses and misery on me it is another matter, and I shall have something to say about it. .7 John Bull is just waking up from a long drunken spree, in which he has lost his cash, and injured his credit, and got handsomely beaten by the Mad Mullah and the Boers, and it is about time our Tory rulers were put in the pillory to answer for their high crimes and mis- demeanours.</p>
        <p>1.-THE TORY RECKONING DAY REVEALS TO "US THE DISGUSTING SELFISHNESS OF ITS</p>
        <p>PARTY LEADERS.</p>
        <p>We sneer at our Yankee friends for giving" the spoils to the victors" after every election, and divid- ing the best paid berths in the country among them- selves and their supporters. But the Tories do worse than this. They gave the best paid and most respon- sible offices in the State to those noblemen who have given most money to the party funds and to their own kith and kin. For the first time in our, history they have introduced a system of government by families, and when any Minister muddles, and messes, and ruins one office they transfer him to another to keep his salary going on. We pay big salaries to enable us to get able and competent statesmen to look after our affairs. We give about one hundred pounds a week to our principal Cabinet Ministers, and we have a right to expect that these well-paid berths should be given to men of wide experience, and proved capacity, and unflinching honesty. But the Tories have given these offices to their sons, and sons-in-law, and nephews, and poor relations. Joseph Chamberlain captured a salary of £5,000 a year at the Colonial Office for himself. His son, Austen, captured £2,000 a year at the, Treasury. His faithful henchman, Jesse Collings, got £1,500 a year as his share of .the plunder. and his friend Powell Williams got 11,500 a year at the War Office. Lord Salisbury took £5,000 a year as Foreign Secre- tary. His son-in-law, Earl Selbome, got £4,500, and his bon, Lord Cranborne, got £1,500 a year. His nephew, Mr. A. J. Balfonr, got £5,000 a year, and Mr. G. W. Balfour got £4425 a year as Chief Secretary for Ireland. If these young :sprigs of nobility were earning their money I would not grumble, but they are keeping better men out of office. This disgraceful Nepotism that looks after the nephews, and younger sons, and poor relations of our aristocratic families is one of the perils of the time. Our Army, and our Navy, and our Civil Service are over-run by a horde of hungry tax eaters, whose only claim to distinction is that they are the sons of their fathers. We never get value for our money by this arrangement. We need not ask whether a candidate for office has got any ability, or brains, or,character. We only need look at his certificate of birth, and if he was born all right he will do. IL—THE RECKONING DAY SHOWS HOW</p>
        <p>RELIGIOUSLY THE TORIES LOOK AFTER THEIR FRIENDS. -</p>
        <p>They not only reward subscribers to their party funds and find snug births for themselves and their able-bodied relations, but they give sops, and doles, and bribes to their friends and supporters throughout the country. The great landowners, and the -Church parsons, and the publicans, and the officers in the Army and Navy are overwhelmingly Tory. They can always be relied upon to vote according to their own interests, whatever may become of their country, so these men are always • under the wing of the Tory party, and basking in its smiles and favours. Lord George Hamilton in one of his speeches said: • "It is to safeguard and protect the interests of our friends, not only when we are in office, but even in the contingency of our being out, that we have acted throughout." Lord George Hamilton not only knows what he is talking about, but he practices what he preaches. He comes of a long line of titled tax-eaters who acquired the nick-name of "the hungry Hamiltons," because they were always ready to swallow any good thing that came in their way. He has quartered himself on the nation as "a glorified pauper." When he is in office he gets ,W,OOO a year, and when he is out of office he has a pension of £2,000 a year. He looks after himself, and he advises his Tory friends to go and do likewise. It is quite touching to observe the interest that the 'Primrose dames and Tory candidates display in the welfare of the working man at election times. I won- der whether they lavish all their blarney on him forhis 'benefit, or for their own. The police tell me that when the public-houses close at night, and a tipsy boozer is turned out with some money left in his pocket, he can always find somebody to help him on his way home. But these benevolent, disinterested friends often relieve 'him of his spare cash before they leave him to his fate. Our Tory rulers have been doing this kind of thing ever since they came into office. In 1896 they took us home, and relieved us of a million and a half a year, and gave it to the landowners- •under !the Agricultuarl Rating PAct. In 1897 they trotted us out again, and did us out of half a million a year,•under the Voluntary Schools Act, for the relief of our squires and parsons. 1n1899 they took us another little ta-ta, and robbed us of three-quarters of a million a year for the benefit of the Irish land- owners. In 1899 they were at the same old game, and they took £lOO,OOO a year out of our pockets for the Church parsons under the Clerical Tithes Act. Their latest bribe is a gift of twelve millions to the Irish landowners under the Land Purchase Act. So you see they have been safeguarding and protect- ing the interests of their friends the landowners, and the parsons, ever since they came into office, and they will carry out this policy of plundering and thieving to the end of the chapter. But you must remember that all these doles, and sops. and bribes to landoxners, and country squires, and Church parsons come out of .your pockets and mine. The less these favoured and privileged people pay to the State the more you and I have got to pay. And when the Government increases the Army and Navy ex- penditure at the bidding of its supporters we have to bear our share of the burden. Our friend the publican has received no bribes or , doles yet, though he has certainly earned a share of the swag. But he has got a promise of good things to come. The Tories have promised him that they will alter the law for his special benefit, and prevent the; magistrates from taking away his liceace. even if they, deem it unnecessary, without giving him full corn- I pensation. This system of government by bribery and comm. tion is no new thing under the Tory rules. It is as old as Toryism itself, but it is a costly and disastrous system for the unlucky taxpayer, and it saps all the honour, and virtue, and manhood out of the nation. It I takes no notice of the wrongs and rights of things. It is destrnctive of all principle, and character, and man- ' hood. It leads men to believe that they may safely , look after themselves, and let the devil take ti2e hinder- I most,. The pions Yankee editor hits off this selfish I spirit when he sings : 'Tic. not by principles nor men My prudent course is steadied. I scent, what nays the best. and then Go in for it hold-headed. 111.—THE TORY RECKONING DAY MAKES OUR REPUTATION LOOK VERY SEEDY. Turn where vou will, and you will find the wreck marks of the last eight years of Tory rule. Our Army has been hnmiliated and beaten again and again both by the savagee of Somaliland and by the Boers in South Africa. Our reputation has been tarnished. Our credit has been shaken. Our money has been recklessly meandered. Our War Office is the lariahing stock of the world for crass stupidity end muddle-headed in- capacity in the nvinsigement of the South African war. Our enormous e‘zponditure and our reckless extravag- ance have aeriously shaken our credit and-involved us In heavy financial losses. When Mr. Gladstone was in office. and we were following a policy of peace. re- trenchment and reform our credit stood high, and our Consols were sold on the Stock Exchange at &lt;£ll4 for each £lOO. When the Tories were in nower, and we had a bad attack of war fever, and we indulged in reckless borrow- ,</p>
        <p>ing, and we went on warlike expeditions to the ends of the earth, our credit began to melt away, and our Con- sols fell to £BB on each £lOO. This is a drop of 26 per cent., and the man who bought at the highest price and was compelled to sell at the lowest would lose about one quarter of his original capital. This is a big price to pay for militant Toryism but if you will have Jingoism, and Imperialism, arid aristocratic rulers you must pay for them. We are paying sweetly now for our folly and wickedness, but the burden falls, like the rain on the just as well as the unjust. Let us see what our mischievous policy is costing us. Under the Liberal Government in 1894 our normal expenditure was only £86,000,000. Inl9ol, under a Tory Government it had grown to £177,700.000. So that in seven years we more than doubled our ordinary expenditure, and our taxes multiplied and grew. We added -sevenpence in the pound to our income tax. We put an extra shilling on each barrel of beer, and sixpence on every gallon of spirits, and fourpence per pound on our tobacco, and sixpence on our cigars, and twopence on our tea, and a half-penny on our sugar. Then we taxed our corn for a year and raised the price of our bread. I Now all these new taxes and war-like expeditions seriously increase the cost of living and reduce the spending power of our money. Twenty-five shillings would buy as much food seven or eight years ago as thirty shillings would buy now. So that the man who 113 s had a standinc, wage of thirty shilling all the time has pradtically had his wages reduced by five shillings per week owing to the increased cost of living. One of the most serious charges I bring against our Tory rulers is that they never pay for their own folly- and rascality. They borrow the money from a confid- ing public, and then saddle the cost of most of their devilment on an innocent posterity. They squandered £249,000,000 on the South African war, but they only took £76,000,000 out of the knaves and fools who dragged us into it. They added £159,000,000 to the National ' Debt, and took about £14,000,000 out of the Sinking Fund. In other words, they paid only six shillings inl the pound towards the cost of the war, and they left, the other fourteen to be paid by their children's chil- dren. But there will be trouble about these huge Tory war debts one of these days, for our children will not go on cheerfully paying our debts for ever. If I rightly read the signs of the times, we are in for a lively reckoning day with the strongest and maddest Government of modern times. Messrs. Balfour, Cham- berlain, and Co. will have to be told how they sent our Tommies to the war in brown paper boots, and armed them with swords that would cut butter if it were red hot, and with guns that were more likely to kill the!man that fired them thanthe man they were aimed at. They will have to be told in pretty plain English how they got into power by lying promi.es and false pretences, and how they gave away millions of our hard-earnKl money in doles, and sops, and rates in aid to their political supporters, the lanc.lords and the par- sons. They must be told to their faces how they watered down our educational system, and destroyed our School 'Boards, and handed over the children of millions of Nonconformists to the tender mercies of the priest and the parson. The passive resisters will becomevery active resisters when the electioncamPaign begins in earnest, and you can take it from me that -there will be wigs on the green. 1111 The Tories have ruled this country for fifteen out of the last eighteen years, and if a Cabinet of lunatics from Colney Hatch could rule us with more criminal recklessness, or crass stupidity, or muddle-headed incapacity,l should be very much surprised. So far as the Government is concerned the reckoning day will not be a very merry time, for the night's debauch will not bear the morning's reflection.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, February 6, 1904</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, February 6, 1904</p>
        <p>day were spent in lectures, either public or private, two months at Bristol, on a visit to his father-in-law.; and yet, before its expiration, he had sent a printed copy of the book to Dr. Franklin. The monumental work was printed (like all his earlier publications) by our townsman, William Eyres, and any who are curious to form some notion of its quality, may see it carefully preserved at theMuseurn and the Old Academy. He adds that, notwithstanding the rapidity of its pro- duction, none of his "hasty performances" were better received than his, "the most hasty of them all," and aptly adds that his object "was not to acquire the character of a fine writer, but of a useful one," and that he could "truly say that gain was never the chief object of arty of his publications, j and that several of them ware written with the prospect of certain loss." They brought him, ere he left the town, however, the degree of Doctor of Laws from the University of Edinburgh, and made him a Fellow of the Royal Society. But, singularly happy" as his life amongst US was, he states that he was most laboriously employed, for nothing more titan a bare subsistence," and this we can well believe when we remember that his salary was only a hundred pounds a year, with a house, and such small perquisites as he could make from a few boarders at the lavish fee of fifteen pounds. More- over, "the academy did not flourish" his wife's health was not satisfactory; it was not possible, as he admits with charming frankness, " even living with the greatest frugality, to make any provisioa for a family (a daughter had been born to him in Warrington); and so, although the laborious part of his work was over, and he tells U 3 that "nothing could be more agreeable to him at. the time than the whole of his employment," in 1767, he "listened to an invitation to take charge of the c)ngregation of Mill Hill Chapel (near the site ot the new Priestley statue), Leeds. I have not space to follnw him through his six more " very happy (and laborious) years"; to speak of his continuous studies and researches; of the publication of his " History and. present State of Discoveries relating to Vision, Light and Colours," and his " Experiments and Observations on different kinds of Air " ; or how he presently obtained the post of Librarian to the Earl of Shelburne, with a salary of two hundred and fifty port ads per annum, a housa to live in, and a certainty for life in case of the Earl's death ; how, at this time, he travelled largely, and made a h )st of influential friends and saw " a great variety of characters, but was " not at all fascinatedl with that mode of life" ; how, ultimately, as " he pesceived evident marks of dissatisfaction, though he never understood the cause of it," he left his Lord- ship's service, but received the stipulated annual pen- sion of a hundred and fifty pounds, and resumed his studies in the midst of a congenial company of fr ends at Birmingham; how, thinking that he sympathized with the French Revolution, a raging mob destroyed his church, his books, snd philosophical appliances, and set his house on fire; or how the name " On which delighted Science loved to dwell Became the bandied theme of hooting crowds ; and how he wrote a sermon, subsequently read amid the ruins of his home, upon the text, "Father, forgive them, for they know not what they do ;" how, after vainly seeking an asylum at Hackney, and discover- ing that his former friends began to hold aloof from him, he emigrated to America in April, 1791; and how, upon the 6th of February, tea years later, after carefully dictating some corrections in his latest pamphlets, he quietly observed: "That is right, I have how done," and died, as he had lived, calm cheerful, brave, magnanimous—" without doubt, a great, learned, p,nd understanding man." 'I have beside me as I write a list of not less than 108 books' and pamphlets written by Dr. Priestley— five' On General Philosophy," six "On Pneumatic Chemistry," seven "On Metaphysics," six "On Civil Liberty," nine "On Religious Liberty," eight "On Ecclesiastical History," sixteen "On the Evidences of the Christian Revelation," ten "Tracts in Defence of Unitarianism," thirty-three works of "Miscellaneous Theology," and eight of " Miscellaneous Literature." I recollect how he " could truly say that he always considered the office of a Christian minister as the most honourable of any on earth, and. in the studies proper to lit always took the greatest pleasure," P reflect how he anticipated Bentham's fain. cons phrase, "The greatest happiness of the greatest number ;" and I contend that, under any or all of these broad aspects, he might fairly claim our recognition: But I mainly think of him as the great natural philosopher 'who read the riddle of the shining air," discovered oxygen, and half a dozen minor gases,placed chemistry upon a new foundation, land in doing so, bequeathed life, health, and happi- ness to millions yet unborn ; and very proudly I re- member that he lived in Warrington and walked its winding streets for six great years ; and very humbly loving it and him, would lay my little tribute at his feet to-day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>JOSEPH PRIESTLEY. A RA:TROiPECT AND AN APPRECIATION. [By Artiti-annett].</head>
        <p>JOSEPH PRIESTLEY. A RA:TROiPECT AND AN APPRECIATION. [By Artiti-annett].</p>
        <p>It has sometimes been alleged that those who are seeking to keep green in Warrington the memory of its illustrious dead are tempted to exaggerate their greatness. There may be something in the gibe, but crreatness is not so abundant, either in the town or ;late, that we can well afford to overlook or to ignore it. As Richard Le Gallienne says:</p>
        <p>"The age grows Lilliput ; the great men go," and, seen at any rate through Memory's spectacles, and coloured with</p>
        <p>"The tender tint which only time can give," the" Warrington Worthies" of a former age appear to me, at least, to overtop the bulk of one's con- temporaries, whether in exalted aim or intel- lectual achievement. If few- of them are giants, by the side of them, too many of the men whom we extol to-day look curi- ously like pigmies. And if Dr. Taylor and the Aikin family, and Wakefield,Enfield, and old William Eyres, are not as great as Hooker or as Caxton say,at least they are deserving of our warm regard and our affectionate remembrance. If we cannot have the Himalayas, shall we shut our eyes to Snowdon and Skiddaw ? And, whatever may be said of may of them, one figure in the little group who won for War- rington its proud old name, "the Athens of England," is admittedly among the giants,and may well be num- bered in the ranks of those whose names the world (apart from Warrington) is not disppsed to willingly let die.</p>
        <p>I refer, of cur, to J03.3110'1. Priastle7," pitriot,and saint, and sage " not less than chemist, preacher, politician. He has had to wait for his due recogni- tion,lbut, as Mrs. Barbauld sang in 1702, he could well afford</p>
        <p>" To give large credit for what debt of fame His country owed him," and upon the hundredth anniversary of his death, not Warrington alone, but Leeds, and Birmingham, and England, and America, and every civilized commun- ity, is glad to pay the debt. And we should, indeed, be very proud to know that Priestley lived in that old house within the gates of the enlarged Academy on which to-day a bronze memorial will be reverently placed; and taught in the forgotten rooms which stood across the site of the existing street, as well as hr a short time in the older building at Bridge Foot; and dwelt among us for six "singularly happy" years. To some of us, such facts as these are not without their use.</p>
        <p>Shades of departed greatness, some still see, With reverent eyes, your bites as they fare About their daily business silently, And for your teaching have not ceased to care. Come to our help! The town has sordid grown, Its dearest memories die from day to day; The civic heart, at times, seems turned to stone, And the sole question left 113--" Will it pay? A.nd it is very meet that, in the roar and hubbub of our common life, the endless struggle for mere wealth, and power, and place, and pleasure, we should leave the all-prevailing littleness (upon occasion),and turn quietly aside for a few moments to acknowledge gratefully our debt to the remembered dead. The life of Dr. Priestley (busy and eventful as it was) may be epitomized in a few words. Born at Fieldhead, near Leeds, on the 13th of March, 1733 he lost his mother at the age of seven, 'and, when nine years old, was adopted by an aunt named. Mrs. Keighley, who gave him a good education, cul- minating at the Daventry Academy, to whtch he went in 1752, remaining there till 1755. A diligent and thoughtful student, with a serious cast of mind, he had been early destined for the ministry. He tells us, in his "Memoirs," that by his residence at Daventry he" greatly improved that disposition to piety which" he "brought to the academy, and freed it from the rigour with which it had been tinctured " ' • that thanks to Dr. Hartley's " Observations of Man," he saw reason to embrace what is generally called the heterodox side of almost every question; - that there he composed the first copy of his "Institutes of Natural and Revealed Religion " ; and that he never lost sight of the great object of his studies, "which was the duties of a Christian minister" ; • but that he was "much discouraged" by an impediment in his speech which he never absolutely overcame. In 1755 he was appointed minister to a small congregation at Needham Market, in Suffolk. We are told that the clergyman in the "Deserted Village" was passing rich with forty pounds a year." Priestley (promised forty) had to be content with thirty, and even this in consequence of his advanced opinions, gradually fell off; and, but for the most wel- eome help of various friends, he says that he does not believe he could have subsisted.</p>
        <p>However, he remained three years at Needham, going thence to Nantwich, where he "found a good-natured, friendly people," with whom he " lived " another " three years very happily," eking out his scanty salary by opening a school. This seems to have been a great success,for he tells us that he was able to buy some books and philosophical apparatus, and appears to. have been making a substantial reputation. He stayed at Nantwich until 1761, when, upon the death of Dr. Taylor (author of the famous Hebrew Concordance) he was invited to succeed him at the Warrington Academy in the somewhat incongruous department of Polite Literature, and, although his school "promised to be more gainful to him," he assented, thinking that his employment at Warrington would be more liberal and less painful,and would also be the means of extending his connections.</p>
        <p>And so he came to the old town, and,though he had not any particular fondness for the studies relating to his profession, he threw himself into the new work with his accustomed zeal, and began to take a new delight in it, and love 'The nursery of men for future years." " In the whole time of my being at Warrington, I was singularly happy," he says, "in the society of my fellow tutors (John Holt, Dr. Taylor, and John Aikin) and of Mr. Seddon, the minister of the place. "We drank tea together every Saturday, and our conversa- tion was equally instructive and pleasing. The tutors having sufficient society among themselves, we had itot ranch acquaintance out of the academy?: It is said that one of the amusements of the little circle was to "meditate the thankless tuse " and periodically deposit the results of their poetic labours in Mrs. Priestley's workbag, and the doctor tells us, with some pardonable pride, that Mrs. Barbauld had imformed him that it was the perusal of some verses of his that first induced her to write anything in. Terse, " so that this country is in some measure in- debted to me," he writes, "for one of the bests poets it can boast of. Several of her first poems were written when she was in my house, or occasions that occurred while she was there."</p>
        <p>It is easy to imagine the delightful coterie, where Wisdom mixed with Beauty, and to pick out several of the poems—" The Mouse's Petition," the lines" To Mrs. P-- with some drawings of birds and insects," the amusing "Inventory of ,the Furniture in Dr. Priestley's Study" for example—and to understand the admiration of the gifted damsel for the mighty thinker from her curiously prophetic lines " To Dr. Priestley," written shortly after the Birmingham Riots, and already' quoted. At Warrington, the doctor married a daughter. of Isaac Wilkinson, a Wrexham ironmaster "a woman of excellent understanding, and much improved by reading," as he quaintly says ; " of great fortitude and strength of mind, and of a temper in the highest degree affectionate and generous ; feeling strongly for others; and little for herself. Also, greatly excel- ling in every thing relating to household affairs, she entirely relieved me of all concern of that kind, which allowed me to give all my time to the prosecution of my studies, and the other duties of my station." How onerous these duties were,the"Memori" amply proves ; and it is worthy of remark that, whilst he lived in Warrington, he wrote courses of Lectures on the "Thebry of language,"on"Oratory and Criticism," on " History and 'General Policy," on the " Laws and Constitutions of England," and on the "History of England ;" also an" Essay or a Course of Liberal Education for Civil and Active Life," and some " Remirks " On Dr. Brown's plan of education, in. which, with doubtful prescience,he declared that educa- tion by the State (which Dr. Brown suggested) was " inimieal to liberty and the natural rights of parents." This nromptly led to an "Essay on Government." and, I must not fail to add the clever "Chart of Biograph*" (started at Nantwich) and the i famous" History and Pres,mt State of Electricity." The latter was the outcome of an introduction to Benjamin Franklin, who generously provided him with the necessary books for his great undertaking. He tells us how, in the course of writing it, he endeavoured to ascertain several facts which were disnnted, and that this led him by degrees into a large field of original experiments, in which he spared no expense that he could possibly furnish. That year five hours of every</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>PRIESTLEY CENTENARY CELEBRATIONS. Tablet to be Uuveiled To-day.</head>
        <p>THE PRIESTLEY CENTENARY CELEBRATIONS. Tablet to be Uuveiled To-day.</p>
        <p>Commendable efforts are being made in 'Warrington to fittingly celebrate the centenary of the death of Dr. Priestley, and at the same time do fitting honour to his memory. We need hardly remind ow readers of the great natural philosopher's connection with Warrington,beyond saying that he spent six "sing- ularly happy "and industrious years as a tutor at the Warringtpn Academy: The doctor's residence still remains in Academy street but is now in the occupation of Messrs. Richmond and Company Ltd. At eleven o'clock this morning a tablet will be unveiled on this historic old building by Dr. T. E. Thorpe, C. 8., F R.S., head of the Government Labora- tories in London and himself author of a new life of Priestley. This tablet, which is of brass, has been most artistically and tastefully designed and modelled by Mr. W. H. Sherburn, of Messrs, Richmond and Co., Ltd., at whose works it has been cast and finished. The tablet, which bears a portrait of the philosopher, is inscribed as. follows :—" Joseph Priestley, natural philosopher resided here from 1761 to 1767, as a tutor in the War- rington Academy. This tablet was erected by the Warrington Society on the centenary Of his death, 6th February, 1904." Last week there was a "Priestley night" at the Old Academy, Bridge Foot, when papers were read by Messrs. H. Stuart Page, F. G. Raddock, and W. H. Sherburn. Unfortunately the exigemcies of space prevented us reporting these as fully as they merited and we should have liked.</p>
        <p>I,lst evening, on the eve of to-day's centenary, a lecture was delivered by the Rev. F. K. Freeston on "Priestley and his Friends," a report of which we give as extensively as space and the lateness of the hour will permit, below.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>" Priestley and His Friends. Paper by the Rev. F. K. Freestoa.</head>
        <p>" Priestley and His Friends. Paper by the Rev. F. K. Freestoa.</p>
        <p>Those who attended the Warrington Acaiemy last evening to listen to a lecture by the Rev. F. K. Frees. ton, ofLondon, formerly pastor of Cairo-street Chapel, were provided with an intellectual treat. Assisted by a varied andchoice collection of slides, Mr. Freeston gave a pictorial history of the' life of Joseph Priestley. The chair was taken by Mr. F. Monks, J.P., who briefly introduced the lecturer.</p>
        <p>'The Warrington Society,said Mr. FREESTON at the outset, had honoured him with an opportunity of taking part in the Priestley centenary. He was very glad indeed to be allowed to be in the Old Academy, and to speak of the most illustrious of Warrington's worthies. He would endeavour, he said, to sketch out the briefest picture of Priestley; to place in due order the chief events in his career and the chief influence on his character; to trace the development of his thought; to glance through the lantern on the friends of his heart. Priestley was the most hated man of his day and yet he was an apostle of simplicity, of purity, of right,sof truth,and of liberty.(Hear, hear.) The early history of Priestley from his birth at Birstall in Yorkshire to his coming to Warrington Academy in 1761 as a tutor, was carefully traced. Priestley was six years in Warrington and Warrington people were proud, said the lecturer, to claim him and honour him—to honour him in that very build- ing which long ago echoed to the low tones of his hesitating voice. At Warrington, Priestley in his own words was "singularly happy," and he and his friends inspired the muse of Dr. Aikin's own daughter, Anna tatetia, afterwards Mrs. Barbauld, who became known to fame by her poems. Mr. Freeston briefly reviewed the history of Warrington Academy, !which ceased to exist 21 years after Priestley left it in 1767. Though the Warrington Academy came to an un- timely end and in that sense might be said to have failed yet its work was taken up and the Manchester College was founded in 1786. "This Academy like that' at Warrington," it was stated of Manchester College, "is 'sfounded upon the most Liberal principles and will be open to young men of all denominations and principles." The Man- chester College at Oxford was still upholding the same traditions of the Old Academy at Warrington. "If people say the Academy failed," continued the lecturer remind them that the Academy did not fail in 'one thing It opened its doors very willingly to Joseph Priestley." In detailing the many steps in Priestley's religious thought the lecturer said he started with Calvinism, de- parted to Baxterianism, then to Arminianism, Arianism</p>
        <p>and finally to Socinianism. What a bad man he must have been! (Laughter.) The excit- ing incidents in Priestley's life whilst at Birmingham were graphically told and his last years at Northumberland Town in Pennsylvania were sympatheticallyalluded to. The lecturer fittingly concluded with a warm eulogy of the Many virtues of Priestley, who in the words of Huxley, was "a heretic but yet a saint." The meeting terminated with an acknowledgment of the services of the Rev. F. K. Freeston, Mr. Frith, (the lanternist) and the Chairman.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>COBDEN CLUB LECTURER AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>COBDEN CLUB LECTURER AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>The Fiscal Question.</p>
        <p>LIBERAL MEETINGS AT BRICK-STREET AND THEWLIS-STREET.</p>
        <p>I The fiscal question has been discussed at two public meetings, held under the auspices of the Warrington Liberal Association, this week. The first meeting was held in Brick-street school on Monday evening and the second in the Workingmen's Mission, Thewlis-street on Tuesday evening. The meeting on Monday evening was presided over by Councillor James Evans, and he was supported on the platform by Mr. C. E.. Parker (presi- dent of the Assoeiation), Mr. C. Jackson Holmes (secretary), Mr. W. Farness (treasurer), Mr. Councillor Peter Peacock, Mr. Councillor Barton, Mr. A. Rathbone, Mr. E. England. The lecturer was Mr. W.A.Wilson, who has done much good work during the fiscal controversy on behalf of the Cobden Club. Tan CHAIRMAN said he was reminded that morning that that day was the 55th anniversary of the adoption of Free Trade principles in this country. (Hear, hear.) He woald content himself, notwithstanding the severe assault which was being made upon those principles, with wishing Free Trade "Many Happy Returns of the Day." (Hear, hear.) Referring to the meeting on the Coal Exchange with reference to the tariff question,the Chairman pointed out that those who took part in it were men somewhat removed from the working-classes. (Heir, hear). He (the chairman) was born before Free Trade principles were introduced and he knew one or two things of those dark days of long ago that made ' him very anxious that they should not return. (Hear, hear). Discussing the present political situation, the chairman said he had remembered a good many Parlia- ments and a good many Governments, but he never remembered a Parliament in which there were two Prime Ministers. (Hear, hear, and laughter.) We needed some good home legislation in this country. (Hear, hear.) Given a fair chance to develop the resources and conditions in this country he had no fear of the need of protection. (Hear, hear.) One of the questions that needed attention was that of mining royalties. (Hear, hear.) Whereas the mining royalty on a ton of paddled iron in Belgium was lid per toil and in Germany 2d. per ton, in England, according to Mr. Glover, of St. Helens, it was ss. Bd. Then there was the question of the land laws. (Hear, hear.) The trade of the country needed to be freed from some of the trammels which beset it on every side, and then there need be no fear of protection. If they viewed the results of protection they would find that it made rich men richer and poor men poorer. (Hear, hear.) Mr. WILSON, in prefacing his address on the fiscal question, said there were two fiscal policies before the country—one was Mr. Chamberlain's and the other Mr. Balfour's. They were antagonistic to some extent. Mr. Balfour had his eye more particularly upon foreign countries, and desired to put a ten per cent. duty on the manufactured goods of foreign countries with the idea of keeping out the manufactured goods of these countries in order to make foreigners Free Irrmders (Laughter). Mr. Balfour told them he was a Free Trader himself and he wanted other countries to be Free Traders also. In order to make them so,he said in effect give them a dose of their own medicine and then they will adopt a policy of Free Trade. (Laughter). They knew.,perfecily well that the putting of duties on imported, manufactured goods never brought Free• Trade. (Hear, hear). Mr. Chamberlain, on the other hand desired to cement the empire together by a preferential tariff on the food of the people. He told them that by doing so they would solve the agricultural problem in England, they would be able to give more wages to the workingman and solve all sorts of social questions. The lecturer proceeded to state that he desired to address himself more particu- larly to the workingman's side of the auestion. This questioa was a workingman's question. It was a bread- and-butter question as it was of the utmost importance that the workingman should get his food- at the oheapest rate. In discussing the wages question, the speaker showed that• wages were not regulated by profits. Profits might be very high and wages very low and vice-versa. In the old days of protection the prices of the necessaries of life were extremely high and the money wages of the working people were ex- tremely low. The average wages of the agricultural labourers were 15s. 6d. a week and they spent three- quarters of their wages upon the necessaries of life. If they raised the cost of the necessaries of life under a protectionist system, it would make a difference of a shilling or eighteen pence per week in the price of necessaries, and it therefore followed that he would have less to spend upon the comforts and luxuries of the home. That would cause a dealine in trade practic- ally in every industry in the country. (Hear, hear). The scheme of Mr. Chamberlain's was a tax on the food of the people, and such a tax meant a lower- ing of the purchasing power of the money of the people and consequently a slice off the wages of the workingman. (Hear, hear.) They could see the effect of protection in France, Ger- many and America. The German workman received a smaller wage than the British workman, and was not able to buy as much food with it. The as ages of a carpenter in Germany were 41d. an hour, whereas the Trades Union rate of wages for a carpenteri in London was 10d. per hour. From careful nfluiries made in America it appeared that the average wages oflimillions of workers were one d 0139 cents a day six years ago and one do! 29 cents a day now. Whilst the average had thus been reduced, the price of the necessaries of life had increased in America 40 per cent within six years. Under i Mr. Chamberlain's scheme t was clear that the taxes would press heaviest on the very poorest in the country. Protection would not solve the agricultural problem. It was tried in the old days and it miserably failed. They would have to alter the land tenure of the country to solve the problem. (Hear, hear.) They would have to give the small men an equal chance with the big men. (Hear, hear.) Furthermore they would have to deal with the question of railway freights. (Hear, hear.) British agri- culturalists only wanted an equal chance with the foreigner in the charges for carriage on the railways. The English farmer, he showed, was charged by the railway Companies a higher price for the transit, of produce, than was the Continental farmer. This was unfair to the British producer and the matter should receive the attention of Parliament.. (Hear, bear.) Referring to our position as a commercial nation, the speaker said we held a position second to none as a manufacturing nation. England was still the workshop of the world, notwithstanding what Mr. Chamberlain said. We were, however, handicapping ourselves by the old notions of mining royalties. We were run- ning in clogs when we ought to be running in running-shoes. The speaker quoted from a book by an American author who stated "Notwithstanding our political and natural advantages, the material well-being of the average artisan in England has actually increased 6-1 cents. a week more since 1856 than that of a labourer in America." rhe average Englishman was not behind the average Ameri6an. When they compared England with an old country like Germany they would see that England certainly had the advantage in regard to the condition of the working-classes, and we had gained much—perhaps not all—in consequence of our Free Trade policy. Protection was detrimental to England and it would be against the interests not only of the middle and commercial classes, but of the working- men to go back to that dark policy. In conclusion the speaker said the old friends of Free Trade had stood by us and benefitedus, audit would be , unwise to adopt the new-fangled comrades who came to t us in the guise of preference or protection. (Applause.) Mr. C. E. PARKER moved a vote of thanks to Mr. Wilson who, he said, had not appealed to the gallery or set off a lot of fireworks, but had taken a firm logical grip of the question. He (the speaker) recently wrote to some of his tanning friends in Germany, asking them for information as to the wages in the various depart- ments of the trade. He found that, on the whole, the wages were from 15 to 25 per cent, lower than the amounts paid for the corresponding work in the tanning yards in the Warring- ton neighbourhood. Another point, which was possibly of more importance, was the purchasing power of the wages. Anyone who knew the prices of necessaries of life, and especially of articles of food which were taxed on importation to Germany, wauld realize how much further money went in England where food was cheap. Mr. Parker instanced how in Germany , poorer families ate horse-meat. If Protection was ' such a good thing and Free Trade such a bad thing how was it that, when one compared two countries subjected to very similar conditions and of about the same age of development, it was found that the wages were higher and the purchasing power greater in the i Free Trade country than n the protected country? (Applause.) He did not see why, with that one fact alone in view, we should be so mad as to turn our backs on Free Trade. As far as the tanning trade was concerned, it would stand- for pro - tection. In no way could protection and preference benefit that industry. They derived their hides and materials from foreign countries, and those materials came to England as make-up cargoes. The prices, therefore, very much depended upon the facilities which their shipping gave to collect the merchandise from about 200 foreign ports. The effect of trying to divert the trade of the country from foreign to colonial ports would be to make their raw goods dearer. It was a wrong principle to try and expand their trade by in- creasing the prices of the goods which they produced. If the members of the tanning trade did not want Pro- tection he did not see why they should be handicapped</p>
        <p>by other trades having it. He proceeded to show how the leather and iron trades were very much dependent upon one another, and that if the iron trade were protected the tanninc, and other idus tries dependent upon it would be bhandicapped. He could not see any sense in the Protection scheme. It was rotten from stem to stern. Alluding to the position in Warrington, Mr. Parker said they had on the one hand a candidate who might, after his last statement on the question, be described as a "whole-hogger." It was not often their member talked politics—(laughter)— but on one of those rare occasions he declared himself to be a follower of Mr. Chamberlain. He (the speaker) was not quite sure whether all those who had been declaring themselves for Mr. Chamberlain would have done so if they had it to do again now. (Laughter and applause.) Their present member was prepared to put a tax upon food, but on the other hand they had la prospective candidate who was thoroughly sound on the fiscal and land questions. Owing to the introduction of the foolish fiscal proposals, other questions which 1 ty near their hearts had been to some extent pushed to the side, and it must be their position in the coming contest to be determined that they would not allow those important questions,which were holding down our industry and preventing its natural development, to be thrust to the side. He hoped that in the contest which was bound to come soon, each one who was present that night would stand , with earnestness and enthusiasm for free bread, free limports and free land. (Applause). Mr. W. FURNESS seconded. He also alluded to the conditions in Germany and to the opposition of the working-classes to a tax upon food. YEngland exported 60 millions more then Germany, 120 millions more thpn France, and 130 millions more than the United Stattis. That, he considered, showed that this country was not on the decline. (Hear,hear) '!Before putting the resolution, the Chairman remarked that he did not know of one single scheme that Mr. Chamberlain had mooted as an important feature of the political life'of the country, that had been carried to a very successful issue. Mr. Chamberlain, in his (the speaker's) view, was now leading the country to revolu- tion. They must mind what they were doing or they might put the clock back. (Hear, hear.) The resolution was heartily carried, and Mr. Wilson having acknowledged the compliment, the meeting con- laded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>SUCCESSFUL MEETING IN THEWLIS</head>
        <p>SUCCESSFUL MEETING IN THEWLIS</p>
        <p>STREET.</p>
        <p>Protection Fallacies Exposed.</p>
        <p>A well-attended meeting was held in the schoolroom Thewlis-street on Tuesday evening: Councillor Tinnion presided and he was accompanied on the platform by ' Messrs. H. NV. Wilson Andrew Ra,thbone, Nathan Cook • Elwell, Crosskill, and Haley. The CHAIRMAN announced that Mr. A.H.Crosileld had telegraphed from London, apologising for his non- attendance, and letters of apology had also been re- ceived from Mr. C. E. Parker,Mr, T. Birchall, J.P.,and Mr. C. Jackson Holmes. He had also to apologise for the absence of the Liberal members of the Coun- cil who were engaged on important business at the Council Chamber and unable to be present. Councillor Tinnion went on to say that he was an avowed Tree Trader. He could not understand how any working-man could be led by the specious promises of Protectionists, if he considered the facts as pre- sented by other countries, notably Germany and America where the hours of labour were longer and the spending power of their wages less than in this country. The advantages of Free Trade far outweighed any of the imaginary advantages at present put for- ward on behalf of protection. (Hear, hear.) Mr. A. W. WILSON, in speaking of the fiscal policy called Free Trade, as pursued by us for more than 50 years past, showed how great was the difference from the previous policy called "Protection." What led to this change of policy? It was not merely to set up an example for other nations to follow, though it was believed they would see that their interests lay in the direction we were going, but because our interests dictated the change and nature aided us. The condition of our people before the change was contrasted by the lecturer with their condition after it, so also was the stationary condition of our foreign trade before the change contrasted with its rapid growth immediately after the change and up to the present times. Pro- ceeding, the lecturer showed the unfairness of estimat- ing the difference at different periods by monetary values in current prices, and pointed out that judging by volume was the only fair way of drawing compari- sons. He also showed the difference of opportunities offered for trade development between old countries of comparatively small areas and few natural resources and new countries of practically unlimited areas and abundant natural resources both in variety and extent. America, he saidl which was often cited as an example of the greater presperity which attends protection than Free Trade, had prospered in spite of rather than by the aid of protection. The aggregate of individuals forming the nation were the very men or their descend- ants who were driven from our shores by the hard con- ditions of life which surrounded them here. A few lineswhich expressed the feeling of the time were quoted in support of this and which ran :</p>
        <p>"To the West, to the West, to the land of the blest, To the West,to the West,to the land of the free, &amp;c." Yet with all this, the prosperity of America, continued the speaker, was due to the poor people. Agriculture had more than any other industry contributed to the national wealth without sharing it in a proportionate degree. The system now obtaining there had favoured the growth of trade and syndicate greater than had been known to exist in Vie previous world's history and they pressed most heavily on the people, as they had been the means of raising the cost of the necessaries of life. The conditions of trade there were similar in kind as here, but were more severe on the people. The rule of wages was higher, but the purchasing power of their money a great deal lower, and that with their higher rate of wages they were not as well off as we with a lower rate. This was emphasized by reference to the higher rate of wages paid in some portions of South Africa, and which allured many men from comparatively good situations here, but who found provisions so dear that they could not live on the £5 per week. When adversity came to the American manufacturer, as times of depression are bound to succeed times of inflation. under any system of Fiscal policy, no consideration was given to the toilers. Works were shut down by scores and the employs discharged by thousands to enable the employers to work off the vast accumulations of stock whose growth the prevailing system had favoured. Even now the people were bewailing their condition and contrasting it unfavourably with that of the workers in England. (Hear, hear.) The CHAIRMAN made some further observations, in which he spoke of how the expenditure of the empire had risen under the present Government. Ten years ago the national expenditure was 95 millions, this year it was 145 millions—an increase of 50 million pounds. Apart from any fiscal question were they going to stand this? This was a growing evil. While it was to be regretted that the expenditure had gone up by leaps and bounds, at the same time it proved the very healthy condition financially of the country, that we could have such an extraordinary advance in expenditure without hearing more than a slight grumble. Had we been in the position of some countries-we should have had, if not trevolution, at any rate a very considerable amount of dissatisfaction. (Hear, hear). He hoped that after carefully reading all they possibly could on the matter, and talking it over with their friends, they would show that they were in favour of what had been a great stand-by to the empire and to them, and not go in for protection. (Hear,hear) A vote of thanks was tendered to the speaker and chairman on the proposition of Mr. Nathan Cook sec- onded by Mr. Crosskill.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>SUDDEN DEATH OF MRS. ELISHA WALLINGTON.</head>
        <p>SUDDEN DEATH OF MRS. ELISHA WALLINGTON.</p>
        <p>Many of our readers will regret to hear of the pain- fully sudden death of Mrs. Eli sha Wallington, a well- known and highly esteemed Warringtonian. The de- ceased lady,who was eighty-one years of age, resided with her !son, Mr. Thomas Wallington, at Latchford Post Office. At noon on Tuesday, apparently in her usual health, she went into the back yard. As about ten minutes elapsed and she did not return her daughter- in-law became alarmed, and on going to an outbuilding found the deceased lady lying there dead. It appeared that she had been seized with a stroke, the actual cause of death being hemorrhage of the brain. Mrs. Walling- ton was a daughter of the late Mr. William Dutton and in her early years resided opposite the old Bridewell in Irlam-street. Before her marriage she carried on the business of a dressmaker. Her husband predeceased her about fourteen years ago and she went to live with her danghter, Mrs. Lane, at Glasgow. She had a desire to spend the evening of her days in her native town and three years ago took up her residence with her son at Latchford. Considering her advanced age she was very active, and retained possession of her faculties to the last. She was in the habit of taking a short walk daily. About twelve months ago she had a stroke but recoverea, and her death came as a shock to her relatives and numerous friends. At one time the late Mrs. Wallington was a worshipper at St. John's Presbyterian Church,but ultimately she became associated with Wycliffe Church and remained a member to the time of her death. She leaves two sons, Messrs. Thomas and W. H. Wallington, and a daughter, Mrs. Lane,of Glasgow. The interment' will take place at the cemetery at 2.30 this afternoon.</p>
        <p>Licences Transferred. - At the County Borough .12olice Court, on Monday, Mr. R. Evans, Solicitor, applied on behalf of the executors of the late Councillor Brophy for the trans- fer of the licence of the Farmers' Arms, Dallam-lane, to Mrs. Brophy. The transfer was approved of, and the following applications were also granted :—Off- licence, 63, Priestl.y-street, from Nathaniel Adams, to Arthur Hughes ; off-licence, 19, Lovely-lane, from Martha A. Potter (deceased) to A. F. G. Potter ;an off- licence, 81, Howley-lane, from J. C. Lunt (deceased) to Martha Lunt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1904-02-06_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>KING OLAF.</head>
        <p>KING OLAF.</p>
        <p>At the Musical Society's concert on Wednesday next Dr. Elgar's "King Olaf " will be performed. As the work is quite new to Warrington audiences some know- ledge of the text and structural plan of the work may be found a useful preliminary to listening to it. It may therefore be explained that the" book " is a com- pilation of Longfellow's poem with interpolated verses, connecting its severed portion.s,by U. A. Acworth The following synopsis, by IMr. A. S, Burrows, of the "Scenes from the Saga of King Olaf," (to use the full titlejof Dr.tElgar's work), is taken from a concert programme of the Sheffield Musical Union. In it the recitatives which serve to prompt the narration of the storyembodied in "King Olaf" are printed in italies to emphasise their function and significance.</p>
        <p>INTRODUCTION': The bards name and describe the book in which is written the story they are about to relate. One of their number, who is evidently recognised to be the chief bard or master of the ceremonies, calls upon the members of the company to constitute themselves as representing Thor, the God of Thunder, and to repeat his challenge.. THE CHALLENGE OF THOR. The whole assembly in response to its chief, is here supposed to represent Thor, who arrogates to himself supremacy in the world, and hurling out defiance to the, Christian religion, issues a challenge to Christ its proto- type.</p>
        <p>'KING OLAF'S RETURN. Another of the bards comes forward and relates how the fugitive Olaf hears and accepts the challenge, and after recounting the youthful Olaf's wanderings and adventures previous to that time, tells of his return home to Norway as King, and of his resolve to establish Christianity in the Kingdom. • Their chief here directs the Skalds to tell how Olaf accomplished his mission. THE CONVERSION. In this scene, the minstrels describe the gathering of Olaf's subjects at the temple of their deity; headed by Ironbeard, they meet the King and his bodyguard of axemen and bowmen.</p>
        <p>King Olaf, ill the person of the tenor bard, offers the religion of Christ to the people, and Ironbeard—which character is for the nonce assumed by the chief bard— in the name of the people refuses it ; whereupon the king, goaded to the act by the defiant words and atti- tude of Ironbeard, takes up his war-axe and shatters the image of Thor. In attempting to avert the destruc- tion of the idol, Ironbeard is mortally wounded, but, defiant to the last, the grim old warrior declares him- self staunch to the faith of his fathers. With dying breath he commends his soul to Odin (the chief god of the Norse religion), and claiming entrance into Wal- halla, the eternal paradise of heroes slain in battle, expires. The people are so much impressed by the manifesta- tion of Thor's impotenceand the death of his champion, that they elect to embrace the new faith, the peace of which, and its completed sacrifice, the king offers them as an alternative to the ever-recurring sacrifices of blood demanded by the tenets of their religion. Meekly surrendering themselves to the newly-revealed power,. in solemn unity they bow before their king, who, with thrilling intensity, invokes upon his kneeling subjects the blessings of the King divine. The 'master bard himself tells how, as a blood-atone- ment, Olaf weds Gudrun the daughter of Ironbeard. GUDRUN. The company of Skalds describes how Gudrun, intent. on avenging her father's death,steals, on the bridal night, with dagger in hand, to where Olaf sleeps; but Olaf wakes and thwarts her design, and ere the dawn of morn rids himself of the treacherous bride. The chief minstrel now commands his 'men to sing of the coming, as an. unbidden guest to Olaf's feast, of the spirit of Odin. THE WRAITH OF ODIN. In the words of a stirring ballad, the assembled bards sing of the strange guest who entertained the company far into the night with his wonderful stories. How the king slept, but woke to find the guest gone; how Olaf, finding no trace of the departure of the stranger, pro- nounced him to have been the spirit of Odin, and inter- preted the visitation to signify the downfall of Odin the Great, and the effectual triumph of the Christian faith.</p>
        <p>The chief bard invites the In,aidens• of the company to sing the story of the wooing of Queen Sigrid by King Olaf, of the insult she suffered at his hands, and of her row to accomplish his death. SIGRID. •</p>
        <p>The minstrel maids sing of the Queen of Svithiod' awaiting the coming of King Olaf, with the ring, taken by Olaf from Lad'e's temple, on her knee.—The two characters are again represented by bards. Olaf arrives, greets the queen, and offers her himself his land, and his religion. Sigrid returns the greeting, but will only consent to become his on condition he swears his love as Odin once swore it on the ring. He refuses the condition, and Sigrid, not heeding his appeal, expresses her con- tempt of "the Southland God,', and protests her con- stancy to the " Norland Gods." At this, King Olaf's. anger rises, and he strikes her cheek with his gauntlet. i King Olaf s warned to fly, and the scene closes with the queen vowing vengeance on the retreating figure. Attention is commanded by the principal bard for the recital of the story of Thyri—the slighted choice of the Wendland King—and her flight to the North. _ _ THYRT.</p>
        <p>In a charming ballad, the minstrels sing of Thyri, the. sister of Svend, the Danish king, fleeing away from King Burislaf of Wendland, to whom she had been betrothed for the short space of eight days. She comes to King Olaf's court,and Olaf eventually marries her. After the ballad, two singers advance to represent King Olaf and the beautiful Thyri, his wife. Thyri laments the loss of her lands, which King Burislaf has Possession of, and deplores her dowerless condition. Olaf, fresh from the delights of a fair morning in early Spring, comes before her with a love offering of Angelicas, but with such thoughts rankling in her mind,. the sweet smelling herb holds no charm for Thyri. Her mood leads her to taunt Olaf into consenting to rescue. her domains from King Burislaf, upon which, having effected her purpose, she once more smiles on he r lord. The bards join in reciting how Queen Sigrid becomes - the bride of King Svend, the Dane-_a unio?t which por- tends evil f3r King Olaf—and relate how she cajoles the. Danish king into setting forth to wreak vengeance on Olaf,</p>
        <p>THE DEATH OF OLAF. Full chorus of Skalds, in which are described the Putting out to sea of Olaf's warships to meet those of the Danes, and the contact of the opposing forces. Vividly portrayed are the deadly combat and the defeat of Olaf, who, ever foremost in the fray, is sur- rounded and outnumbered, and so perishes in the flood. EPILOGUE. The bard-chief finally pictures Astrid, the 'mother of Olaf, in the Convent of Drontheim, kneeling at midnight - and listening to the voice of one speaking in the dark- ness without.</p>
        <p>The voice which Astrid heard, purports to be that of Saint John taking up the challenge in response to the entreaty of the departed spirit of Olaf. The saga-men, echoing the words of the saint, signify the ultimate acceptance of the challenge of Thor, and the continuance of Olaf's mission, but this time in the true Christian spirit of love, and by the power of the/. Great Spirit Divine, which comes "not as a vulture, but as a dove."</p>
